window.__ARXIV_DATA__ = {"papers":[{"id":"2611.34944v1","title":"AnyCam: Anything Can Represent Camera for Video Generation","abstract":"Camera-controlled video generation re-renders a video along a target camera trajectory, yet moving the camera around an object, the most natural way to change the viewpoint of an object-centric scene, remains difficult. Existing methods condition either on camera parameters, which are scale-ambiguous in monocular video, or on explicit geometry such as warped point clouds or depth, which breaks down under large viewpoint changes. We present AnyCam, a method that represents the camera with an anchor object in the scene: we reconstruct it in 3D and render it along the target trajectory, a camera signal that is scale-aware, view-complete and easy to specify relative to the object. A video diffusion model transfers the content and dynamics of the source video to the new viewpoint. Its H-Concat layout aligns source and target frames in time, so the model can be distilled into AnyCam-AR, a streaming few-step generator for on-the-fly camera control. Both models are trained on paired videos with pixel-aligned anchor renderings from multi-camera videos and static-scene captures. Experiments on diverse datasets and trajectories show that AnyCam performs favorably against camera-controlled video generation and 3D reconstruction methods in camera control and visual quality.","authors":["Weijie Lyu","Zhixin Shu","Yuntian Ke","Ming-Hsuan Yang"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:59Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34944v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34944v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34944v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":88955,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Camera-controlled video generation re-renders a video along a target camera trajectory, yet moving the camera around an object, the most natural way to change the viewpoint of an object-centric scene, remains difficult. Existing methods condition either on ca...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34943v1","title":"Tracking Any Point with Agentic Visual Reasoning","abstract":"A general-purpose Vision-Language Model (VLM) has surpassed expert vision models in many domains such as object detection and video recognition. However, point correspondence is known to be challenging for VLMs since it goes beyond object-level recognition: it requires pixel-level localization of the same physical surface point across observations, even when appearance changes or the point disappears and later reappears. In this paper, we introduce AgenTAP, a VLM-based agent that directly predicts point coordinates and visibility and actively reasons over videos. Starting from its own estimate, the agent autonomously chooses what to inspect and uses the resulting evidence to verify point identity and refine its predictions. With GPT-6 Astra, it surpasses expert vision models in both point localization and visibility prediction, without correspondence-specific fine-tuning. We further use AgenTAP to re-identify physical points after disappearance and reconnect fragmented tracks, constructing EgoTAP-4D, a dataset of 3D point trajectories in long real-world egocentric videos. On sampled reappearing points, its predictions approach human agreement, and adding EgoTAP-4D to D4RT training improves average 3D tracking performance.","authors":["Xuweiyi Chen","Jin Yao","Linzhan Mou","Lei Gong","Di Liu","Cheng Zhang","Ziqiao Ma","Sihan Xu","Adam W. Harley","Shengyi Qian","Zezhou Cheng"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:58Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:58Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34943v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34943v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34943v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":146344,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：A general-purpose Vision-Language Model (VLM) has surpassed expert vision models in many domains such as object detection and video recognition. However, point correspondence is known to be challenging for VLMs since it goes beyond object-level recognition: i...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34941v1","title":"Improving and Expanding Video Editing Capabilities Without Videos","abstract":"Image editing models can follow a wide range of instructions, but video editing models still lag behind, limited in part by the difficulty of collecting paired source and edited videos. Is it possible to transfer advanced image editing capabilities to video editors without new paired videos? We introduce Image-to-Video Transfer (I2VT), which improves pretrained video editors and expands them to new editing tasks using an image editor. It enables efficient post-training with no architectural changes and no new videos, using only individual images. Our key idea is to let the video editor role-play as an image editor: it edits an image as a one-frame video, with the image editor guiding the video editor's predictions at each denoising step. Although the video editor sees only single images during training, at test time it applies the improved and expanded capabilities to whole video clips while preserving temporal consistency. To push the boundaries of video editing, we introduce OpenVE++, an extension of OpenVE-Bench that adds four new editing tasks useful for video production, including action changes, expression changes, body movements, and scene relighting. Our experiments across three different video editors show that I2VT improves existing editing capabilities and expands them to new tasks, and our models achieve state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance on both OpenVE-Bench and OpenVE++.","authors":["Ji Xie","Yanhao Jia","Xiangpeng Yang","Martin Q. Ma","Sihan Xu","Erik Cambria","Louis-Philippe Morency"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:55Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34941v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34941v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34941v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":109851,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Image editing models can follow a wide range of instructions, but video editing models still lag behind, limited in part by the difficulty of collecting paired source and edited videos. Is it possible to transfer advanced image editing capabilities to video e...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34940v1","title":"LuxAvatar: Real-Time Relightable Head Avatars via Causal Neural Shading of 3D Intrinsic Proxies","abstract":"We present LuxAvatar, a feed-forward framework for real-time head avatar animation and relighting from a single portrait. To achieve both 3D controllability and complex facial light transport, we factorize avatar synthesis into an animatable 3D intrinsic Gaussian proxy and a causal autoregressive neural shader. This proxy disentangles canonical geometry and reflectance attributes, rapidly rasterizing deformed Gaussians into view- and motion-consistent PBR buffers. Conditioned on these buffers, our neural shader formulates forward rendering as a conditional video diffusion process, bypassing explicit PBR computation to synthesize realistic shading effects under target illumination. We achieve real-time streaming through heterogeneous distillation from a pretrained multi-step bidirectional relighting teacher to a two-step causal autoregressive neural shader, inheriting broad synthetic lighting priors while minimizing conditioning and decoding latency. To further specialize these priors to human heads, we construct a hybrid relighting dataset for domain-specific fine-tuning, capturing authentic facial dynamics and physically grounded light transport. Extensive experiments show that our framework enables 3D-controllable, real-time portrait animation and relighting, outperforming state-of-the-art methods in visual fidelity, motion control, and lighting accuracy.","authors":["Yipengjing Sun","Chenyang Wang","Boyao Zhou","Jiapeng Zhu","Ka Leong Cheng","Shengping Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:46Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34940v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34940v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34940v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":79852,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present LuxAvatar, a feed-forward framework for real-time head avatar animation and relighting from a single portrait. To achieve both 3D controllability and complex facial light transport, we factorize avatar synthesis into an animatable 3D intrinsic Gaus...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34939v1","title":"A Mesh for Every Moment","abstract":"We present MEMO (a Mesh for Every MOment), a method that reconstructs the geometry of complete dynamic scenes, captured by multiple synchronized cameras, and extracts a mesh at every frame. Existing dynamic Gaussian splatting methods either model appearance alone or target isolated objects segmented by foreground masks, and all of them recover meshes by TSDF fusion or Poisson reconstruction, whose fixed grids limit the detail they can capture. At the core of MEMO is a per-Gaussian motion model inspired by Gaussian processes, which represents each Gaussian's position and rotation over time as a combination of temporal Gaussian kernels. The model keeps every Gaussian a valid surface element throughout the sequence and carries an implicit representation of the dynamic geometry, so a mesh can be derived at any time from a field defined by the Gaussians. Although common in real captures, large and fast motion poses a challenge that dynamic Gaussian splatting methods usually overlook. To account for it, we design a track-based initialization scheme that fits each Gaussian's initial trajectory to point tracks in closed form. We evaluate MEMO on Blender-Moments, a new benchmark of nine animated scenes with ground-truth meshes at every frame, and on two real-world datasets. MEMO reconstructs the most accurate geometry among state-of-the-art methods, and remains competitive in novel-view synthesis and training time. Project page: https://memo-4d.github.io","authors":["Roy Or-El","Jeong Joon Park","Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:45Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34939v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34939v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34939v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":102883,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present MEMO (a Mesh for Every MOment), a method that reconstructs the geometry of complete dynamic scenes, captured by multiple synchronized cameras, and extracts a mesh at every frame. Existing dynamic Gaussian splatting methods either model appearance a...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34935v1","title":"Can image generation help visual reasoning? An empirical study","abstract":"Language models think in words; then multimodal models might think in images. We test whether a generated helper improves the answer to a visual question. A model describes the helper it needs and then answers using the original and generated images together. Across 13 vision-language model and generator pairs, generation lowers mean accuracy by 3.7 percentage points, with declines on nine of ten tasks. Two unified models, BAGEL and Janus-Pro-7B, each lose accuracy on eight tasks. On VSTAR, adding a generated image to the same planning hint lowers accuracy by 10.47 points. Repeated image edits yield different outcomes across tasks. ChartQAPro and MathVista stay below their no-generation baselines, while ERQA gains three points after ten edits.","authors":["Xueyang Yu","Yifan Jing","Taiming Lu","Linrong Cai","Zhipeng Cai","Yunyang Xiong","Gabriel Sarch","Zhuang Liu"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:31Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34935v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34935v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34935v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":67406,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Language models think in words; then multimodal models might think in images. We test whether a generated helper improves the answer to a visual question. A model describes the helper it needs and then answers using the original and generated images together....","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34931v1","title":"DynMF2: Rigid Motion Factorization by Expectation-Maximization for Monocular 4D Reconstruction and Motion Segmentation","abstract":"A single casual video leaves a moving scene under-determined: a point seen once from each place can slide toward or away from the camera and still match every frame. We constrain the reconstruction with the structure of the motion itself. Building on DynMF, we model a scene's motion as a handful of SE(3) trajectories, each rigid, and let every point follow one of them alone, never a blend. DynMF2 computes this factorization directly from 3D point trajectories, with no rendering, by Expectation-Maximization (EM). Its M-step fits, in closed form, every rigid motion to the points assigned to it, and its E-step reassigns every point to the motion that explains it with the least error. The background is simply one more motion, the camera's, so no masks are needed. Three tools then revise the factorization: requery adds trajectories where the rendering shows parts the tracker missed, merge joins motions that move together, and split separates those that do not. Thus the number of motions follows the scene. Without masks, DynMF2 clearly outperforms the mask-based baselines in view synthesis on iPhone and NVIDIA and in 3D tracking on iPhone. It recovers what masks leave out, from thin parts and details to small movers far in the background. Its motions segment the moving parts of MOVi-F and iTACO scenes competitively, although DynMF2 is never trained to segment. What moves in a scene is not decided in advance: it emerges from the rigid motions themselves.","authors":["Agelos Kratimenos","Yile Hua","Kostas Daniilidis"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:14Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34931v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34931v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34931v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":58885,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：A single casual video leaves a moving scene under-determined: a point seen once from each place can slide toward or away from the camera and still match every frame. We constrain the reconstruction with the structure of the motion itself. Building on DynMF, w...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34929v1","title":"High-Fidelity Image Matting with Pixel-Space Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Diffusion models have shown strong potential for image matting by transferring generative priors learned from large-scale pretraining. However, existing approaches largely rely on latent diffusion models, whose compressed representations and VAE-based reconstruction introduce a mismatch with the high-frequency precision required for matting. We present PixelMatte, a framework that adapts pretrained pixel-space diffusion transformers for high-fidelity image matting. We show that strong image-conditioned matting capabilities can be acquired even from a pure text-to-image model without paired image-to-image pretraining. Our approach reuses the backbone's native patch embedding to jointly process the input image, trimap, and noisy alpha matte as spatially aligned tokens, enabling direct pixel-space prediction without additional condition encoders or adapters. With pixel-accurate synthetic supervision and real-image fine-tuning, PixelMatte predicts alpha mattes in a single forward pass. Two lightweight calibration modules, LayerShift and High-Pass Refiner, further improve regional opacity accuracy and boundary fidelity. Experiments across four benchmarks and two distinct pixel diffusion backbones demonstrate substantial improvements over existing methods, reducing SAD and gradient errors by 24-44% relative to RenderMatte on P3M-500-NP and AM-2K, while achieving competitive performance on AIM-500 and RenderMatte-2K. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of pixel-space generative priors for precision-sensitive image matting. Project page: https://white-yc.github.io/pixelmatte/.","authors":["Baihan Yang","Xin Lin","Yufan Wei","Baiang Li","Yuheng Liu","Juanxi Tian","Xiaohui Xie","Truong Nguyen"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34929v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34929v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34929v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":46059,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Diffusion models have shown strong potential for image matting by transferring generative priors learned from large-scale pretraining. However, existing approaches largely rely on latent diffusion models, whose compressed representations and VAE-based reconst...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34914v1","title":"Cairn: Object-Centric 3D Trajectory Priors for Steering Frozen VLAs","abstract":"Vision-language-action models (VLAs) are capable low-level controllers, but they tie where objects go to the layouts they were trained on: when objects are moved or obstacles block the way, they carry them to the wrong place or into collisions. Fine-tuning on new layouts needs robot data for each one and erodes skills the model already has. We introduce Cairn, which keeps the VLA frozen and steers it at test time along learned 3D trajectories of the object in its gripper. A small prior reads point clouds of the scene and the held object, along with its pose when grasped and the instruction, and samples SE(3) trajectories up to its release that lead it around obstacles and turn it to fit through gaps. The prior and the VLA form the two levels of a hierarchical planner in which feedback runs both ways: the VLA's intended motion chooses and reshapes the trajectories, and the chosen one guides the VLA's denoising. Its predicted actions, mapped to the held object's poses through the grasp, are pulled toward the next waypoint, while the VLA's weights stay untouched. Across 1,941 held-out LIBERO scenes with displaced objects and obstacles, Cairn raises the success rate of a frozen π0.5 from 24.5% to 95.1%, above that of a π0.5 fine-tuned on our training layouts, without hurting standard LIBERO. On the third-party LIBERO-PRO benchmark, it raises success from 27.3% to 38.0%, and from 54.4% to 81.1% on the episodes it steers. On a real Franka, with a prior trained purely in simulation, it guides the frozen DROID π0.5 around an obstacle and to a placement at the limit of the arm's reach, where it otherwise fails. All code, models and data will be made public.","authors":["Agelos Kratimenos","Kostas Daniilidis","Evangelos Chatzipantazis"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:57:29Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:57:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34914v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34914v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34914v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":68759,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision-language-action models (VLAs) are capable low-level controllers, but they tie where objects go to the layouts they were trained on: when objects are moved or obstacles block the way, they carry them to the wrong place or into collisions. Fine-tuning on...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34912v1","title":"LiveVAE: Causal Window Attention Makes Efficient Streaming Video VAE","abstract":"While video diffusion models have grown faster and increasingly streamable, the video VAE remains a primary latency bottleneck across interactive generation and editing pipelines. Existing VAEs and their acceleration methods rely mainly on heavy convolutions or full frame attention, and most focus only on decoding, leaving encoding costly for streaming conditional generation and editing. This raises a natural question: Do video VAE encoding and decoding actually require such global spatiotemporal modeling? By analyzing the effective receptive fields of pretrained video VAEs, we find that both encoding and decoding a patch rely almost entirely on its spatial and temporal neighbors. To exploit this strong locality, we introduce LiveVAE, a streaming encoder and decoder built on causal window attention, which cuts per frame compute while keeping the temporal KV cache bounded for streaming. Decoupled encoder/decoder distillation followed by joint finetuning preserves the pretrained latent space, making LiveVAE a drop-in replacement for the original VAE. On MiniMax-H3, LiveVAE achieves 5.2-6.2x faster encoding and 3.0-3.3x faster decoding than the official VAE with higher PSNR. Paired with our dedicated inference infrastructure, LiveVAE pushes the speedup to 13.4x with almost no quality loss. Code and models will be released.","authors":["Jiachen An","Sijie Zhao","Le Li","Hongyu Liu","Zhiyuan Li","Tianyu Yang","Xiaoyu Kong","Xunliang Cai","Yong Zhang","Chi-Man Pun","Xiaoming Wei","Xiaodong Cun"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:57:14Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:57:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34912v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34912v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34912v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":78001,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：While video diffusion models have grown faster and increasingly streamable, the video VAE remains a primary latency bottleneck across interactive generation and editing pipelines. Existing VAEs and their acceleration methods rely mainly on heavy convolutions...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34907v1","title":"Agentic LongLive: Asynchronous Agentic Memory for Long Video Generation","abstract":"Real-time autoregressive video generators stream minutes of video and accept new prompts on the fly, but struggle to keep people, objects, and places consistent over a long interactive story. Agent systems provide such memory for offline multi-shot generation, yet they read the whole script in advance, plan each shot before generating it, and regenerate failed shots, which a live stream does not allow. We present Agentic LongLive, an asynchronous agentic memory for long video generation. A memory agent on separate GPUs reads only the prompts revealed so far, never the generated video, and keeps a text state and evolving images for every person, object, and scene. From this entity memory it composes a global image and a per-shot memory image, which an auxiliary GPU prefills into the generator's attention sink before the next shot starts, and writes entity cards for the prompts that name them, so the video never waits for memory and video errors are not written back. A small language model, trained with reinforcement learning and an image reward calibrated using downstream video outcomes, decides what to remember before the next prompt is known. On LongStory-Online, a new benchmark of 25 stories (857 shots) revealed one shot at a time, Agentic LongLive improves persistent-state accuracy of LongLive-2.0, its base generator, by 26.3 points, protagonist identity by 44.9, and revisited-place consistency by 40.4, at about 1% extra generation time on H100. The full system streams faster than real time on one 8xB200 node with the generator in BF16 (34.3 FPS), keeps image quality stable over 14-minute stories, and improves identity on two MiniMax-H3 generators, one of which, LongLive-H3, also runs in real time with it. To our knowledge, it is the first real-time video generator with text and image memory kept by an LLM agent.","authors":["Shaoteng Liu","Shuai Yang","Wei Huang","Luozhou Wang","Song Han","Yukang Chen"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:54:03Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:54:03Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34907v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34907v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34907v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":144653,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Real-time autoregressive video generators stream minutes of video and accept new prompts on the fly, but struggle to keep people, objects, and places consistent over a long interactive story. Agent systems provide such memory for offline multi-shot generation...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34903v1","title":"ProbFaS: Probabilistic Video Frame Selection for MLLMs via Multi-Fidelity Calibration","abstract":"Processing long videos with multimodal large language models (MLLMs) is prohibitively expensive, making informative frame selection essential for accurate video understanding. The default uniform sampling often misses task-relevant keyframes, while recent training-free selectors instead pick frames by how well each one matches the question under an image-text alignment (ITA) score. However, we show that ITA signals correspond poorly with the MLLM's true needs: their rankings correlate weakly with downstream accuracy and are even negatively correlated on over 40% of questions, whereas the MLLM's internal yes/no judgment yields a much stronger signal. We present ProbFaS, a training- and label-free probabilistic pipeline that fuses both signals for key frame selection. ProbFaS treats dense ITA scores as cheap, biased measurements and sparse MLLM judgments as accurate, costly ones, unifying them via co-kriging, a multi-fidelity Gaussian process, to calibrate relevance across all frames. A temporal determinantal point process then selects frames capturing relevance peaks while preserving temporal coverage. Across Video-MME, MLVU and LongVideoBench, ProbFaS is the most accurate among fourteen state-of-the-art training-free selectors and uniform sampling in 13 of 15 settings (8-128 frames), outperforming the strongest baseline by up to 3.1 percentage points. On MLVU and LongVideoBench, 32 ProbFaS frames match or surpass the top baseline at 128 frames and uniform sampling with up to 768 frames.","authors":["Tianru Zhang","Mayank Nautiyal","Hugo Eidmann","Prashant Singh","Ekta Vats"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:53:10Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:53:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34903v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34903v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34903v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":134771,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Processing long videos with multimodal large language models (MLLMs) is prohibitively expensive, making informative frame selection essential for accurate video understanding. The default uniform sampling often misses task-relevant keyframes, while recent tra...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34899v1","title":"Enhancing Maritime Domain Awareness using Latent Space Representations","abstract":"This work presents a novel application of Earth Observation (EO) embeddings for Maritime Domain Awareness (MDA) from the perspective of the geospatial foundation model TerraMind. By operating directly on compressed latent-space representations rather than on the original Sentinel-1 imagery, this approach directly addresses the data gravity bottleneck that increasingly constrains operational EO processing, in line with the objectives of the Embed2Scale project. In particular, linear and non-linear probing of compressed embeddings, latent space representations of synthetic aperture radar (SAR) imagery, is used to detect, localise, and characterise vessels from Sentinel-1 GRD images. Vessel detection achieves a ROC-AUC of 0.95, while vessel localisation has a median error of around 300 m for vessels longer than 100 m, and vessel AIS type-code classification achieves 62% accuracy for the majority classes. The latter, however, is highly correlated with vessel length. These results reveal both the potential and current limitations of Geospatial Foundation Models (GeoFMs) when applied to fine-grained MDA understanding in real-world scenarios. Additionally, a new maritime remote sensing dataset is released, pairing about 2,000 Sentinel-1 images with temporally aligned AIS signals from commercial vessels. Through manual inspection, each candidate sample was verified for bounding-box quality and for consistency between the AIS-reported vessel length and the observed vessel extent, cross-checking vessel identity and location against publicly available vessel records and, where available, photographs. The dataset was made ready for use with the NeuCo-Bench neural compression benchmark framework, publicly available on HuggingFace through https://huggingface.co/datasets/embed2scale/S1-single-ship-downstream .","authors":["Rikard Vinge","Miguel A. Belenguer-Plomer","Isabelle Wittmann","Octavian Dumitru","Michele Lazzarini","Sergio Albani","Conrad M Albrecht"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:51:36Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:51:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34899v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34899v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34899v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":47975,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：This work presents a novel application of Earth Observation (EO) embeddings for Maritime Domain Awareness (MDA) from the perspective of the geospatial foundation model TerraMind. By operating directly on compressed latent-space representations rather than on...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34894v1","title":"What a Drop Reveals: Sim-to-Real Material Estimation for Deformable Objects from Video","abstract":"How an object deforms when it hits the ground reveals what it is made of. From a single real video of a drop, we estimate a deformable object's density-normalized stiffness $E/ρ$, and its yield stress $σ_Y/ρ$ when the impact causes plastic deformation, with a feed-forward predictor trained only in simulation. Real videos carry no material labels, so such predictors are developed and validated on synthetic data alone. We show that synthetic validation ranks their geometric inputs in the wrong order. Material acts through 3D deformation, and tracklets lifted to 3D with estimated depth are indeed the most accurate input in simulation. However, on real drops they are the least accurate, and better depth does not repair them: the failure persists with simulator depth in training, LiDAR depth at test time, and exact depth on rendered replays of the real drops. Short 2D optical-flow tracklets do the opposite: they improve nothing in simulation, yet they transfer best. Material is therefore better read from what perception measures consistently in both domains than from what is most informative in simulation. Our predictor, PhysReader, reads the video and 2D tracklets and learns the lift to 3D in simulation by matching a teacher that sees exact 3D deformation. To measure transfer, we introduce PhysReader-Real: 16 everyday objects dropped five times each, with reference materials fit jointly to each object's drops and checked against tensile tests. On PhysReader-Real, PhysReader is closer to the reference materials than every other input and baseline. The one exception is a per-scene optimizer started from PhysReader's prediction, which ends slightly closer but takes 49 minutes per video instead of 12 seconds.","authors":["Ziyue Peng","Yanxi Hu","Junyoung Kim","Andrew Chen","Jin Yang","Chandrajit L. Bajaj","Etienne Vouga","William T. Freeman","Qixing Huang","Brandon Y. Feng"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:47:56Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:47:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34894v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34894v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34894v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":320884,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：How an object deforms when it hits the ground reveals what it is made of. From a single real video of a drop, we estimate a deformable object's density-normalized stiffness $E/ρ$, and its yield stress $σ_Y/ρ$ when the impact causes plastic deformation, with a...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34884v1","title":"FineDex: Bridging Fine-Grained Human-Object Interaction Generation to Dexterous Humanoid Manipulation","abstract":"Dexterity is, at its core, finger-level control, yet existing dexterous manipulation and human-object interaction (HOI) generation methods rarely let one specify how an interaction is performed: five-fingered hands are often reduced to grippers, or finger usage is copied from individual human demonstrations. We present FineDex, a framework that makes finger-level intent both expressible and executable. FineDex learns coordinated human body, hand, and object motions that follow fine-grained instructions on hand selection, individual fingers, and contacts with articulated objects. To bridge generation and execution, FineDex leverages generated interactions as actionable priors for an agent that discovers physically feasible contact strategies through practice in a physics-based digital twin. By adapting at test time without per-instance retraining, FineDex generalizes across fine-grained instructions and object configurations. Experiments demonstrate state-of-the-art HOI generation quality and fine-grained controllability, substantial improvements in dexterous manipulation success, and successful deployment on a real dexterous humanoid robot.","authors":["Yujia Zeng","Yuxin Chen","Yuzhen Song","Runhan Huang","Chensheng Peng","Yiyang Shao","Koushil Sreenath","Masayoshi Tomizuka","Yilun Du"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:40:47Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:40:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34884v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34884v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34884v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":44454,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 agent, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Dexterity is, at its core, finger-level control, yet existing dexterous manipulation and human-object interaction (HOI) generation methods rarely let one specify how an interaction is performed: five-fingered hands are often reduced to grippers, or finger usa...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34881v1","title":"Stranger Things: When Objects Appear Without Their Typical Neighbors","abstract":"Visual context is a powerful cue for object recognition: scenes inform what, where, and at what scale objects are likely to appear. Yet over-reliance on context becomes a failure mode when models predict objects based on their typical neighbors rather than the objects' own visual features, which we call typicality bias. While extensively studied in the pre-Transformer era, it has often been assumed that architectural and scale improvements would naturally solve it, and a systematic diagnostic across the modern vision stack remains absent. We provide such a diagnostic framework. We measure typicality bias via normalized pointwise mutual information (NPMI) over object pairs, and introduce STRANGE-Bench (Synthetic Typicality-RANked Grounded Evaluation Benchmark): a photorealistic, layout-controlled benchmark spanning 17 sub-tiers from typical to never-co-occurring object pairs. Across five model families (specialist detectors, open-vocabulary detectors, self-supervised, vision-language, and multimodal large language models) and three recognition tasks (supervised detection, open-vocabulary detection, and classification), the bias is prevalent: gaps of 5-11 p.p. persist when objects appear outside their typical company, and do not consistently shrink with scale. To mitigate it, we turn to data augmentation: existing augmentations are designed for visual diversity, not for breaking co-occurrence shortcuts, and sample from the very distribution that produces typicality bias. We propose Anchor-Atypicality Sampling (AAS), a simple sampling rule for paste-based augmentations that uses the NPMI signal to tilt the training-time co-occurrence distribution toward atypical partners. AAS reduces the typicality gap by up to 1.6 p.p. on natural-image COCO splits and 23.0 p.p. on synthetic STRANGE-Bench across three model families, without sacrificing overall performance.","authors":["Siddhartha Gairola","Jiahao Xie","Anna Kukleva","Francesco Locatello","Bernt Schiele"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:39:28Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:39:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34881v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34881v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34881v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":181196,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Visual context is a powerful cue for object recognition: scenes inform what, where, and at what scale objects are likely to appear. Yet over-reliance on context becomes a failure mode when models predict objects based on their typical neighbors rather than th...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34877v1","title":"Towards Closing the Performance Gap in Cross-Modal Place Recognition","abstract":"Cross-modal place recognition (CMPR) matches natural language descriptions against databases of geo-tagged reference images, enabling localization without visual queries. Despite its potential for language-guided navigation, existing vision-language models lag far behind visual place recognition (VPR) baselines, even after task adaptation. Unlike general-purpose text-to-image retrieval, place recognition relies on subtle local cues to distinguish visually similar locations. We therefore hypothesize that a primary bottleneck in CMPR is capturing fine-grained correspondences between image regions and text details, which requires higher visual token granularity, strong text representation capacity, and local cross-modal matching. To address this challenge, we propose CrossPlace, a two-stage CMPR framework. Stage 1 performs global retrieval by pairing a jointly aligned DINOv2-B/14 visual backbone with a progressively fine-tuned BGE-Large text encoder, while Stage 2 refines top candidates using a lightweight token-level cross-attention reranker. Through systematic analysis, we isolate the drivers of retrieval accuracy and show that visual token density provides the largest performance improvement, while text encoder capacity and task fine-tuning offer complementary gains. To evaluate robustness under realistic conditions, we also extend CMPR evaluation to city-scale and severe weather and illumination settings. Across all existing and new benchmarks, CrossPlace achieves double-digit Recall@1 improvements over prior cross-modal methods, closes the gap to unimodal text retrieval, and narrows it to unimodal VPR. Code and benchmarks are available at https://github.com/oferidan1/CrossPlace","authors":["Ofer Idan","Dan Badur","Yoli Shavit"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:38:09Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:38:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34877v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34877v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34877v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":73268,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Cross-modal place recognition (CMPR) matches natural language descriptions against databases of geo-tagged reference images, enabling localization without visual queries. Despite its potential for language-guided navigation, existing vision-language models la...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34863v1","title":"Focus Only on What's Important: Training-Free Focus Maps for Frozen Vision Transformers in Long-Video Object Segmentation","abstract":"Vision transformers are strong but costly image encoders for video object segmentation. Training-free token merging cuts compute uniformly across the frame, including on tracked objects. We give a frozen DINOv3 ViT inside a trained Cutie head a training-free focus map: full resolution where the segmenter expects its objects and coarser merging elsewhere. Protecting only the previous frame's mask works on short clips but fails on long videos, where fast motion pushes targets outside the margin and into background merging, locking in loss. Our policy instead takes the union of velocity-shifted and block-matched masks, runs a camera-compensated surprise check, delegates uncertain regions to the compared merger, and falls back to it when all objects are lost. On long videos (LVOS V2 valid, pre-specified protocol, three ViT-S seeds), it beats ALGM 0.98 by +2.76 J&F (95% object CI [+1.22,+4.44]; video-cluster [+0.99,+4.52]) at 16% fewer counted encoder FLOPs (0.385 vs. 0.459), sitting 1.0 J&F point below the dense-encoder baseline; at ViT-B the gain over ALGM 0.95 is +3.89 [+2.03,+5.87]. On DAVIS 2017 val it stays within -0.19 J&F of the dense encoder at ViT-S (-0.12 at ViT-B). Controls qualify the result: protecting only the previous mask under ALGM matches or exceeds accuracy at higher FLOPs (0.486), so expectation and surprise mainly reduce counted encoder compute. Practically, lower counted encoder compute did not reduce startup-inclusive mean Cutie step time on a 12-video LVOS sample (3%-8% lower in steady state; an equally engineered ALGM is faster at ViT-S and ViT-B). Section 5.2 separately examines a Cutie permanent-memory schedule and its latency and memory trade-offs. Code: https://github.com/boletoms/focused-cv","authors":["Harsha Karipineni","Rajesh Reddy Chintha"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:30:28Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:30:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34863v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34863v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34863v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93132,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision transformers are strong but costly image encoders for video object segmentation. Training-free token merging cuts compute uniformly across the frame, including on tracked objects. We give a frozen DINOv3 ViT inside a trained Cutie head a training-free...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34862v1","title":"BOP-Refer: Benchmark for Monocular 3D and 2D Object Grounding","abstract":"We introduce BOP-Refer, the first benchmark for monocular 3D and 2D grounding of everyday and industrial objects sourced from 9 diverse BOP datasets. We pair images from these datasets with VLM-generated and human-verified referring expressions, and derive accurate ground-truth 3D and 2D bounding boxes of the referred objects from their 3D models and 6DoF pose annotations. Our systematic evaluation of 17 frontier VLMs across the major proprietary and open-weight families (Gemini, GPT, Claude, Qwen, Gemma) shows that the best model, Gemini 4 Carbon, reaches 72.6% AP_IoU2D in 2D but only 12.4% AP_IoU3D in 3D, despite the more lenient IoU thresholds in 3D. The predicted 3D boxes are usually centered on the target objects in the image but often off in depth by more than the object's size, suggesting that metric scale estimation, which needs reliable object size priors, is currently by far the main source of error.","authors":["Tomas Hodan","Vineet Bhat","Martin Sundermeyer","Jonathan Tremblay","Yash Patel","Médéric Fourmy","Stephen Tyree","Junwen Huang","Varun Burde","Martin Cífka","Eric Brachmann","Vincent Lepetit","Stan Birchfield","Jiri Matas","Carsten Rother"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:29:36Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:29:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34862v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34862v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34862v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":168192,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We introduce BOP-Refer, the first benchmark for monocular 3D and 2D grounding of everyday and industrial objects sourced from 9 diverse BOP datasets. We pair images from these datasets with VLM-generated and human-verified referring expressions, and derive ac...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34860v1","title":"DocDUR-Net: Degradation-Aware Unified Document Image Restoration","abstract":"Captured document images often suffer from diverse degradations caused by uncontrolled imaging conditions, which reduce readability and obscure fine text structures. Single-degradation restoration methods are usually trained and deployed for individual degradation types, and therefore require model selection before restoration. Unified restoration models alleviate the need to maintain multiple restoration networks by sharing one network across tasks, but representative unified document image restoration methods still depend on external task selection. This dependency limits automatic document image restoration. We propose DocDUR-Net, a degradation-aware unified restoration network that derives restoration guidance directly from the input image. It introduces a Degradation Perception Module (DPM) to extract degradation representations and predict degradation weights, and Degradation Prior-guided Adaptive Feature Fusion (DP-AFF) to combine learnable degradation priors with degradation features extracted from the input. The resulting guidance is injected into a shared restoration backbone, enabling adaptive restoration without external task selection. DocDUR-Net also supports a manual mode with explicit task selection. Experiments on document image restoration benchmarks show that DocDUR-Net achieves the best average PSNR of 29.405 dB and SSIM of 0.960 among unified methods while remaining competitive with single-degradation methods.","authors":["Haowei Xu","Peirong Zhang","Xuhan Zheng","Lianwen Jin"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:29:14Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:29:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34860v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34860v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34860v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":30843,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Captured document images often suffer from diverse degradations caused by uncontrolled imaging conditions, which reduce readability and obscure fine text structures. Single-degradation restoration methods are usually trained and deployed for individual degrad...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:51+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34854v1","title":"TIDE-Flow: Fine-Grained Temporal Evidence and Structured Displacement Matching for Spike Optical Flow","abstract":"Spike cameras capture high-speed motion through an integrate-and-fire mechanism, providing fine-grained temporal observations without exposure blur. However, their binary streams encode accumulated illumination rather than instantaneous intensity, making reliable motion estimation challenging. Existing spike-based methods exploit temporal aggregation to construct motion representations, but this process may weaken fine-grained firing patterns. Meanwhile, correlation-based estimators often rely on learned matching features without explicitly characterizing the spatial distribution and ambiguity of candidate displacements. To address these challenges, we propose TIDE-Flow, a spike-based optical flow estimation framework that jointly exploits Temporal Integration Evidence (TIE) and Displacement Evidence Encoding (DEE). TIE combines raw spike patterns, multi-scale firing statistics, temporal moments, and boundary-aware interval cues, preserving complementary temporal information before spatial feature compression. DEE further integrates structured candidate-grid features with explicit displacement statistics to characterize matching geometry and competing correspondences. Both components are incorporated into a difference-correlation recurrent refinement framework, enabling effective temporal motion modeling and correspondence estimation. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of TIDE-Flow, with controlled ablations validating the contributions of temporal and displacement evidence modeling.","authors":["Hanwen Liang","Wenxuan Liu","Wenxin Huang","Jingling Yuan","Zhaofei Yu","Xian Zhong","Tiejun Huang"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:26:42Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:26:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34854v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34854v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34854v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34849v1","title":"Real2SimLab: A Unified Platform for Real-to-Simulation Transfer","abstract":"Real-to-simulation transfer, which builds simulations from real-world observations, has long been studied in research and industry. Recently, coding agents have become capable of generating simulation programs from video recordings, and several benchmarks have been proposed to evaluate them. However, each individual benchmark defines its own inputs, deliverables, and scoring pipeline. This leads to $N \\times M$ integrations when evaluating $N$ methods on $M$ benchmarks. To this end, we present Real2SimLab, a unified platform for real-to-simulation transfer. It evaluates a method by running its written program and scoring the output simulation with the original scorer of each benchmark. Moreover, Real2SimLab provides a pipeline to create training data and reward annotations for real-to-simulation methods. Our pipeline carefully avoids data contamination by filtering out the video recordings that exist in benchmarks. For each video recording, a program is first produced, and then our pipeline computes its reward with the same code that Real2SimLab uses for evaluation. So far, it supports 47 coding agents and 5 non-agent methods on 6 benchmarks. Both the platform and Awesome-Real2Sim, our research map of nearly 300 real-to-simulation papers, are open source, and we invite the Real2SimHub community to build them together.","authors":["Yongchang Zhang","Jie Huang","Yunhe Ni","Ningyu Zhou","Guangyu Zhao","Ruixun Liu","Rui Huang","Yidi Wu","TianZe Zhang","Zixiang Jiang","Xiang Li","Kevin Shen","Yiwu Zhong"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:23:41Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:23:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34849v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34849v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34849v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34839v1","title":"Diffusion Steering via Adaptive Reward Estimation and Resampling","abstract":"Text-to-image diffusion models can generate images that satisfy complex prompts, yet often produce images that omit requested objects, attributes, or relationships. We ask if models can more reliably generate text-aligned images by redistributing probability toward their more successful outcomes. We propose TrailMix, a training-free method that uses the base model's distribution of possible outcomes to adaptively guide generation toward better text alignment. During denoising, TrailMix generates inexpensive lookahead images from parallel trajectories (trails) and scores their alignment with the prompt using an external judge. The resulting distribution of reward scores allows us to estimate how strongly to steer generation toward a desired alignment level. We use these estimates to reweight and resample trajectories, favoring more promising outcomes. Across three text-to-image benchmarks, TrailMix outperforms existing baselines, with particularly strong gains on rare multi-object compositions. We further extend TrailMix to targeted image editing and introduce SEAM-Bench (Semantic Editing and Attribute Maintenance), a systematic benchmark evaluating whether semantic edits preserve other image content. Experiments on SEAM-Bench and existing benchmark PIE-Bench demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach for controlled image editing. Our results show how a model's own possible outcomes can inform reliable and controllable generation.","authors":["Abinitha Gourabathina","Sana Tonekaboni","Marzyeh Ghassemi","Akbar Rafiey"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:18:51Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:18:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34839v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34839v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34839v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34813v1","title":"Solve Vision with Code: Coding Agents Are Stronger Visual Reasoners Than Video Models","abstract":"Video generation models are increasingly evaluated as visual reasoners: given a first frame and a natural-language prompt, can the model produce the video in which the specified task is carried out? The paradigm is considered advantageous because the reasoning is native to the visual medium, unfolding directly in the frames rather than through language or code. Whether this native route is in fact the more reliable path to visual reasoning has not been tested against a symbolic alternative under the same conditions. We therefore pose the same tasks, with identical inputs, output specification and evaluator, to both media: rather than denoising pixels, a coding agent writes a program that renders the answer video, which an unmodified rule-based evaluator scores. On VBVR-Pro-Bench (Xu et al., 2026), a 500-instance suite spanning five cognitive categories with symbolic ground truth, the agent receives the same first frame and prompt as the video model, with no access to ground truth, metadata or generation models, and one attempt per instance. Three closed-model agents (Codex with gpt-6-astra, Claude Code with Fable 5.1 and Gemini CLI with Gemini 3.1 Pro) score 0.923, 0.894 and 0.893, each more than 0.22 above the strongest video model. Among 23 runnable open-weight models, the best, GLM-5, reaches 0.572, above all video models except the RL-trained one. Together, we show that for tasks whose solutions can be computed from a first frame and a prompt, program synthesis is currently a substantially more reliable route to visual reasoning than video generation. We further distinguish depictive programs, which navigate the image to recover a transferable scheme, from propositional programs, which operate on hard-coded values, revealing how visual reasoning unfolds in agent-written programs and what it implies for its nature of reasoning in general.","authors":["Dezhi Luo","Fengyuan Yu","Haotian Zhang","Zehong Zhao","Ran Ji","Siyuan An","Huayi Qin","Qingying Gao","Zhongang Cai","Lei Yang","Ziqi Huang","Ruisi Wang","Maijunxian Wang","Hokin Deng"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:01:22Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:01:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34813v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34813v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34813v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34811v1","title":"HOG-WAM: Improving Visual Robustness through Future Gradient Prediction","abstract":"World-action models jointly predict future observations and actions, yet visual corruption can disrupt the resulting policies. We introduce HOG-WAM, which adds future Histograms of Oriented Gradients (HOG) as structural supervision derived directly from demonstration images. An auxiliary HOG diffusion transformer (DiT) module reads RGB features through asymmetric attention, allowing its objective to shape the visual representation used for control. The module is used only during training and removed at inference, preserving FastWAM's RGB-to-action architecture. We evaluate four controlled observation corruptions through a visual-corruption extension of RoboTwin, termed RoboTwin-Plus; HOG-WAM achieves the highest success among the compared policies under all four. Mixed-noise success increases from 78.86% to 82.86% relative to FastWAM, with comparable performance on clean scenes. On LIBERO Plus, overall success increases from 74.49% to 75.26%, with the largest gain under sensor noise, from 74.14% to 78.89%. Across four Franka tasks, average success increases from 40% to 59% under clean observations and from 17% to 24% under noisy observations. A representation ablation favors combining RGB observations with auxiliary HOG prediction over replacing RGB with HOG. These findings support future-gradient prediction as complementary supervision for visually robust control, with larger absolute gains on physical tasks than in simulation. Code is available at https://github.com/iPPLab/HOG-WAM.","authors":["Boxuan Dong","Yupeng Qian","Ying Wu","Heshuai Li","Junhao Tan","Zhenxiong Tan","Weijia Wu","Weihao Yu"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:59:24Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:59:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34811v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34811v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34811v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34810v1","title":"WorldExpand: Building Coherent 3D Worlds via Sparse Joint Panoramic Expansion","abstract":"Constructing complete 3D worlds from a single panorama is challenging because large portions of the scene remain unobserved. Existing panoramic-video methods expand spatial coverage through dense sequential generation, but neighboring frames contain substantial redundancy, long trajectories accumulate errors, and independently generated directions may be inconsistent. We present WorldExpand, which formulates single-panorama world expansion as the generation of sparse, complementary, and high-resolution panoramic observations. This representation concentrates the generation budget on informative scene regions and preserves the fine-grained details required for 3D construction. WorldExpand addresses two key challenges: where to observe and how to synthesize. Our Coverage-Guided View Selection identifies informative target locations using scene-adaptive quadrants and warp-based coverage scores, while our Joint Panoramic Diffusion uses Gaussian-splatting-based geometric warps to jointly synthesize four pose-grounded and cross-view-consistent panoramas. A diagnostic-guided global--local attention schedule retains global attention only in sensitive layers, reducing the training and inference costs of high-resolution joint generation. The resulting posed panoramas can be directly fed into an existing reconstruction pipeline to build an explicit 3D representation. Experiments show that WorldExpand recovers content behind occluders and around distant corners, improves both novel-view synthesis and downstream 3D reconstruction metrics across various datasets, and generalizes robustly to diverse in-the-wild scenes. WorldExpand runs significantly faster than the state-of-the-art panoramic-video method while producing higher-resolution observations with stronger cross-view consistency.","authors":["Yuyang Yin","Chenjie Cao","Xuhui Zuo","Yuning Gong","Pengfei Wang","Junta Wu","Zhenwei Wang","Yao Zhao","Tengfei Wang","Yunchao Wei","Chunchao Guo"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:58:06Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:58:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34810v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34810v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34810v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34801v1","title":"Guidance as Variance Matching","abstract":"Guidance repels the prediction of a diffusion model away from a weaker reference prediction. It substantially improves sample quality, but its mechanism remains poorly understood. We first find that, at the scales used in practice, guidance does not improve the prediction of the posterior mean (the model's training target); it makes the prediction worse, while samples improve. Second, posterior-mean predictions have less variance than the data, and we find that guidance restores it. We introduce variance-matched guidance: a closed-form rule that picks the guidance scale by matching the variance of the guided predictions to that of the data. Without tuning, it recovers the hand-picked scales of eleven widely used models across image, video, audio, motion, and world generation; to our knowledge, no previous explanation of guidance predicts them. Applied per noise level, it yields a schedule without tuned hyperparameters that users prefer to hand-crafted ones on SD 3.5 Medium. Third, we offer an interpretation of the mechanism behind guidance: each model makes a random error in predicting the posterior mean, and guidance rescales the difference of these errors into a sample with the posterior's covariance, which improves sampling. This view also suggests two new hypotheses that our experiments support: guidance can work with negative scales, and guiding a model with its own checkpoint needs a larger scale than guiding it with an independently trained model.","authors":["Ali Backour","Artem Lukoianov","Frank Permenter","Tommi Jaakkola","Justin Solomon","Vincent Sitzmann"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:53:37Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:53:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34801v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34801v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34801v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34795v1","title":"CIRKDV2: Cross-Image Relational Knowledge Distillation with Contextual Modeling for Efficient Semantic Segmentation","abstract":"Existing Knowledge Distillation (KD) methods for semantic segmentation often guide the student to mimic the teacher's structured information generated from an individual image. However, they ignore the global semantic relations across various images that are valuable for KD. This paper proposes Cross-Image Relational KD V2 (CIRKDV2), which focuses on transferring structured pixel-to-pixel, pixel-to-region, and channel-to-channel relations modeled from global images in the dataset. A large teacher network often constructs a well-structured feature space in terms of global contextual dependencies. CIRKDV2 makes the student mimic the better structured semantic relations of the teacher, thus improving the feature representational capability. Beyond relational distillation, we further propose contextual feature distillation by using context aggregation to augment student pixel embeddings to match the semantic level with the teacher. This avoids the semantic gap problem between the teacher and student occurred in traditional feature distillation studies. Experimental results on Cityscapes, ADE20K, Pascal VOC, and COCO-Stuff-164K demonstrate that CIRKDV2 outperforms the state-of-the-art semantic segmentation distillation methods by large margins across various network architectures. Our code and pre-trained models are publicly available at https://github.com/winycg/CIRKD.","authors":["Chuanguang Yang","Yu Wang","Chengqing Yu","Xinqiang Yu","Weilun Feng","Yuqi Li","Zhulin An","Libo Huang","Boyu Diao","Fei Wang","Fuzhen Zhuang","Yongjun Xu","Yingli Tian","Tingwen Huang","Yongduan Song"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:49:00Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:49:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34795v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34795v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34795v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34783v1","title":"SceneDesigner3D: Agentic Evolutionary Search for Principled Rearrangement of 3D Indoor Scenes","abstract":"Scene rearrangement is an essential task for 3D content creation applications, requiring generation of diverse, aesthetic and functional scene rearrangements. We present SceneDesigner3D, an agentic evolutionary search method for 3D indoor scene rearrangement that systematically explores the design space of 3D scenes and generates multiple solutions according to common interior design principles. Given an input scene and a text prompt, SceneDesigner3D represents the rearrangement as an editable program and evolves a population of candidate layouts. Starting from distinct design ideas, a VLM agent mutates the programs by refining promising candidates, exploring new rearrangement strategies or redesigning from scratch, while verifying the layouts for physical validity. Proposals are scored by a VLM judge for adherence to the input prompt and satisfaction of design principles such as balance, zoning and emphasis, as well as numerically computed principles such as circulation and clutter. An archive of top-performing, structurally varied candidates further increases diversity of rearrangements. Experiments on the SAGE-10k and SceneSmith datasets show that SceneDesigner3D improves prompt adherence and computed design principle scores over baselines by up to 5.1% and 15.2%, respectively. Code will be made publicly available.","authors":["Chandan Yeshwanth","Angela Dai"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:41:34Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:41:34Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34783v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34783v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34783v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34782v1","title":"Generation as Reverse Encoding","abstract":"Pretrained visual representations accelerate generative learning by abstracting away appearance and spatial detail. Yet the same abstraction makes the return to pixels far harder, shifting much of the burden onto a larger decoder. We ask why the representation-to-pixel path should be learned independently when the encoder already provides a learned pixel-to-semantics path (the semantic encoding path). We introduce RevFlow, based on the principle of generation as reverse encoding. RevFlow uses intermediate representations of a frozen encoder as successive generative targets, first generating deep semantic representations, then shallow representations that retain appearance and spatial detail, before decoding to pixels. To learn these transitions without training a separate generator for each stage, we develop HyC-DiT, a unified generative model with a standard DiT backbone shared across stages and stage-specific condition injection. We further show that native matching in semantic representation space enables few-step generation. RevFlow accelerates both learning and sampling. On ImageNet $256 \\times 256$, RevFlow reaches 1.96 FID at 690M and 1.74 FID at 1.55B after only 5 training epochs. It also achieves 30.25 dB reconstruction PSNR with an 89M decoder. After a short native-matching adaptation phase, RevFlow-XL achieves 2.85 FID using a single sampling step in each stage. Our code repository is at https://github.com/ChengShiest/RevFlow","authors":["Cheng Shi","Sibei Yang","Hanzhong Guo","Yipeng Qin","Pietro Lio","Yizhou Yu"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:40:56Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:40:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34782v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34782v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34782v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34750v1","title":"SOLVE: Sparse, Ordered Keyposes Make LLM Agents Versatile and Efficient Robot Manipulator","abstract":"LLM agents have made remarkable progress in robot manipulation, yet they suffer from expensive inference, dense step-by-step control, and tool bottlenecks. We introduce SOLVE, a training-free, versatile and efficient agent policy: a multimodal LLM agent writes a sparse, ordered sequence of 6-DoF end-effector keyposes per call, observes the result, and writes the next, while a motion planner interpolates between them. The agent decides the number and spacing of keyposes for both fast long-range motion and fine-grained manipulation. This granularity matches the sparsity of teleoperated manipulation: most demonstrations in DROID can be compressed into a few keyposes instead of dense actions. SOLVE grounds keyposes with sparse depth queries answered by noisy sources, such as multi-view triangulation, stereo, or monocular depth, and copes with that noise itself. It also improves from execution feedback, reflecting on its own rollouts and distilling a few rules per task. SOLVE reaches state-of-the-art performance of 74.5% on RoboDojo and 84.5% on RoboLab, with only $1.7 per episode on average, 12x less than previous dense-control agent policies. On a real robot, the same agent achieves an 88% success rate (75 of 85 episodes) over 16 challenging zero-shot tasks. Our project page is at https://serenos.github.io/solve-website.","authors":["Xiang Li","Hongyu Zhou","Ya-Li Li","Tao Li","Rui Zhang","Junjie Lai","Weihua Zhang","Shengjin Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:25:17Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:25:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34750v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34750v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34750v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34730v1","title":"WorldInteract: Controllable Streaming Video Generation for Multi-Material Interactions","abstract":"Video world models aim to create interactive physical worlds that capture how diverse materials move and interact. Yet material-dependent dynamics remain underexplored in controllable video generation, limiting reliable control in scenes with different materials. We present WorldInteract, a streaming video generation framework that decomposes cross-material interaction into material-dependent behavior generation and contact response computation. External pushes and contact impulses are expressed through a shared velocity-increment interface, allowing material behaviors learned from interaction-free videos to be reused in interacting scenes. A physics controller predicts contacts and computes velocity increments from estimated masses and restitution coefficients, while a causal generator executes these increments using learned material behaviors. This separation enables cross-material interaction without supervised contact videos spanning diverse material pairings and contact configurations. We train the generator on 30k simulated interaction-free videos spanning 22 material classes and introduce Material-Aware RL, using verifiable physical rewards to refine material behavior and control execution. WorldInteract substantially outperforms state-of-the-art methods on Physics-IQ and delivers more reliable push control on real-world images.","authors":["Ahmed Hatem","Caoliwen Wang","Robert Hoang","Siyuan Chen","Spencer Szabados","Zike Wu","Xiyang Tan","Shenyifan Lu","Rafael Bischof","Bernd Bickel","Renjie Liao","Peter Yichen Chen"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:14:27Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:14:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34730v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34730v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34730v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34724v1","title":"LiDAR Intensity Reconstruction with Limited Real Data: An Architecture Benchmark under Fixed-Epoch Training","abstract":"Physics-derived simulation can supplement scarce real-world LiDAR intensity measurements, but its effectiveness depends on the learning architecture and the amount of real training data available. We benchmark nine neural architectures for LiDAR intensity reconstruction from depth, semantic labels, incidence angle, and material reflectance, using real SemanticKITTI and synthetic VoxelScape range-image data. All models follow a fixed 20-epoch protocol; this controls the number of training passes, not the computational cost across architectures. We report mean absolute error (MAE), mean squared error (MSE), peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), structural similarity (SSIM), inference timing, and spectral-energy agreement. We also evaluate four selected architectures with fixed-size training mixtures containing 0\\%, 1\\%, 5\\%, 10\\%, 20\\%, 50\\%, or 100\\% real frames. With real-only training, UNet-V2 achieves the best reported reconstruction metrics (MAE 0.234, MSE 0.140, PSNR 8.64 dB, and SSIM 0.647), followed by a denoising diffusion model (DDPM). In several mixtures containing 1--10\\% real data, DDPM achieves lower error and higher image-quality metrics than the evaluated deterministic alternatives, whereas UNet-V2 leads with 100\\% real data. Spectral analysis additionally reveals architecture-dependent discrepancies across frequency bands. These findings characterize reconstruction trade-offs under the stated data mixtures and fixed-epoch protocol, without establishing compute-matched superiority or isolating the diffusion objective from backbone differences.","authors":["Christopher M. Coovrey","Aniruddha Bora"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:10:43Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:10:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34724v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34724v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34724v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34719v1","title":"Reading, Not Adapting: Unlocking Frozen Visual Knowledge for AI-Generated Image Detection","abstract":"Generalization to unseen image generators has driven efforts to adapt pretrained visual representations for detection. Yet recent findings show that vision foundation models already encode transferable detection knowledge, while further adaptation can impair generalization. This motivates a shift from backbone adaptation to knowledge readout. Conventional linear probing offers a direct way to read this knowledge but is limited to final-layer RGB features. Multi-cue detectors broaden the evidence base, but their designs introduce other tradeoffs: adaptation may compromise pretrained transferability, heterogeneous encoders complicate feature integration, and late fusion precludes intermediate cross-view interaction. To overcome these limitations, we propose ReMoT, a Mixture-of-Transformers-inspired framework that reads complementary knowledge from spatially aligned RGB and wavelet views within a fully frozen visual foundation. ReMoT employs staged joint attention to enable controlled local and global cross-view interaction, while learnable fusion queries progressively read complementary evidence across Transformer depth without writeback. ReMoT achieves 91.2% average accuracy across eight benchmark groups with 3,074 trainable parameters, attaining the highest overall average among the evaluated methods. These results support structured knowledge readout as a promising alternative to backbone adaptation. Code is available at https://github.com/yunncheng/ReMoT.","authors":["Yuncheng Guo","Ying Dong","Xiaodong Gu"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:09:32Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:09:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34719v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34719v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34719v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34700v1","title":"Seeing the Right Context: Grounded On-Policy Distillation for Remote Sensing","abstract":"Remote sensing scene understanding requires multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to interpret queried targets in relation to nearby objects and surrounding structures, which serve as spatial references or define the scope of an answer. However, standard answers and bounding boxes often leave these contextual relationships implicit. Thus, we construct GeoContext-5K to make them explicit in 5,394 training pairs for visual question answering (VQA), with spatial cues, visual facts, and supporting regions. GeOPD uses these annotations for on-policy distillation, guiding a frozen teacher with region marks on the full image and a visual fact while preserving surrounding spatial references. It prioritizes teacher corrections that agree with the shift in the student's next token distribution when evidence is restored. A complementary objective weights the contrast between the student's scores on the two views by how much masking lowers the teacher's log probability for the same sampled token. Experiments on five remote sensing VQA benchmarks show that GeOPD outperforms the evaluated open-source MLLMs and matched post-training baselines, improving over the 4B and 9B base models by 5.81 and 2.97 percentage points on average. The dataset and code will be publicly released at https://github.com/YeeCharm/GeOPD.","authors":["Yichang Luo","Hourun Zhu","Shixiong Liu","Kan Wei","Shi Liang","Yidan Zhang","Lei Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:58:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:58:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34700v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34700v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34700v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34693v1","title":"ShowNow: Fine-Grained Speculative Streaming for Responsive World Models","abstract":"Interactive world models must generate video continuously while responding promptly to user actions. Real-time throughput alone leaves input latency unresolved: coarse generation blocks delay action updates, and generation after input adds work to the response path. We introduce ShowNow, a fine-grained speculative streaming framework that uses computation available during playback to prepare action-conditioned futures before input arrives. Single-latent commitment enables frequent decisions, while configurable preparation covers one to eight actions. We construct a matched multi-future dataset to train alternative continuations from a common state; shared-history tree execution and fused commitment make their preparation efficient. On a single H100, our 5B model sustains 480p generation at 16 fps with eight prepared futures. In ViZDoom, the complete ShowNow deployment reduces median input-to-display latency from 620 to 208 ms relative to the native-block SolarWM-FT deployment. Matched-policy experiments reduce restart empty ticks from 1.56% to 0.09% (94%) and improve on-time responses to action changes. These results establish complementary roles for fine-grained commitment and advance preparation, with measured trade-offs in visual fidelity and long-horizon consistency. ShowNow turns playback-time computation into responsive visual interaction. Project: https://github.com/showlab/ShowNow.","authors":["Yiren Song","Guian Fang","Mike Zheng Shou"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:52:46Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:52:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34693v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34693v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34693v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34681v1","title":"Find the Clue, Not the Scene: Rethinking Reasoning for Entity Recognition in Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Answering a knowledge-based visual question requires first recognizing the entity in the image and then recalling knowledge about it. Thinking vision-language models (VLMs) reason for thousands of tokens before answering, yet it remains underexplored how well such thinking VLMs perform each of these two steps. We therefore analyze thinking VLMs on this task and make two key observations. (1) Recognition is the bottleneck, since 76.3% of errors across four models on ViQuAE stem from misrecognizing the entity, and 84.9% of these failures are resolved once the entity name is provided in the question. (2) Recognition is driven by entity-recall clues rather than by general descriptions of the image. An entity-recall clue is a distinctive visual detail that helps the model recall the entity, such as keys, a traditional attribute of Saint Peter. Based on these findings, we propose ClueRL, a reinforcement learning approach that extends Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with an advantage localized to the entity-recall clue in the thinking trace. Rather than injecting new entity knowledge, ClueRL changes how VLMs think: it leads them to attend more closely to visual details and spot these clues, helping them use the knowledge they already have. Consequently, it outperforms both GRPO and a perception-reward baseline on three entity recognition benchmarks. Moreover, this clue-seeking behavior generalizes beyond entity recognition, the only task used for training. Since recognition is the bottleneck, the gains carry over to knowledge-based visual question answering. They even extend to general multimodal reasoning, raising Qwen3.5-4B by 4.4 and 4.0 points on the ScienceQA and MMMU subsets of MMStar, respectively.","authors":["Kyungmin Min","Soyeon Kim","Junseok Kim","Kyomin Jung"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:44:26Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:44:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34681v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34681v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34681v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34680v1","title":"Neural Rendering Compiler: Generative Video Models as Programmable Renderers","abstract":"Generative video models are becoming powerful renderers, yet lack the predictable control interfaces of conventional systems such as HTML/CSS engines or Blender. Their capabilities emerge implicitly from training, making it difficult to determine what they can reliably execute. We present Neural Rendering Compiler (NeurCo), which automatically probes frozen video models to construct model-specific capability manuals describing which semantic requirements they can reliably satisfy. NeurCo represents user intent in a model-agnostic intermediate representation of objects, constraints, events, and cameras, then compiles it into model-specific execution strategies. Reliable requirements remain unchanged, while unreliable ones are selectively rewritten or compiled into alternative execution strategies. We introduce VComBench, a benchmark of 380 compositional, multi-requirement tasks spanning text-to-video, image-to-video, and reference-to-video generation. NeurCo improves requirement satisfaction from 46.8% to 52.3% on Wan and from 66.5% to 69.3% on Minimax H3, and achieves further gains when combined with prompt enhancers, including on Seedance 2.5. Our analysis reveals model-specific differences in control capabilities and trade-offs between controllability and photorealism, highlighting capability-guided compilation as a promising approach to programmable generative video rendering.","authors":["Yuxia Zhu","Xinglong Xu","Hao Liang","Linzhuang Sun","Caijun Jia","Qifeng Cai","Jincen Luo","Lei Li","Alden Cai","Asher Qin","Wentao Zhang","Cheng Tan"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:44:16Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:44:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34680v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34680v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34680v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34675v1","title":"RoboEvo$^2$: Recursive Robot Self-Improvement via Harness-Policy Co-Evolution","abstract":"Robots should adapt to unfamiliar environments and acquire new skills through interaction, then execute those skills efficiently with practice. Vision-language-action policies provide efficient control, but often need additional human demonstrations to handle unfamiliar settings; reasoning agents can discover solutions using broad prior knowledge and robot-control APIs, but repeated deliberation and repair are costly. Inspired by human learning through reasoning and practice, we propose RoboEvo$^2$, a framework for recursive harness-policy co-evolution. In Policy-to-Harness (P2H) evolution, policy failures guide a coding agent to search complementary Code, Code+ICL, and Code+Policy routes, consolidating verified solutions into reusable skills, routing strategies, and experience memory. In Harness-to-Policy (H2P) evolution, verified trajectories provide supervised training, while Counterfactual Preference Distillation (CPD) teaches the policy to favor successful corrections over failures from the same starting state. The improved policy provides stronger motor skills for subsequent search, while its remaining failures redirect harness evolution, closing the reciprocal loop. Through recursive co-evolution without additional human demonstrations, RoboEvo$^2$ improves standalone success from 11.0% to 84.1% and hybrid success from 30.4% to 90.5% on LIBERO-PRO, with similar gains and reduced agentic costs on RoboTwin C2R. Further experiments demonstrate autonomous task acquisition, cross-embodiment harness transfer, and 95.0% hybrid success on the Unitree G1-D. Our models and code will be publicly released.","authors":["Chenzhi Liu","Jiehong Lin","Wei Huang","Xuzhao Huang","Haoyuan Li","Yang Zhang","Junchao Huang","Chenyeyang Cai","Bo Wang","Kazuhiro Kosuge","Zhongrui Wang","Xiaojuan Qi"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:39:10Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:39:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34675v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34675v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34675v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34657v1","title":"Counting Beyond Frames: Codec Motion Tells Frozen Diffusion VLMs How Many","abstract":"Counting how many times something happens is among the simplest questions about a video and among the hardest for video-language models, autoregressive and diffusion alike. The difficulty lies between the frames: a model samples only a few of them, while each repetition unfolds as movement in between. Video codecs already record this movement densely as block displacements, and codec-based video models use it to shape their visual input. We are the first to show that this motion provides direct evidence for the count: a candidate count is a testable motion hypothesis, and unwinding the cycles it implies measures how strongly the observed directions support it. We present CoDiCount, a unified training-free framework that turns this test into a count potential and applies it while a frozen diffusion vision-language model (dVLM) denoises its answer. At every denoising step, structured count messages map each option letter, or each grammar-valid edit of an open integer, to the count it expresses and reweight the model's own probabilities within that set. Consistent gains over the official decoders on MotionBench, TOMATO, and SVCBench demonstrate the broad generalization of CoDiCount across diffusion models, datasets, and diverse counting tasks. With CoDiCount, a 7B dVLM outperforms GPT-5.1 and Gemini-2.5-Pro on MotionBench, surpasses GPT-4o and much larger open models on TOMATO, and matches GPT-5.4 on SVCBench.","authors":["Zhuoqin Li","Jiayu Ding","Ge Li"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:30:09Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:30:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34657v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34657v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34657v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34649v1","title":"Atunda: Building an Afrogenic Dance Movement Dataset for AI-Driven Classification","abstract":"This paper introduces Atunda, the first systematically collected video dataset of Afrogenic dance moves, designed for training and evaluating deep learning models for movement classification. The first phase of the dataset includes nearly 400 videos covering 13 distinct dance moves from African and African Diaspora performative culture, making it the first AI-ready resource of its kind. Using this dataset, we train and evaluate various configurations of temporal convolutional neural networks, achieving a classification accuracy exceeding 95%. The baseline results provide a benchmark for future studies in movement classification, especially in the understudied area of Afrogenic performative culture and tradition. This work lays the foundation for developing in the future digital metrics of similarity, automated attribution systems, and tools that preserve and promote the cultural heritage of Afrogenic dances.","authors":["Diego Carrascosa","Tristan Pank","Qudus Onikeku","Mas'ud Onikeku","Jeremy A Frusco","Osubi Craig","Angelos Barmpoutis"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:25:43Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:25:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34649v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34649v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34649v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34644v1","title":"Motion Beyond Pixels: Learning Future Visual Representations for Object-Centric Dynamics","abstract":"Many embodied world models predict future states in latent spaces optimized for RGB reconstruction, which do not explicitly prioritize object-level semantics and geometry relevant to motion generation. We present MotionRep, a visual representation world model that jointly generates future DINO representations and 3D object flow, coupling semantic and geometric information with object-centric dynamics. To make high-dimensional DINO features amenable to generative modeling, we introduce DINO-VAE, which combines temporal and channel compression with latent regularization to produce compact latents while preserving semantic and geometric information. Conditioned on an initial observation, object query points, and a language instruction, a unified diffusion transformer predicts future visual representations and 3D object flow through joint attention. MotionRep employs asymmetric modality-specific noise schedules to keep the visual stream ahead of 3D object flow during joint denoising, allowing progressively refined future visual representations to guide flow generation. Experiments on simulated and real-world manipulation benchmarks demonstrate that MotionRep outperforms pixel-based baselines in 3D object flow accuracy and generalization to unseen scenes, while requiring only three denoising steps for inference. These results support predictive visual representations as an effective future-state space for object-centric dynamics modeling.","authors":["Chengzhi Zhao","Juelin Li","Zhennan Wang","Sheng Wang","Zhuoman Liu","Yuhao Lu","Jiongxu Chen","Haitang Feng","Yanjie Li","Lin Shao"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:24:11Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:24:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34644v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34644v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34644v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34640v1","title":"PRAM: Activating Latent Multimodal Knowledge through Partwise Reasoning","abstract":"Emergent cross-modal classification and retrieval enable transfer between modalities without direct paired supervision. However, existing methods often prioritize sample-level alignment, overlooking local discriminative information and partwise statistical relations. To this end, we propose partwise reasoning across modalities (PRAM), which activates latent multimodal knowledge in frozen encoders by composing partwise statistical relations through a shared pivot modality. Specifically, we first present a multi-granularity covariance regularization module that estimates covariances from global and local vectors in two collections of modality-pivot pairs, stabilizing local estimates through adaptive blending with their cross-part means. Second, we devise a pivot-based spectral relational reasoning module that infers global and partwise statistical relations between target modalities through regularized covariance composition and extracts paired projection directions and their correlation strengths via whitening and spectral decomposition. Finally, we introduce a multi-granularity score fusion module that standardizes and fuses matching scores from global and local relations, incorporating task-specific information on pivot associations and class semantics for class prediction and candidate ranking. Extensive experiments across ten datasets demonstrate that PRAM achieves state-of-the-art performance in 16 classification settings and 19 retrieval settings, while exhibiting highly competitive performance in three sensing scenarios.","authors":["Haidong Chen","Ying Guo","Shangze Li","Zhongkuan Mao","Linrui Xu","Canran Xiao","Fei Shen","Tat-Seng Chua"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:21:29Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:21:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34640v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34640v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34640v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34622v1","title":"Beyond Pose Control: Anatomically Consistent Ultrasound Video Generation","abstract":"Camera-controlled video generation anticipates how a scene appears from new viewpoints by relying on a prior learned implicitly from large-scale data. Ultrasound poses the same problem, with the probe pose as the viewpoint, but under opposite conditions: anatomy offers an explicit prior that every sonographer relies on, whereas tracked scans are too scarce to learn it implicitly. As a result, pose control alone is not enough, and pose-conditioned ultrasound generators produce implausible anatomy in unscanned regions and inconsistent anatomy on revisits. We go beyond pose control and give the generator the two kinds of knowledge that make up a sonographer's mental model. An anatomical prior, built from a population of subjects, guides generation where nothing has been scanned, and is refined online by every generated chunk towards the scanned subject. A 3D latent memory, designed around the cross-sectional physics of ultrasound, compounds the generated slices into a volume of the patient and reslices it at new poses, keeping revisited anatomy consistent. The fine-grained memory enters the generator at the input, while the coarse anatomy prior guides the layout across transformer blocks. Experiments on thyroid, kidney, and lumbar spine show improved visual fidelity, pose following, and anatomical consistency over pose-conditioned and memory-augmented baselines.","authors":["Yue Zhou","Fangxu Han","Tony Danjun Wang","Nassir Navab","Yuan Bi"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:08:58Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:08:58Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34622v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34622v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34622v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34608v1","title":"DuCo: Exploiting Dual Cross-Layer Consistency for Sparse Geometric Attention","abstract":"Dense global attention is crucial for capturing cross-view geometry in visual geometry foundation models, yet its quadratic complexity limits efficient inference on long image sequences. Existing sparse attention methods primarily select entries of the global attention matrix based on attention probabilities and construct masks independently across layers, overlooking value redundancy and cross-layer information reuse. By analyzing attention distributions and clustering value representations across layers, we reveal two forms of \\emph{cross-layer consistency}, characterized by highly similar attention probability distributions and localized changes in value redundancy structures. Building on these observations, we propose \\textbf{DuCo}, a training-free sparse attention framework that exploits historical attention information and value redundancy for efficient geometric inference. Specifically, DuCo leverages historical attention information to estimate current-layer dense attention probabilities and introduces value clustering to exploit redundancy, with clusters efficiently refined through local updates across layers. Both factors are integrated through probability-weighted value distortion to jointly optimize four cluster representation states under an attention budget for sparse mask construction. Experiments across three visual geometry foundation models and five benchmarks demonstrate favorable accuracy--efficiency trade-offs.","authors":["Liangyu Xiang","Junyu Gao","Xiaofeng Wang","Changsheng Xu"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:59:54Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:59:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34608v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34608v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34608v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34603v1","title":"VisualMontage: Manipulating the Beliefs of Vision-Language Agents with Authentic Image Sequences","abstract":"As agents based on vision-language models (VLMs) increasingly interpret social-media content, errors in their analysis may lend credibility to rumors and amplify their spread. In this paper, we study the problem of visual cognitive collusion, in which coordinated posts combine authentic images and locally faithful captions to induce VLM agents to infer and accept attacker-chosen target claims through their own reasoning. To enable systematic evaluation of this cognitive threat, we introduce a new benchmark comprising 544 target cases and 33.2K candidate images across eight application domains. We further propose VisualMontage, a multi-agent framework for simulating such attacks through narrative planning, image selection and ordering, and caption assignment under iterative review. We evaluate this threat through extensive experiments across 12 vision-language models, examining modality effects, production-team components, and downstream defenses. Using identical attack feeds across models without victim-specific optimization, we demonstrate cross-model transferability, achieving mean attack success rates of 61.93% for proprietary models and 68.71% for open-weight models. Moreover, 92.65% of generated feeds pass independent model-based validity auditing. Even after downstream review, residual attack success rates range from 35.3% to 76.7% across the evaluated configurations. Our additional controlled comparisons further reveal that captions can improve model-rated posting naturalness while reducing attack success. Together, these findings reveal a novel cognitive threat in social-media interpretation and motivate safeguards that assess how authentic posts jointly shape agents' reasoning and conclusions.","authors":["Jinwei Hu","Lai Wei","Zhuoyun Li","Youcheng Sun","Yi Dong","Xiaowei Huang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:57:55Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:57:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34603v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34603v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34603v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34601v1","title":"You Only Look Multiple Times: YoloMT, a Real-Time Streaming Model on a T4","abstract":"We introduce a new model for real-time streaming captioning on a single GPU. YoloMT is a 449.8M-parameter streaming captioner that provides Pareto-frontier performance among generative vision models in its class. Distilled from a 27B teacher by SFT and GRPO, it leads every model under 1B parameters on OVO-Bench, MVBench, StreamingBench and Video-MME, answered from its captions, and beats LFM2.5-VL-1.6B and SmolVLM2-2.2B. YoloMT is designed to read more frames cheaply and make them informative, encoding each frame once and caching it as 16 tokens. This allows the model to incorporate and infer from past frames without additional vision passes, making it efficient and effective. We also provide a detailed recipe for training YoloMT, with its stages and data, as well as ablations that demonstrate the contribution of each component.","authors":["Nessim Ben Abbes","Duc Han Le","Sabri Mtibaa","Van-Tam Nguyen"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:57:40Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:57:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34601v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34601v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34601v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34598v1","title":"SEAM: A Self-Evolving and Adaptive Probing Framework for Automatic MLLM Hallucination Evaluation","abstract":"The severe susceptibility of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to hallucinations poses a critical threat to their reliability, making a rigorous evaluation framework indispensable. However, existing evaluation pipelines largely rely on predefined test items, suffering from high construction costs, rapid obsolescence as MLLMs evolve, and ineffectiveness in probing model-specific weaknesses. To address these limitations, we propose SEAM, a self-evolving and adaptive probing framework for automatic MLLM hallucination evaluation. Specifically, SEAM introduces a composable Hallucination Probe Construction Library comprising ten hallucination-probing skills, three elicitation wrappers, and five question formats. Building on this library, SEAM establishes a dual-loop framework. In the inner loop (adaptive probing), skill-guided visual analysis, question composition, and hierarchical quality verification are coordinated with verifier and target-response feedback to refine test items and uncover model-specific hallucinations. In the outer loop (skill evolution), a skill updater distills historical probing trajectories into localized, reusable rule updates, which undergo held-out validation and are frozen before testing. Driven by these innovative structural designs, SEAM establishes an automated and self-evolving evaluation paradigm. Experiments across four target MLLMs and ten hallucination types demonstrate that SEAM substantially outperforms representative static benchmarks, achieving an 89.2\\% relative improvement in mean valid hallucination discovery rate across 12 matched comparisons.","authors":["Shiyu Zhang","Zengyi Wo","Yuming Liao","Ran He","Caifeng Shan","Yanbo Fan"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:57:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:57:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34598v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34598v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34598v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34591v1","title":"ADClaw: A Programmable Visual Agent for Industrial Anomaly Detection and Understanding","abstract":"Industrial anomaly detection (IAD), the task of identifying and localizing deviations from normal product conditions, is essential to automated quality inspection in manufacturing. Many vision-language models for IAD rely on large domain-specific datasets for fine-tuning and make predictions in a single pass, leaving no opportunity to re-examine ambiguous regions. Although tool-using agents can revisit these regions, predefined tool interfaces limit how they can combine intermediate results in a follow-up check. When such checks are insufficient, models may miss subtle defects or misidentify the defect type. To address this limitation, we introduce ADClaw, an inspection agent that uses executable code to test anomaly hypotheses without task-specific fine-tuning. For each hypothesis, a Structured Normality Prior identifies the applicable rules and retrieves normal visual references for the inspection target. Guided by this prior, the agent writes and executes code in a persistent Inspection Workbench to combine masks, anomaly maps, and measurements from earlier steps into a new test of the suspected defect. After each check, Hypothesis-Guided Evidence Reasoning assesses whether the evidence supports the suspected defect and its location, using gaps or inconsistencies in that evidence to guide the next code action. ADClaw achieves competitive results in detection and localization across 2D and 3D industrial anomaly detection benchmarks and in anomaly understanding on MMAD.","authors":["Xinyi Wu","Rongxin Gao","YuHui Lin","Yonglin Hong","Chu Li","Wei Li","Dingzhao Li","Shijia Zhao","Chaopeng Li","Haixin Sun"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:51:47Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:51:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34591v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34591v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34591v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34581v1","title":"ChemoMAE: Self-Supervised Spectral Representation Learning Reveals Chemical Heterogeneity in Historical Wood","abstract":"In historical timber buildings, multiple environmental factors act over centuries on wood whose chemical composition varies across anatomical regions, producing complex patterns of degradation. Understanding how timber members degrade is important for the effective conservation and restoration of historical buildings, yet the mechanisms underlying these patterns remain unclear. To investigate these mechanisms, we used self-supervised learning to establish a common basis for comparing specimens from timber members that differ in wood species and service history. We examined transverse sections of 49 wood specimens from several historical buildings in Japan using near-infrared hyperspectral imaging. ChemoMAE, a self-supervised Transformer, learns pixelwise spectral representations through masked denoising that exploits multicollinearity among spectral variables. Clustering these representations yields spatial maps without predefined chemical-state categories. Because anatomy and degradation can produce spatially continuous regions, we formulated Local Label Agreement (LLA) to quantify agreement among labels assigned to neighboring pixels. Using spectra alone, ChemoMAE yielded higher mean LLA than baseline methods on validation specimens. We then trained ChemoMAE anew and fitted cluster centers across all specimens. Spatial structures consistent with wood anatomy and the expected effects of surface exposure emerged in the resulting maps. By cross-referencing regional spectra and spatial distributions across specimens in light of wood anatomy, service histories, and prior studies, we proposed models of internal and surface degradation as mechanistic hypotheses. This exploratory AI for Science framework gives researchers a basis for linking latent patterns learned through self-supervision with domain knowledge when investigating how material states form.","authors":["Ryuji Yamaguchi","Miyuki Matsuo"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:45:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:45:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34581v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34581v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34581v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34575v1","title":"Sage-Med: Scaling Medical Multimodal Intelligence","abstract":"Establishing effective scaling laws for medical multimodal large language models (MLLMs) remains a persistent and fundamental challenge. Our systematic evaluation shows that existing medical MLLMs tend to underperform frontier general-purpose MLLMs across a range of medical tasks, while scaling model size primarily improves text capabilities rather than multimodal perception, and enabling reasoning modes fails to close this visual gap. To investigate how medical multimodal intelligence scales in practice, we introduce Sage-Med-Preview, a 35B-parameter medical multimodal model with 3B activated parameters, built by combining stage-specific curation over 322.7 million candidate records with a progressive training curriculum spanning medical pretraining, staged supervised fine-tuning, and adaptive reinforcement learning. Across this curriculum, progressive supervised refinement and adaptive reinforcement learning yield consistent stage-wise gains, substantially advancing both specialized visual understanding and medical language capabilities. Evaluated against 77 competing models across 238 clinical tasks spanning 61 public and 13 private multi-center benchmarks in more than 40 modalities, Sage-Med-Preview achieves an average score of 58.26% on the 31 public multimodal benchmarks. It outperforms the state-of-the-art open-source medical model Hulu-Med-235B-A22B at 50.99% and the general scientific model Intern-S2-Preview at 52.24%, and approaches the advanced proprietary model GPT-6.2-Sol across multiple tasks such as comprehensive multimodal understanding, report generation, and visual grounding. Although substantial challenges remain in complex medical reasoning and multi-step clinical workflows, this report provides empirical evidence, actionable data and training recipes, and a rigorous benchmark toward scalable clinical multimodal intelligence.","authors":["Sage-Med Team",":","Ming Hu","Cheng Tang","Wei Li","Rongbin Li","Feilong Tang","Junru Hu","Hongze Zhu","Qiujuan Yan","Huanze Tang","Shaocong Wei","Siyuan Yan","Min Cen","Zhongxing Xu","Zhonghua Wang","Lie Ju","Zhen Yu","Wenjie Li","Shiyan Su","Jing Hao","Jingyong Liang","Diping Song","Fang Yan","Chenglong Ma","Junzhi Ning","Haochen Xue","Zanting Ye","Chenrun Wang","Jinjie Wei","Yi Xin","Jianghan Shen","Siqi Luo","Jiyao Liu","Huidong Xie","Jun Zhao","Rulin Zhou","Longquan Jiang","Ziyan Huang","Ying Chen","Hanxiao Zhang","Junyang Wu","Mouxiao Bian","Jiashi Lin","Chenyu Wang","Qi Qin","Jie Huang","Yiling Zhou","Jiawei Chen","Kefan Wang","Jingjing Hua","Yaoyu Liu","Huihui Xu","Tianbin Li","Jin Ye","Ziyang Chen","Shujian Gao","Zilin Lu","Jiangtao Yan","Changkai Ji","Wenhao Tang","Xinyi Zeng","Yuxing Chen","Hongwei Yang","Rongzhao Zhang","Jinru Ding","Yingcheng Wu","Zhenfei Yin","Jinzhe Li","Lu Lu","Yun Zhong","Zhishan Lin","Bingli Wang","Xiaohan Ren","Jianmin Qian","Penghao Zhao","Zhongbo Tian","Zhenbang Zhou","Peiling Dai","Mengjing Zhao","Jingyu Zheng","Pinan Liu","Shiwei Li","Victoria Mar","Cristina Vico-Alonso","Qiang Wu","Xianming Liu","Lingtong Huang","Dili Song","Minghui Zhang","Wenlong Zhang","Qipeng Guo","Yuqiang Li","Nanqing Dong","Yun Gu","Qi Duan","Hongliang Ren","Hongming Shan","Yin Xie","Xiang An","Benyou Wang","Yueming Su","Yefeng Zheng","Zongyuan Ge","Chun-Mei Feng","Jie Xu","Yu Qiao","Junjun He","Yirong Chen"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:39:45Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:39:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34575v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34575v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34575v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34568v1","title":"Physics-Guided Generative Modelling of Oxy-Combustion Flame Images: From Conditional LSGANs to Denoising Diffusion Models","abstract":"Validating industrial oxy-combustion burner designs traditionally requires costly experimental campaigns in which each operating condition is physically reproduced on an instrumented rig and imaged via OH* chemiluminescence, rendering iterative design exploration prohibitively slow. This work develops deep generative surrogate models that map a 15-dimensional vector of continuous operating parameters (thermal power, swirl numbers, momentum ratios, mixture fractions, etc.) directly to a $512\\times64$ heat-release flame image, trained on 1,609 experimental samples from the OXYTEC dual-swirl non-premixed oxy-combustion database. Two complementary generative paradigms are investigated, as they offer opposite strengths for surrogate modelling: a custom spatially-anchored conditional least-squares GAN (cLSGAN-L1-CBN), whose single-pass generation enables near-instantaneous inference, and a custom physics-guided denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM), whose iterative sampling favours the smooth, continuous heat-release gradients of fluid flows. Both are validated through ablation on a stratified held-out test set. The optimized cLSGAN reaches RMSE 0.022 and FID 20.68 and generates an image in about 20 ms on a mobile workstation GPU, though extended training leaves its texture realism unchanged; the DDPM improves steadily over long training horizons, reaching RMSE 0.019 and FID 20.29 at 40,000 epochs, at the cost of roughly thirty seconds per image on the same hardware --- three orders of magnitude slower than the adversarial surrogate. A nearest-neighbour analysis traces the persistent worst-case errors to sparsely sampled regions of the parameter space, and a parameter-sensitivity study confirms smooth, physically consistent responses to perturbed inputs. These results demonstrate that carefully guided generative models can act as reliable visual surrogates for combustion experiments.","authors":["Imane Er-rabhi","Gorkem Oztarlik","Jean Caudal","Renaud Gaudron"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:35:33Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:35:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34568v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34568v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34568v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34564v1","title":"Score-based Black-box Attack via Adversarial Condition Guided Diffusion Model","abstract":"Generative models enable unrestricted adversarial attacks that aim to fool classifiers while preserving the natural appearance and semantics of the original image. However, existing diffusion-based black-box attacks largely rely on transferability from surrogates, making their effectiveness sensitive to differences between surrogate and victim. Improving transferability can also distort semantic content and degrade image quality. Moreover, retaining the original prompt to preserve fidelity leaves the diffusion model's conditional prior underused for adversarial guidance. We address these limitations by adapting score-based black-box attacks to reverse diffusion, using the victim's output probabilities to guide latent updates without accessing its gradients. We further introduce adaptive-target guidance, which uses the victim's current runner-up class as an adversarial condition to steer denoising towards the decision boundary. Because such guidance can introduce unwanted semantic changes, we also propose target-search guidance, which searches for a small perturbation of the original condition embedding using a score-based attack. This achieves comparable attack effectiveness with better image quality than runner-up guidance. Experiments on the ImageNet-Compatible dataset show that our framework outperforms transfer-based baselines in both attack effectiveness and image quality.","authors":["Yuhang Chen","Chih-Hong Cheng","Kurt Debattista","Siddartha Khastgir","Xingyu Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:33:29Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:33:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34564v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34564v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34564v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34561v1","title":"Compact Vision-Language Navigation in Continuous Environmentsvia On-Policy Training and Route-Bearing Output","abstract":"Instruction-following robots need navigation policies that reach high accuracy and remain inexpensive to train and to run on a single graphics processing unit (GPU). The most accurate vision-language navigation policies for continuous environments rely on language models with 4 to 8 billion parameters, long observation histories, and large auxiliary corpora. Two properties of the standard training setup limit smaller policies. Imitation labels cover the states that an expert visits, whereas the running policy reaches states that its own errors produce. In addition, an image-plane waypoint exists only while the route lies inside the camera view. We present \\textbf{\\paper}, a monocular red-green-blue (RGB) policy with 2 billion (2B) parameters that addresses both limitations. \\paper adapts Qwen3-VL-2B with low-rank adapters and processes an eight-frame memory and the current view in one forward pass. The policy predicts the route direction as a bearing in the body frame of the agent, which remains defined for every heading, and a deterministic controller converts this bearing into motion. This design allows training to relabel the states that the policy itself visits. On-policy correction improves success, while bearing decoding outperforms image-plane waypoint scanning across all evaluated start headings with identical weights. On the \\valunseen split, \\paper achieves \\rFinSR\\% success on Room-to-Room in Continuous Environments (\\rtor), averaged over three seeds, and \\rRxFinSR\\% on its Room-Across-Room counterpart (\\rxr). Inference requires \\rDecMs\\,ms and \\rInferGB\\,GiB of memory, while training takes \\rCostTrainTyp\\ GPU-hours on one 12\\,GB consumer GPU.","authors":["Reshad Ul Karim","Syeda Maliha Tabassum","Md. Khalilur Rhaman","Md Rashad Al Hasan Rony"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:32:39Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:32:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34561v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34561v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34561v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34560v1","title":"M$^{2}$AdaptVLA: Multimodal Memory-Augmented Vision-Language-Action Model with Predictive Test-Time Adaptation","abstract":"Effective robotic manipulation calls for multimodal memory and rapid adaptation to shifts in contact dynamics. However, many existing policies, which retain either visual or wrench history, may miss key cues from the other modality. Moreover, even with wrench memory, fixed policies can struggle when contact conditions shift at deployment. To this end, we introduce M$^{2}$AdaptVLA, a vision-language-action model that integrates visual and wrench memories with predictive test-time adaptation. For visual memory, the model uses test-time training (TTT) to encode observation history in fast weights. Each entry in the bounded TTT bank combines the corresponding frame's features with a readout from these weights, preserving cues that predate the oldest retained frame. For wrench memory, a context gate controls how much the action expert draws on wrench history as it jointly denoises future actions and wrenches. For predictive test-time adaptation, the auxiliary wrench prediction loss also provides online self-supervision. Using measured wrenches as targets, we update low-rank policy adapters by backpropagating only through the final denoising step. Real-world experiments show that the TTT bank outperforms visual memory baselines on progress tracking, scene recall, and demonstration following. On contact-sensitive tasks, the context gate yields higher success rates than direct injection or gated cross-attention. Predictive test-time adaptation improves robustness to shifts in contact dynamics. On eight tasks requiring visual and wrench memory, M$^{2}$AdaptVLA achieves a mean success rate of 82.71%, a 31.46% absolute gain over the strongest baseline. The project page is available at https://tlf-tlf.github.io/M2AdaptVLAPage/.","authors":["Liufan Tan","Tong Wang","Yuehao Yin","Jian Chen","Yihang Yin","Ying Qu","Wenyu Li","Ruyi Gan","Hao Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:32:21Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:32:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34560v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34560v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34560v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34549v1","title":"CADENCE: Sensor-GroundedWorld-Consistent Gaussian SLAM for Collaborative Mobile Reconstruction","abstract":"Collaborative reconstruction with consumer phones promises faster and more complete indoor maps. However, heterogeneous devices introduce degraded observations and unreliable cross-device associations, so a single poor contributor or incorrect match can corrupt the shared Gaussian map. To address this, we propose \\textbf{CADENCE}, a collaborative Gaussian simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) system that separates finding cross-device matches from deciding whether and how peer observations update the map. To find matches, a sensor-grounded regional memory summarizes committed observations and ranks candidate peer regions using appearance, relative pose, gravity, barometric altitude, and observation quality. Deterministic geometric verification and optional physical checks then decide which of the proposed matches are accepted. Once a match is accepted, separate cue-based confidences control how its observations contribute to pose constraints, geometry, appearance, and memory. As a result, useful depth can still contribute even when color is unreliable. Throughout this process, local tracking remains isolated from mapping, verification, and networking. We evaluate CADENCE on eight Matterport3D houses that were not used to train the memory. On these houses, regional memory achieves an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of $0.95$ to $0.97$ and thus outperforms appearance retrieval, while verification keeps false-link rates below $0.5\\,\\%$. Furthermore, soft confidence weighting reduces the mean receiver peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) loss from $2.66$ to $1.05$\\,dB across eight degradations while retaining useful peer depth. Finally, using inertial and platform motion, CADENCE tracks all twelve held-out iPhone scans with a mean absolute trajectory error of $6.98$\\,cm, and its refined maps achieve better average rendering quality than the baselines.","authors":["Reshad Ul Karim","Abrar Samin","Md Rashad Al Hasan Rony"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:26:02Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:26:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34549v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34549v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34549v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34545v1","title":"Recalling the Past to Answer the Present: Context-Aware Multi-Evidence Reasoning for Streaming Video Question Answering","abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in video understanding, yet existing video question answering (VQA) benchmarks mainly evaluate models in offline settings. Real-world streaming scenarios instead require models to answer context-dependent queries by retrieving and reasoning over evidence distributed across the video history. We introduce StreamRecall, a benchmark for context-aware Multi evidence reasoning in streaming video QA. StreamRecall requires models to retrieve, associate, and reason over temporally scattered evidence to answer queries at the appropriate moment. It contains 4,983 questions over 462 videos, with context-aligned query times and fine-grained temporal evidence annotations. We further propose the Evidence Utilization Cascade to diagnose model reasoning through evidence localization, grounding, chain completion, and answer derivation. To enable efficient long-term streaming reasoning, we present EviStream, a training-free causal visual memory mechanism that preserves reasoning-critical evidence under a fixed KV budget. Experiments reveal substantial gaps between current VLMs and realistic streaming requirements, establishing StreamRecall as a foundation for developing evidence-aware streaming video intelligence.","authors":["Cui Yakun","Tiantian Geng","Huixiang Kong","Xingqun Qi","Sirui Han","Yike Guo"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:24:02Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:24:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34545v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34545v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34545v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34544v1","title":"HAS: Hierarchical Adaptive Frame Sampling for Video Understanding","abstract":"Due to context-length constraints, most MLLMs cannot process full-length videos and therefore rely on sampling a subset of frames as input. However, existing sampling methods, ranging from uniform sampling to relevance-based selection, are often driven by a single sampling principle and thus struggle to accommodate the heterogeneous evidential requirements of different queries: some demand a holistic understanding of how the video unfolds over time, while others hinge on fine-grained events within a short temporal window. To address this limitation, we propose \\textbf{Hierarchical Adaptive Frame Sampling (HAS)}, a two-stage frame sampling framework. In the first stage, \\emph{Backbone Frame Construction}, we apply a Determinantal Point Process (DPP) to sample frames that are both query-relevant and non-redundant. These selected frames capture key moments across the video and form the backbone of the sampling set, providing a foundation for subsequent enrichment. In the second stage, \\emph{Adaptive Contextual Enrichment}, we analyze the temporal distribution of the backbone frames to infer the query's requirements and adaptively allocate the remaining frame budget between Local and Global Context. The Local Context enriches the backbone with fine-grained temporal dynamics and short-range causal relations, whereas the Global Context connects temporally isolated evidence and provides a holistic view of the entire video to support global understanding. Through such a hierarchical two-stage process, HAS effectively addresses diverse query requirements. Incorporated into three leading MLLMs, it demonstrates consistently superior performance across the Video-MME, LongVideoBench, and MLVU benchmarks.","authors":["Yuyao Sun","Daiqi Shi","Shuang Li","Liao Zhang","Yang Cai","Deqing Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:23:58Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:23:58Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34544v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34544v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34544v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34521v1","title":"PURA: Video Token Pruning via Unified Spatiotemporal Redundancy Assessment","abstract":"Video large language models incur substantial inference costs from long visual token sequences. Existing video token compression methods mitigate these costs by exploiting spatial redundancy within frames and temporal redundancy across frames. However, many approaches impose predefined processing orders or fixed allocation ratios for spatial and temporal compression, limiting their ability to adapt to the varying balance of spatiotemporal redundancy across videos. To address this limitation, we propose PURA, a training-free method that prunes visual tokens through unified spatiotemporal redundancy assessment. For each token, PURA constructs spatial and temporal reference candidates and selects the reference with the lowest representation error across the two dimensions. The representation error is combined with token importance to determine each token's removal cost. To avoid the time overhead of removing the lowest-cost token one by one, we introduce Coordinated Pruning, which iteratively groups mutually compatible low-cost removals into batches for simultaneous execution and updates the pruning state after each batch until the target budget is reached. Extensive experiments on three video large language models and five video understanding benchmarks demonstrate that PURA achieves a favorable trade-off between task performance and inference efficiency.","authors":["Yuyao Sun","Hongli Yuan","Shuang Li","Tao Deng","Deqing Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:08:53Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:08:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34521v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34521v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34521v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34515v1","title":"Decoder-as-a-Probe: Verbalizing and Steering Visual Representations in LVLMs","abstract":"It is commonly accepted that high-level concepts are represented linearly in the representation space of large neural networks. For Large Vision Language Models (LVLMs), finding those interpretable directions in visual representations is important for interpretability. To find them, the classical approach is to train a linear classifier on the representations, but it needs a large amount of labels and only recovers what can be read from the representations, not what the model actually uses. In this paper we start from the following insight: The LLM Decoder of an LVLM is also a classifier whose classes are the possible next tokens. So we use its first-order Taylor expansion as a linear classifier. Hence, we introduce Decoder-as-a-Probe (DaP), which significantly outperforms classical probing and steering baselines especially when training data is sparse. (1) It verbalizes what an LVLM perceives in a given image. (2) It can surgically change the perception of the LVLM about specific attributes (e.g. change only the color, the clothes worn or the action done). Due to its simplicity and data efficiency, we believe DaP can serve as a plug-and-play tool for LVLM research, such as interpretability, hallucination mitigation or instruction tuning.","authors":["Adhemar de Senneville","Xavier Bou","Jérémy Anger","Rafael Grompone","Gabriele Facciolo"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:04:57Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:04:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34515v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34515v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34515v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34509v1","title":"Long-Horizon Planning with Latent World Models via Graph Anchors","abstract":"Latent world models plan in a compact latent space by predicting the outcomes of actions. Their planners judge a plan by the latent distance between its predicted end state and the goal image. We show that this distance fails in two distinct ways. First, it loses directional signal a few steps from the goal, causing distant goals to fail. Second, it weighs the robot arm over the manipulated object, causing manipulation to fail at contact. We measure both failures directly on recorded data by asking whether a lower latent distance corresponds to true progress toward the goal. Across five environments, six visual representations (including raw pixels and V-JEPA 2), and nine Meta-World tasks, this ranking test cleanly separates the two failures, and the graph distance to a goal predicts where closed-loop planning breaks down. Both failures share a single remedy: replacing the distant goal with a nearby target that lies on a recorded trajectory. We realize this principle with Graph Anchors, a training-free planner for frozen latent world models. It builds a sparse graph once over the model's own training frames and aims each planning step at a node a few moves ahead along the shortest path to the goal, keeping paths on recorded transitions wherever contact matters. On goals up to 400 steps away, Graph Anchors raise LeWM's success from 13% to 85% on PointMaze-Medium, from 8% to 87% on PointMaze-Large, and from 5% to 71% on Push-T, where stronger search toward the goal fails and where prior training-free planners break down under missing step horizons, subsampling shortcuts, or fragmented contact.","authors":["Syed Nazmus Sakib","Abdul Monaf Chowdhury","Peizhen Li","Nafiul Haque","Chandak Chakma","Md Mehedi Hasan","Zehong Cao","Shifat E. Arman"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:58:31Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:58:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34509v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34509v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34509v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34489v1","title":"Who Is Ego? Scaling Cross-View Camera Wearer Identification and Segmentation beyond Humans","abstract":"A first-person (ego) image reveals what a camera sees, but not who or what carries it. Given a synchronized third-person (exo) image, camera wearer identification asks who is ego: which candidate carries the ego camera? We extend this task beyond humans to robots, vehicles, animals, and drones, and broaden its spatial scope (where is ego) from selecting a provided candidate mask to wearer segmentation with absence prediction and mutual camera localization across ego-exo views. We introduce FindEgo-1M, comprising 1.16M real and simulated ego-exo pairs across 12 wearer types, 17 domains, and 111 test settings. Our approach exploits a geometric relation: the ego camera's projection into the exo image, i.e., the epipole, lies on or near its wearer. We introduce EgoRay-ZS, the first method to turn a frozen feed-forward 3D reconstruction model, VGGT-$Ω$, into a training-free solution for wearer identification and segmentation. Its geometric readout ranks candidate masks or segmentation proposals by angular offset and distance to the ego camera, while the relative pose provides mutual camera directions. We then develop the full EgoRay model through pose-supervised training on FindEgo-1M and label-free test-time adaptation constrained by candidate masks. Without task-specific training, EgoRay-ZS achieves 78.0% identification accuracy on TF2023's original labels, versus 52.9% reported for the dataset-trained PEN. On FindEgo-1M, EgoRay achieves state-of-the-art chance-corrected accuracy of 87.1 and 77.7 on seen and unseen settings. On matched queries, EgoRay also outperforms GPT-6 Astra by 31.6 and 21.3 points on seen and unseen settings, respectively, while running $18\\times$ faster. Data, code, and models will be released.","authors":["Yifan Jin","Zheng Zhang","Tianwen Qian","Dong Wang","Bo Zhao","Yanwei Fu","Yuqian Fu"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:48:48Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:48:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34489v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34489v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34489v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34476v1","title":"TACC: Target-Aware Carrier Compression for Dense-Context Novel View Synthesis","abstract":"Recent feed-forward approaches mark a paradigm shift in novel view synthesis by eliminating the reliance on per-scene optimization, directly inferring 3D representations from input views. However, their reliance on global scene-token context incurs substantial memory and latency overhead, constraining their ability to handle increasingly dense source-view contexts. Compressing source representations offers an intuitive way to limit this token budget. Prior general-purpose compression methods are not tailored for novel view synthesis, as their fixed, target-agnostic nature fails to adapt evidence allocation to individual target views. To overcome this, we introduce TACC, a target-aware compression module tailored for feed-forward latent NVS. Specifically, TACC organizes each source view into a progressive carrier hierarchy. Driven by source-target geometric relevance, it adaptively retrieves tokens to dynamically allocate exact computational quotas. This selective retrieval directly yields a compact, target-conditioned representation. Extensive experiments on the RE10K demonstrate that under dense input settings, TACC achieves $9.23\\times$ and $64\\times$ higher Effective FPS than Full Context on LagerNVS and LVSM, respectively. Effective FPS excludes one-time source encoding and Carrier construction.","authors":["Yiming Fan","Yijia Guo","Zewen Guo","Liwen Hu","Yuheng Yuan","Yang Li","Zhiwei Li","Xitong Ling","Caihong Yuan","Shengbo Chen","Tiejun Huang","Lei Ma"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:41:33Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:41:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34476v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34476v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34476v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34465v1","title":"Evidence-Aligned Benchmarking and Learning for Medical Video Language Models","abstract":"A medical video language model should answer from the visual evidence its question asks about. We find three places where this breaks down and address each with an approach we call MedEvidU. First, benchmarks reward answers that ignore the video: on one dataset, the position of a clip within its recording predicts the surgical step in 37% of questions, and a constant answer beats every trained model on a standard safety-assessment metric. We build MedVidBench-v2, 34 tasks from 18 surgical and clinical video datasets, in which prompts and metrics are audited with answers given without video, and which tests unseen hospitals and windows cut blindly from whole recordings. Second, common video encoders merge consecutive frames into shared tokens, so a question about one frame is answered from a blend of two. Query-slot inputs give each queried frame its own tokens at almost no extra cost. Third, a fine-tuned model has memorized its training questions, which leaves little to learn from. Label-matched question resampling (LQR) adds unseen questions about the same recordings while keeping the answer distribution fixed, in a final supervised epoch and in reinforcement learning. Together, these raise the grounding IoU of a 4B model from 30.4 to 44.4, above a supervised 27B model, and instrument grounding at an unseen hospital from 33.9 to 72.5. On blindly cut windows, event-presence accuracy improves by 5.5 points and localization F1 by 8.1 points. Project page: https://gaozhongpai.github.io/MedEvidU/","authors":["Zhongpai Gao","Anwesa Choudhuri","Meng Zheng","Benjamin Planche","Terrence Chen","Ziyan Wu"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:35:42Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:35:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34465v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34465v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34465v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34461v1","title":"SceneDesigner++: A Unified 9-DoF Pose Control Framework for T2I and T2V Generation","abstract":"Precise 9-DoF pose control is essential for visual content generation, enabling users to specify object location, size, and orientation in 3D space. However, existing methods typically focus on either text-to-image (T2I) or text-to-video (T2V) generation, with limitations in pose controllability and temporal coherence. We present SceneDesigner++, a unified framework for 9-DoF pose control across both T2I and T2V generation. We construct ObjectPose9D++, a dataset containing high-quality images and videos with accurate pose annotations. Trained on ObjectPose9D++, SceneDesigner++ combines absolute and relative pose encoding strategies to decouple frame-wise spatial grounding from inter-frame motion modeling. Specifically, absolute pose encoding leverages CNOCS map sequences to provide explicit, frame-wise 9-DoF pose guidance. Meanwhile, our relative pose encoding, termed VPRoPE, incorporates relative pose information into rotary position embeddings to explicitly model inter-frame pose transitions, thereby promoting temporally coherent object motion. A learnable orthogonal transformation is further introduced for each attention head, allowing different heads to capture complementary geometric relationships while preserving input feature norms to promote training stability. We further adopt a two-stage training strategy to learn the absolute and relative pose encoding mechanisms separately. Benefiting from this lightweight and decoupled design, SceneDesigner++ also supports integration with an independently trained camera-control module, enabling separate or joint control over object and camera motion. Finally, we introduce T2IVPoseEval, a benchmark for evaluating diverse object poses and motion patterns in both T2I and T2V tasks. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SceneDesigner++ consistently outperforms same-backbone baselines in pose fidelity and motion quality.","authors":["Xincheng Shuai","Zhenyuan Qin","Henghui Ding","Dacheng Tao"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:33:51Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:33:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34461v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34461v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34461v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34457v1","title":"ProxyGenBench: Benchmarking Proxy-Guided Video Generation","abstract":"Recent video generators produce realistic videos, yet controlling how scenes unfold remains challenging. Structural proxies, such as animated 3D blockouts, guide generation by specifying scene layout, motion, camera behavior, and event order. However, these coarse inputs leave details unspecified and may contain artifacts that should not be reproduced. Successful proxy-guided generation must therefore Preserve intended structure, Complete missing content and correct proxy artifacts, and Bind text, image, and audio conditions to the right entities and events. We introduce ProxyGenBench to study these interacting requirements, with 312 evaluation cases from 60 scenes across three scene tasks and controlled variations in proxy fidelity, text detail, and multimodal inputs. We evaluate five video generators using verified, case-specific checklists. Experiments show that proxies improve spatiotemporal control, but greater proxy detail can coincide with poorer completion, while strong structural adherence does not ensure correct binding.","authors":["Zhenchen Tang","Haolin Lu","Songlin Yang","Xiaotong Zhao","Ran Zhang","Bo Peng","Shuai Li","Haotian Fan","Haobin Zhong","Yanni Zhang","Songlian Li","Ning Zhang","Xun Gao","Alan Zhao","Jing Dong"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:31:23Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:31:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34457v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34457v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34457v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34435v1","title":"Most of the Way There: Turning a Strong Video Prior into an Action-Conditioned World Model","abstract":"Modern video generators excel at producing visually plausible futures guided by text and image prompts. Yet their emphasis on semantic plausibility does not guarantee fidelity to the physical consequences of specific actions. Action-conditioned world models (ACWMs), in contrast, aim to simulate how the world responds to robot actions, enabling scalable policy evaluation and improvement without costly real-world rollouts. The challenge is not merely to generate a plausible future, but to predict what follows from a specific physical action. As video priors grow stronger, we ask how little adaptation is truly needed to bridge this gap. We introduce Dianjing, a minimal recipe that preserves and unlocks the capabilities of strong video priors through unified physical action conditioning, training concentrated at the high noise levels where motion and layout, and thus the consequences of actions, are decided, and native history conditioning. Built upon MiniMax H3, a 33B video foundation model, Dianjing freezes the pretrained backbone and trains only 0.34% of its parameters using approximately 65 hours of robot video across two embodiments, without additional robot-specific pretraining or camera calibration. Controlled ablations show that where training is allocated across noise levels matters more than how actions enter the network. On WorldOracle, which evaluates predictions against paired real-world executions, Dianjing achieves the best scores on four of the five evaluation tracks and ranks second on AgiBot-Long. Our findings suggest that strong video priors already bring us most of the way to world modeling; lightweight physical action grounding can unlock their potential for real-world prediction.","authors":["Zanyi Wang","Xia Chen","Liu Liu","Xianglong Guo","Dengyang Jiang","Haodong Li","Tingyang Xiao","Wenhao Hu","Zhizhong Su"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:19:27Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:19:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34435v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34435v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34435v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34430v1","title":"One Representation to Rule Them All -- Open-Vocabulary Change Detection from Text or Visual Examples","abstract":"Open-vocabulary change detection (OVCD) aims to localise user-specified changes without retraining for predefined categories. Existing approaches typically rely on textual descriptions and combine separate models for region proposal, semantic recognition, and temporal comparison. This limits how target concepts can be expressed and makes detection dependent on several intermediate predictions. We introduce OVTSID3, a training-free framework that unifies region grouping, concept identification, and temporal comparison in the representation space of DINOv3. OVTSID3 supports two complementary modes of concept specification: zero-shot textual queries or one-shot visual prompting through a masked reference pair. OVTSID3 outperforms all prior training-free OVCD methods on two building and two natural-disaster benchmarks, raising the average F1 by 8.9 points. OVTSID3 also supports semantic change detection and achieves state-of-the-art on SECOND. These results demonstrate the flexibility of a unified representation for text- or example-guided change detection without task-specific model training.","authors":["Blaž Rolih","Matic Fučka","Filip Wolf","Luka Čehovin Zajc"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:16:57Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:16:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34430v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34430v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34430v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34422v1","title":"Can Visual World Models See Force?","abstract":"World models and vision-language-action policy models are increasingly proposed as foundations for robot manipulation. They predict how scenes evolve and which actions to take, yet successful manipulation depends not solely on following observable trajectories but also on the hidden variable of interaction force. Neurophysiology has shown that humans possess a visual prior for this hidden variable, motivating us to ask the question: Do the representations of visual foundation models also capture the forces involved in an interaction? We define force awareness as the ability of representations to (1) indicate when contact force is applied, (2) track how it changes during an interaction, and (3) reflect how large it is. We probe frozen image, video, world- and policy model representations with a lightweight readout trained on synchronized video and force measurements from two manipulation datasets. Our experiments show that most models capture when contact occurs but not how much force is being applied. Surprisingly, robot post-training does not improve this either, highlighting an important gap in current models capabilities.","authors":["Felix Tristram","Benjamin D. Killeen","Nassir Navab"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:12:10Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:12:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34422v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34422v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34422v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34420v1","title":"DocSEAM: Bidirectional Cross-Page Encoding for Multi-Page Document Understanding","abstract":"Multi-page document vision-language models encode each page independently, creating a feature-level discontinuity at page boundaries: visual tokens are formed without access to content on adjacent pages, and the language model must reason over these page representations formed in isolation. We introduce DocSEAM (Document Selective Enrichment via Anchored Masking), a lightweight module inserted between a pretrained vision encoder and the language model that injects the inductive bias of local cross-page context into visual representations. For each page, DocSEAM forms a local triplet with its two neighbors, applies center-anchored masked self-attention with directional salience gates to fuse information from neighbor pages, and returns only the enriched center-page tokens, leaving the visual token count unchanged. After a supervised cross-page warm-up and multi-page fine-tuning with DocSEAM frozen, our 9B-parameter model achieves state-of-the-art results among the evaluated open-weight models of up to 10B parameters on five multi-page document VQA benchmarks. We further show that under controlled re-pagination, DocSEAM reduces sensitivity to boundary placement and improves cross-page span localization","authors":["Hoang-Nhat Nguyen","Chen Change Loy"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:11:06Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:11:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34420v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34420v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34420v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34414v1","title":"SemProxy: Semantic 3D Proxies for Controllable Agentic 3D Modeling","abstract":"Coding agents are increasingly capable of generating 3D models, yet enabling users to effectively control the modeling process remains challenging. Existing workflows rely primarily on text prompts and reference images, which often struggle to convey precise spatial intentions and require iterative corrections. We introduce SemProxy, a lightweight intermediate representation that bridges intuitive human interaction and agentic 3D modeling. Instantiated as labeled 3D bounding boxes, semantic 3D proxies allow users to directly specify object parts and spatial arrangements while leaving geometric details to coding agents. These proxies can be serialized into structured text and rendered as images, making them directly accessible to existing coding agents without additional training or modifications. We further develop ProxyStudio, a user interface that integrates text, image references, and interactive 3D proxy editing into agentic modeling workflows. We demonstrate the versatility of SemProxy across object- and scene-level modeling tasks with different coding agents, and evaluate its effectiveness in supporting controllable human-agent collaborative 3D modeling through a user study.","authors":["Shuyuan Zhang","Changjian Li"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:08:05Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:08:05Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34414v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34414v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34414v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34397v1","title":"Strong Starts, Simple Recipes: Unified Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation from Multimodal Specialists","abstract":"Multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) integrates separately trained domain specialists into a single unified model. Existing methods focus on refining the distillation procedure while initializing the student from a base model or a multi-domain SFT checkpoint, which leaves capability integration largely to distillation itself. Instead, we treat the starting point as a design variable and propose Unified MOPD (Uni-MOPD), which pairs a strong start with a simple recipe: a training-free uniform merge of specialists from the same base model initializes the student, and short MOPD with uniform domain sampling and full-vocabulary reverse KL refines it. Across five multimodal domains and 19 benchmarks, our study establishes three findings. (1) The starting point matters more than subsequent training choices: before any training, the uniform merge already matches the score that the base model reaches after 200 MOPD updates, and after short distillation it matches higher-scoring, search-based starts. (2) Short MOPD recovers most of the capabilities the merge leaves unintegrated; it improves every domain, with the largest gains where the domain teacher still leads. (3) From a strong start, uniform domain sampling and full-vocabulary reverse KL match or exceed every evaluated sampling strategy and divergence, without schedules, per-token weighting, or truncation hyperparameters. Uni-MOPD outperforms Open-MOPD at the same training budget while training 3.3 times faster than its released code. We will release the training framework, specialist checkpoints, and unified student.","authors":["Wenzheng Chang","Taiqiang Wu","Yuqi Xu","Yongfu Zhu","Zelin Peng","Ji Zhao","Shuai Dong","Wenpu Liu","Caoyuan Ma","Xuehui Wang","Ziyue Wang","Weichu Xie","Haiyuan Wan","Ngai Wong","Tong He","Wenqi Shao","Jiaqi Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:59:32Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:59:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34397v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34397v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34397v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34370v1","title":"MoCA4D: Monocular 3D Bounding Box Lifting and Tracking with Camera and Object Motion","abstract":"Monocular 3D bounding box tracking must account for both object motion and changes in camera viewpoint. Framewise box lifting with temporal filtering leaves these motion components implicit, while static-scene fusion assumes stationary objects. We introduce MoCA4D, a framework for 3D box lifting and tracking from monocular video, associated 2D box prompts, and camera intrinsics. An observation branch estimates per-frame geometry, while a dynamic branch predicts a camera transformation shared across objects and a separate rigid transformation for each object. We propose Star Optimization to refine current depth and orientation using the observation and historical boxes propagated through these transformations. The refined box then guides subsequent propagation. Across all evaluated objects in Argoverse 2 and ADT, Star Optimization reduces center motion error by 38.1% and 38.7%, respectively, relative to Kalman filtering of the same observations. On MOVi-F, the dynamic branch achieves the lowest corner and rotation errors and the highest IoU among the evaluated methods over eight-transition motion windows. Real-data camera-only controls further link predicted object motion to improved localization on dynamic-object tracks.","authors":["Changwoo Jeon","Jongyoo Kim"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:34:03Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:34:03Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34370v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34370v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34370v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34360v1","title":"One Patch, Many Policies: Object-Associated Physical Patch Attacks on Robotic Manipulation","abstract":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) and World Action Models (WAMs) are vulnerable to adversarial visual interference, motivating the study of universal and transferable physical patch attacks. We propose Object-Associated Patch Attack (OAPA), a universal and transferable physical patch framework that enables an attacker to select which task object's manipulation to disrupt through patch placement, without re-optimizing the patch. Our framework combines object-associated placement, differentiable physical compositing, and final action-expert representation divergence. Physical compositing accounts for perspective and occlusion across camera views, while representation divergence guides patch optimization using either relative RMS or cosine distance. Experimental results demonstrate strong attack transferability across task suites and policies, together with the ability to selectively disrupt manipulation of an attacker-chosen task object through patch placement.","authors":["Yiqian Liu","Yuqi Lin","Hui Lu","Jiaming He","Qi Zhu","Deyi Ji","Bing Li","Kaizheng Wang","Zhiyuan Zha","Yi Yu"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:28:04Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:28:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34360v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34360v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34360v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34347v1","title":"AIRIS: Benchmarking Social Embodied Intelligence in Egocentric Streaming Videos","abstract":"Embodied agents operating around people must not only recognize social cues, but also decide when the available evidence is sufficient, whether to intervene, and how to respond without violating social norms. Existing benchmarks typically evaluate these abilities in isolation or with access to completed videos, obscuring where an online social decision fails. We introduce AIRIS, a causal streaming benchmark that formulates social embodied intelligence as a time-anchored chain of Attention, Intention, Reaction, and Interaction (A-I-R-I). AIRIS contains 8,000 expert-curated questions grounded in 357 egocentric clips across 11 context categories and 36 capability dimensions. At each query, models receive only the observation prefix available at that time; decision-aware metrics reward calibrated waiting and complete reactions while penalizing premature commitment. Evaluating 45 proprietary and open-source general-purpose, embodied, streaming-video, and omni-modal models reveals fragmented and temporally miscalibrated competence. General-purpose VLMs excel at passive cue grounding and intent recognition, whereas embodied specialists lead on isolated reaction sub-decisions; yet across all families, models frequently guess intents before sufficient evidence emerges and fail to jointly align intervention timing, action domain, and concrete execution within the same episode. AIRIS thus provides a diagnostic foundation for bridging the gap between passive social perception and reliable embodied interaction.","authors":["Yuchen Zhou","Hongsheng Wu","Jiayu Tang","Yunxuan Mao","Hanjing Ye","Shenghao Zhang","Jing Tang","Chun-Kai Fan","Yufei Huang","Yonggen Ling","He Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:18:30Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:18:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34347v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34347v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34347v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34344v1","title":"SPARC: Semantic Calibration with Latent Multimodal Knowledge for Robust Image-Text Retrieval","abstract":"Robust image-text retrieval aims to maintain accurate cross-modal matching when image or text queries are incomplete, supporting reliable information access in real-world applications. However, existing methods typically focus on adapting to distribution shifts, without adequately addressing candidate discrimination for incomplete queries and ranking instability. To this end, we propose semantic projection and alignment with relational constraints (SPARC), which elicits latent multimodal knowledge from frozen encoders to improve candidate discrimination and ranking stability. Specifically, we first devise a semantic calibration module on a product of spheres that refines incomplete visual and textual features through tangent-space updates, maintaining the unit-sphere structure used for cosine similarity. Second, we introduce a robust semantic guidance module that combines latent multimodal knowledge encoded in complete image-text features with cross-modal semantic anchors to promote semantic alignment of the calibrated features. Finally, we introduce a matching relation constraint module that fuses original query features with their calibrated counterparts and applies a soft ranking constraint to penalize reductions in initially positive matching margins, thereby limiting ranking perturbations. Extensive experiments on Flickr30K and COCO demonstrate that SPARC outperforms competing methods under various image occlusion and word deletion settings, while achieving consistent gains across different pretrained backbones.","authors":["Zhongkuan Mao","Wenzhuo Zhao","Haidong Chen","Shangze Li","Yidong Wang","Jinhe Bi","Yunpu Ma","Junfeng Fang","Fei Shen","Tat-Seng Chua"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:16:30Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:16:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34344v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34344v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34344v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34319v1","title":"PostMesh: Self-Rewarded Preference Post-Training for Flow-Based Artist Mesh Generation","abstract":"Artistic mesh generators turn a dense 3D object into an artist mesh: a compact, quad-dominant mesh that is easy to edit. Most of current models are auto-regressive methods which decode a mesh token by token, take tens to hundreds of seconds per mesh. Diffusion- and flow-based generators are much faster, but their meshes still fall short of artist quality. To our knowledge, PostMesh is the first post-training method for a diffusion- or flow-based artist mesh generator, and it needs no expensive artist-made ground truth: the generator is trained on its own samples, scored by a reward computed from the dense input and the output. We propose an artistic mesh reward that keeps the output consistent with the dense input while making the mesh structure into artistic. We also propose a multi-negative flow preference optimisation that transfers the reward to the generator. After a short LoRA post-training, PostMesh is comparable to or better than slow autoregressive generators on most artist-mesh quality metrics and in a blind VLM preference test, at a fraction of their time.","authors":["Gihyun Kwon"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:05:42Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:05:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34319v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34319v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34319v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34292v1","title":"WebVibe: Multimodal Interactive Website Generation via Reverse Synthesis and Visual Self-Distillation","abstract":"Although multimodal large language models translate single screenshots into static HTML, generating interactive functional websites from design specifications still struggles with multi-page routing and stateful widget transitions. Examining existing training pipelines reveals two bottlenecks: (1) in forward data synthesis (X -> Y), writing the specification X before code execution leaves unimplemented or altered requirements in X as supervision noise; and (2) in reinforcement learning, scalar rewards discard the rich state transitions observed during replay. Based on these findings, we introduce WebVibe and Interactive-SDPO to train InteractiveCoder. WebVibe reverses synthesis into Y -> X by verifying cross-page and in-page interactions in the browser before deriving multimodal inputs from replay-confirmed states to build the 31,542-site WebVibe-32K corpus with reusable execution contracts. Interactive-SDPO then conditions a weight-sharing self-teacher on the replay trace and a 2x2 state-transition montage to provide token-level supervision at zero inference-time overhead. On the public Vision2Web L2, one-shot InteractiveCoder-9B nearly 3x its base model's functional score (20.2 to 55.2 FS) and matches a 1T-parameter coding agent in a single pass with 111x fewer total parameters and 78x fewer tokens. On our WebVibeBench, InteractiveCoder-4B and 9B further outperform all evaluated baselines by over 23 points.","authors":["Tianxiang Jiang","Sheng Xia","Yilin Long","Kanghui Tian","Linquan Wu","Yongheng Zhang","Daixian Liu","Di Yin","Yi Wang","Yu Qiao","Qinglong Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:46:28Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:46:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34292v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34292v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34292v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34290v1","title":"StateWeaver: Multi-Agent Coordination of Story-State Transitions for Long-Horizon Video Generation","abstract":"Modern video foundation models generate high-quality short clips, but preserving long-video consistency and correctly depicting event-driven state changes remain challenging. Existing agentic workflows face these challenges because continual planning and repair accumulate context, while reference updates remain limited. We introduce StateWeaver, which combines Active States and Hierarchical Collaboration to connect review-driven visual reference updates with cross-shot state inheritance. Active States translate state discrepancies identified during review into concrete visual asset requirements that guide reference construction, validation, and regeneration, preserving identity and already-correct content while repairing target attributes. Confirmed generation results are linked to the corresponding asset states, story time, and reference versions for verification and reuse by subsequent shots. Hierarchical Collaboration isolates execution contexts by task, clip, and repair round, and uses hierarchical memory to organize this process across multiple sets. Across multiple benchmarks, StateWeaver outperforms existing methods and produces long videos with sustained cross-shot consistency under predefined and autonomously planned storyboards.","authors":["Modi Jin","Xuying Zhang","Yijing Guo","Haoran Li","Yanwen Ma","Jing Wang","Chenyu Ge","Ming-Ming Cheng","Haoyang Huang"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:43:37Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:43:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34290v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34290v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34290v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34288v1","title":"Rehearse in 3D, Render in 2D: Physically Consistent and Editable Robot World Models","abstract":"Embodied world models aim to predict future interactions that are both visually realistic and physically consistent. Yet explicit 3D motion prediction alone does not guarantee that object structure and contact relationships are preserved. Beyond motion prediction, 3D representations provide an explicit state space for imposing structural and physical constraints. Building on this insight, we introduce RehearsalWorld, an editable robot world model driven by 3D Interaction Rehearsal (3DIR). Given an initial RGB-D observation and robot actions, Interaction-Consistent Rehearsal (ICR) uses a pretrained 3D point-flow model as a motion prior. It refines the predicted trajectories through response-aware object grouping, persistent grasp memory, and contact structure refiner, preserving scene stability, object structure, and grasp attachments. Rehearsal-Aligned Video Generation (RAVG) then uses geometric projections of the rehearsal as temporal conditioning. We fine-tune a video generator on projection-video pairs to synthesize realistic interaction videos. Thanks to the decoupling of physical response from visual synthesis, RehearsalWorld also supports independent editing of material properties and camera viewpoints. Compared with PointWorld on real DROID grasping sequences, RehearsalWorld reduces gripper-relative offset by approximately 79% to 84% across deformable, articulated, and rigid objects and lowers reference motion error. For video prediction, it achieves a PSNR of 23.92 dB and an FVD of 149.","authors":["Guangyu Liu","Hanzhang Wang","Huijia Zhang","Hanbei Zhan","Linjie Hu","Bin Xiao","Xuan Zhou","Dong Zhang","Hanwei Liu"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:41:56Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:41:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34288v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34288v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34288v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34284v1","title":"ReMeDi: Single-Image PBR Material Estimation via Reparameterization between Metal and Dielectric Hypotheses","abstract":"Physically based rendering (PBR) material estimation facilitates relighting, material editing, and 3D content creation. As the reflectance of a surface heavily depends on whether it is a metal or a dielectric, producing any map that determines albedo involves a decision on metalness, which we call the \"intended metalness\" of the map. Rendering maps with mismatched intended metalness yields physically implausible or misinterpreted materials. The metallic--roughness workflow makes metalness explicit and thus encourages base color to match it, but does not enforce this match, especially for automated methods directly predicting each map. Therefore, we propose ReMeDi, which enforces the match through two components. First, we reparameterize base color as a metalness-weighted blend of its two variants, estimated under the all-metal and all-dielectric hypothesis, respectively. Second, as available supervision cannot teach a single network both hypotheses at every pixel, we restrict each of two separate networks to one hypothesis, so that each variant is ensured to follow its hypothesis. Besides enforcing the match, this also improves each estimated material map. Experiments show that ReMeDi outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both material accuracy and relighting fidelity.","authors":["Fan Fei","Xin Huang","Yixin Zhu","Jiawei Lu","Xin Yang","Jinglin Liang","Ruining Tang","Yifei Feng","Boxin Shi","Chunchao Guo"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:37:52Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:37:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34284v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34284v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34284v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34279v1","title":"Semantic State Reading for Joint Image-Semantic Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Pretrained visual representations improve diffusion-transformer training, yet exploiting both global context and local spatial structure requires deciding which semantic states to model and how to access them. We introduce Semantic State Reading (SSR), which jointly denoises image latents, a global CLS token encoding image-level context, and a compact patch state preserving spatial correspondence. SSR couples this global-local modeling with distinct reading paths: local states are fused with corresponding image tokens at the input, while independent projections inject the current global state into every transformer block. Both semantic scopes thus remain accessible throughout denoising. Ablations show complementary benefits from local-state modeling and repeated global reading, and semantic-trajectory interventions alter image layout under a fixed class label. On ImageNet $256 \\times 256$, SiT-XL/2 with SSR reaches or surpasses the reference FIDs of SiT, REPA, and Representation Entanglement for Generation (REG) with $100\\times$, $40\\times$, and $2\\times$ fewer training iterations, respectively. SSR achieves unguided FID 1.78 at 2M iterations and FID 1.34 with classifier-free guidance alone. Combining classifier-free guidance with autoguidance yields FID 1.31 at 1M iterations, achieving the best FID in our main comparison table.","authors":["Guanxi Lai","Zixuan Li","Zhiwei Li","Qi Li"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:36:00Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:36:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34279v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34279v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34279v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"}],"favorites":[],"latest":{"generated_at_utc":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","category":"cs.CV","keywords":["video","retrieval","3d","agent","representation","instance","multimodal","diffusion"],"fetch_window_hours":72,"daily_window_hours":30,"match_count":82,"new_or_recent_papers":[{"id":"2611.34944v1","title":"AnyCam: Anything Can Represent Camera for Video Generation","abstract":"Camera-controlled video generation re-renders a video along a target camera trajectory, yet moving the camera around an object, the most natural way to change the viewpoint of an object-centric scene, remains difficult. Existing methods condition either on camera parameters, which are scale-ambiguous in monocular video, or on explicit geometry such as warped point clouds or depth, which breaks down under large viewpoint changes. We present AnyCam, a method that represents the camera with an anchor object in the scene: we reconstruct it in 3D and render it along the target trajectory, a camera signal that is scale-aware, view-complete and easy to specify relative to the object. A video diffusion model transfers the content and dynamics of the source video to the new viewpoint. Its H-Concat layout aligns source and target frames in time, so the model can be distilled into AnyCam-AR, a streaming few-step generator for on-the-fly camera control. Both models are trained on paired videos with pixel-aligned anchor renderings from multi-camera videos and static-scene captures. Experiments on diverse datasets and trajectories show that AnyCam performs favorably against camera-controlled video generation and 3D reconstruction methods in camera control and visual quality.","authors":["Weijie Lyu","Zhixin Shu","Yuntian Ke","Ming-Hsuan Yang"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:59Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:59Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34944v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34944v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34944v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":88955,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Camera-controlled video generation re-renders a video along a target camera trajectory, yet moving the camera around an object, the most natural way to change the viewpoint of an object-centric scene, remains difficult. Existing methods condition either on ca...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34943v1","title":"Tracking Any Point with Agentic Visual Reasoning","abstract":"A general-purpose Vision-Language Model (VLM) has surpassed expert vision models in many domains such as object detection and video recognition. However, point correspondence is known to be challenging for VLMs since it goes beyond object-level recognition: it requires pixel-level localization of the same physical surface point across observations, even when appearance changes or the point disappears and later reappears. In this paper, we introduce AgenTAP, a VLM-based agent that directly predicts point coordinates and visibility and actively reasons over videos. Starting from its own estimate, the agent autonomously chooses what to inspect and uses the resulting evidence to verify point identity and refine its predictions. With GPT-6 Astra, it surpasses expert vision models in both point localization and visibility prediction, without correspondence-specific fine-tuning. We further use AgenTAP to re-identify physical points after disappearance and reconnect fragmented tracks, constructing EgoTAP-4D, a dataset of 3D point trajectories in long real-world egocentric videos. On sampled reappearing points, its predictions approach human agreement, and adding EgoTAP-4D to D4RT training improves average 3D tracking performance.","authors":["Xuweiyi Chen","Jin Yao","Linzhan Mou","Lei Gong","Di Liu","Cheng Zhang","Ziqiao Ma","Sihan Xu","Adam W. Harley","Shengyi Qian","Zezhou Cheng"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:58Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:58Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34943v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34943v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34943v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":146344,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：A general-purpose Vision-Language Model (VLM) has surpassed expert vision models in many domains such as object detection and video recognition. However, point correspondence is known to be challenging for VLMs since it goes beyond object-level recognition: i...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34941v1","title":"Improving and Expanding Video Editing Capabilities Without Videos","abstract":"Image editing models can follow a wide range of instructions, but video editing models still lag behind, limited in part by the difficulty of collecting paired source and edited videos. Is it possible to transfer advanced image editing capabilities to video editors without new paired videos? We introduce Image-to-Video Transfer (I2VT), which improves pretrained video editors and expands them to new editing tasks using an image editor. It enables efficient post-training with no architectural changes and no new videos, using only individual images. Our key idea is to let the video editor role-play as an image editor: it edits an image as a one-frame video, with the image editor guiding the video editor's predictions at each denoising step. Although the video editor sees only single images during training, at test time it applies the improved and expanded capabilities to whole video clips while preserving temporal consistency. To push the boundaries of video editing, we introduce OpenVE++, an extension of OpenVE-Bench that adds four new editing tasks useful for video production, including action changes, expression changes, body movements, and scene relighting. Our experiments across three different video editors show that I2VT improves existing editing capabilities and expands them to new tasks, and our models achieve state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance on both OpenVE-Bench and OpenVE++.","authors":["Ji Xie","Yanhao Jia","Xiangpeng Yang","Martin Q. Ma","Sihan Xu","Erik Cambria","Louis-Philippe Morency"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:55Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34941v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34941v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34941v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":109851,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Image editing models can follow a wide range of instructions, but video editing models still lag behind, limited in part by the difficulty of collecting paired source and edited videos. Is it possible to transfer advanced image editing capabilities to video e...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34940v1","title":"LuxAvatar: Real-Time Relightable Head Avatars via Causal Neural Shading of 3D Intrinsic Proxies","abstract":"We present LuxAvatar, a feed-forward framework for real-time head avatar animation and relighting from a single portrait. To achieve both 3D controllability and complex facial light transport, we factorize avatar synthesis into an animatable 3D intrinsic Gaussian proxy and a causal autoregressive neural shader. This proxy disentangles canonical geometry and reflectance attributes, rapidly rasterizing deformed Gaussians into view- and motion-consistent PBR buffers. Conditioned on these buffers, our neural shader formulates forward rendering as a conditional video diffusion process, bypassing explicit PBR computation to synthesize realistic shading effects under target illumination. We achieve real-time streaming through heterogeneous distillation from a pretrained multi-step bidirectional relighting teacher to a two-step causal autoregressive neural shader, inheriting broad synthetic lighting priors while minimizing conditioning and decoding latency. To further specialize these priors to human heads, we construct a hybrid relighting dataset for domain-specific fine-tuning, capturing authentic facial dynamics and physically grounded light transport. Extensive experiments show that our framework enables 3D-controllable, real-time portrait animation and relighting, outperforming state-of-the-art methods in visual fidelity, motion control, and lighting accuracy.","authors":["Yipengjing Sun","Chenyang Wang","Boyao Zhou","Jiapeng Zhu","Ka Leong Cheng","Shengping Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:46Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34940v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34940v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34940v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":79852,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present LuxAvatar, a feed-forward framework for real-time head avatar animation and relighting from a single portrait. To achieve both 3D controllability and complex facial light transport, we factorize avatar synthesis into an animatable 3D intrinsic Gaus...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34939v1","title":"A Mesh for Every Moment","abstract":"We present MEMO (a Mesh for Every MOment), a method that reconstructs the geometry of complete dynamic scenes, captured by multiple synchronized cameras, and extracts a mesh at every frame. Existing dynamic Gaussian splatting methods either model appearance alone or target isolated objects segmented by foreground masks, and all of them recover meshes by TSDF fusion or Poisson reconstruction, whose fixed grids limit the detail they can capture. At the core of MEMO is a per-Gaussian motion model inspired by Gaussian processes, which represents each Gaussian's position and rotation over time as a combination of temporal Gaussian kernels. The model keeps every Gaussian a valid surface element throughout the sequence and carries an implicit representation of the dynamic geometry, so a mesh can be derived at any time from a field defined by the Gaussians. Although common in real captures, large and fast motion poses a challenge that dynamic Gaussian splatting methods usually overlook. To account for it, we design a track-based initialization scheme that fits each Gaussian's initial trajectory to point tracks in closed form. We evaluate MEMO on Blender-Moments, a new benchmark of nine animated scenes with ground-truth meshes at every frame, and on two real-world datasets. MEMO reconstructs the most accurate geometry among state-of-the-art methods, and remains competitive in novel-view synthesis and training time. Project page: https://memo-4d.github.io","authors":["Roy Or-El","Jeong Joon Park","Ira Kemelmacher-Shlizerman"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:45Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34939v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34939v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34939v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":102883,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We present MEMO (a Mesh for Every MOment), a method that reconstructs the geometry of complete dynamic scenes, captured by multiple synchronized cameras, and extracts a mesh at every frame. Existing dynamic Gaussian splatting methods either model appearance a...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34935v1","title":"Can image generation help visual reasoning? An empirical study","abstract":"Language models think in words; then multimodal models might think in images. We test whether a generated helper improves the answer to a visual question. A model describes the helper it needs and then answers using the original and generated images together. Across 13 vision-language model and generator pairs, generation lowers mean accuracy by 3.7 percentage points, with declines on nine of ten tasks. Two unified models, BAGEL and Janus-Pro-7B, each lose accuracy on eight tasks. On VSTAR, adding a generated image to the same planning hint lowers accuracy by 10.47 points. Repeated image edits yield different outcomes across tasks. ChartQAPro and MathVista stay below their no-generation baselines, while ERQA gains three points after ten edits.","authors":["Xueyang Yu","Yifan Jing","Taiming Lu","Linrong Cai","Zhipeng Cai","Yunyang Xiong","Gabriel Sarch","Zhuang Liu"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:31Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34935v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34935v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34935v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":67406,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Language models think in words; then multimodal models might think in images. We test whether a generated helper improves the answer to a visual question. A model describes the helper it needs and then answers using the original and generated images together....","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34931v1","title":"DynMF2: Rigid Motion Factorization by Expectation-Maximization for Monocular 4D Reconstruction and Motion Segmentation","abstract":"A single casual video leaves a moving scene under-determined: a point seen once from each place can slide toward or away from the camera and still match every frame. We constrain the reconstruction with the structure of the motion itself. Building on DynMF, we model a scene's motion as a handful of SE(3) trajectories, each rigid, and let every point follow one of them alone, never a blend. DynMF2 computes this factorization directly from 3D point trajectories, with no rendering, by Expectation-Maximization (EM). Its M-step fits, in closed form, every rigid motion to the points assigned to it, and its E-step reassigns every point to the motion that explains it with the least error. The background is simply one more motion, the camera's, so no masks are needed. Three tools then revise the factorization: requery adds trajectories where the rendering shows parts the tracker missed, merge joins motions that move together, and split separates those that do not. Thus the number of motions follows the scene. Without masks, DynMF2 clearly outperforms the mask-based baselines in view synthesis on iPhone and NVIDIA and in 3D tracking on iPhone. It recovers what masks leave out, from thin parts and details to small movers far in the background. Its motions segment the moving parts of MOVi-F and iTACO scenes competitively, although DynMF2 is never trained to segment. What moves in a scene is not decided in advance: it emerges from the rigid motions themselves.","authors":["Agelos Kratimenos","Yile Hua","Kostas Daniilidis"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:14Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34931v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34931v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34931v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":58885,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：A single casual video leaves a moving scene under-determined: a point seen once from each place can slide toward or away from the camera and still match every frame. We constrain the reconstruction with the structure of the motion itself. Building on DynMF, w...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34929v1","title":"High-Fidelity Image Matting with Pixel-Space Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Diffusion models have shown strong potential for image matting by transferring generative priors learned from large-scale pretraining. However, existing approaches largely rely on latent diffusion models, whose compressed representations and VAE-based reconstruction introduce a mismatch with the high-frequency precision required for matting. We present PixelMatte, a framework that adapts pretrained pixel-space diffusion transformers for high-fidelity image matting. We show that strong image-conditioned matting capabilities can be acquired even from a pure text-to-image model without paired image-to-image pretraining. Our approach reuses the backbone's native patch embedding to jointly process the input image, trimap, and noisy alpha matte as spatially aligned tokens, enabling direct pixel-space prediction without additional condition encoders or adapters. With pixel-accurate synthetic supervision and real-image fine-tuning, PixelMatte predicts alpha mattes in a single forward pass. Two lightweight calibration modules, LayerShift and High-Pass Refiner, further improve regional opacity accuracy and boundary fidelity. Experiments across four benchmarks and two distinct pixel diffusion backbones demonstrate substantial improvements over existing methods, reducing SAD and gradient errors by 24-44% relative to RenderMatte on P3M-500-NP and AM-2K, while achieving competitive performance on AIM-500 and RenderMatte-2K. These results demonstrate the effectiveness of pixel-space generative priors for precision-sensitive image matting. Project page: https://white-yc.github.io/pixelmatte/.","authors":["Baihan Yang","Xin Lin","Yufan Wei","Baiang Li","Yuheng Liu","Juanxi Tian","Xiaohui Xie","Truong Nguyen"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:59:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:59:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34929v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34929v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34929v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":46059,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Diffusion models have shown strong potential for image matting by transferring generative priors learned from large-scale pretraining. However, existing approaches largely rely on latent diffusion models, whose compressed representations and VAE-based reconst...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34914v1","title":"Cairn: Object-Centric 3D Trajectory Priors for Steering Frozen VLAs","abstract":"Vision-language-action models (VLAs) are capable low-level controllers, but they tie where objects go to the layouts they were trained on: when objects are moved or obstacles block the way, they carry them to the wrong place or into collisions. Fine-tuning on new layouts needs robot data for each one and erodes skills the model already has. We introduce Cairn, which keeps the VLA frozen and steers it at test time along learned 3D trajectories of the object in its gripper. A small prior reads point clouds of the scene and the held object, along with its pose when grasped and the instruction, and samples SE(3) trajectories up to its release that lead it around obstacles and turn it to fit through gaps. The prior and the VLA form the two levels of a hierarchical planner in which feedback runs both ways: the VLA's intended motion chooses and reshapes the trajectories, and the chosen one guides the VLA's denoising. Its predicted actions, mapped to the held object's poses through the grasp, are pulled toward the next waypoint, while the VLA's weights stay untouched. Across 1,941 held-out LIBERO scenes with displaced objects and obstacles, Cairn raises the success rate of a frozen π0.5 from 24.5% to 95.1%, above that of a π0.5 fine-tuned on our training layouts, without hurting standard LIBERO. On the third-party LIBERO-PRO benchmark, it raises success from 27.3% to 38.0%, and from 54.4% to 81.1% on the episodes it steers. On a real Franka, with a prior trained purely in simulation, it guides the frozen DROID π0.5 around an obstacle and to a placement at the limit of the arm's reach, where it otherwise fails. All code, models and data will be made public.","authors":["Agelos Kratimenos","Kostas Daniilidis","Evangelos Chatzipantazis"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:57:29Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:57:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34914v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34914v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34914v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":68759,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision-language-action models (VLAs) are capable low-level controllers, but they tie where objects go to the layouts they were trained on: when objects are moved or obstacles block the way, they carry them to the wrong place or into collisions. Fine-tuning on...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34912v1","title":"LiveVAE: Causal Window Attention Makes Efficient Streaming Video VAE","abstract":"While video diffusion models have grown faster and increasingly streamable, the video VAE remains a primary latency bottleneck across interactive generation and editing pipelines. Existing VAEs and their acceleration methods rely mainly on heavy convolutions or full frame attention, and most focus only on decoding, leaving encoding costly for streaming conditional generation and editing. This raises a natural question: Do video VAE encoding and decoding actually require such global spatiotemporal modeling? By analyzing the effective receptive fields of pretrained video VAEs, we find that both encoding and decoding a patch rely almost entirely on its spatial and temporal neighbors. To exploit this strong locality, we introduce LiveVAE, a streaming encoder and decoder built on causal window attention, which cuts per frame compute while keeping the temporal KV cache bounded for streaming. Decoupled encoder/decoder distillation followed by joint finetuning preserves the pretrained latent space, making LiveVAE a drop-in replacement for the original VAE. On MiniMax-H3, LiveVAE achieves 5.2-6.2x faster encoding and 3.0-3.3x faster decoding than the official VAE with higher PSNR. Paired with our dedicated inference infrastructure, LiveVAE pushes the speedup to 13.4x with almost no quality loss. Code and models will be released.","authors":["Jiachen An","Sijie Zhao","Le Li","Hongyu Liu","Zhiyuan Li","Tianyu Yang","Xiaoyu Kong","Xunliang Cai","Yong Zhang","Chi-Man Pun","Xiaoming Wei","Xiaodong Cun"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:57:14Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:57:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34912v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34912v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34912v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":78001,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, diffusion 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：While video diffusion models have grown faster and increasingly streamable, the video VAE remains a primary latency bottleneck across interactive generation and editing pipelines. Existing VAEs and their acceleration methods rely mainly on heavy convolutions...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34907v1","title":"Agentic LongLive: Asynchronous Agentic Memory for Long Video Generation","abstract":"Real-time autoregressive video generators stream minutes of video and accept new prompts on the fly, but struggle to keep people, objects, and places consistent over a long interactive story. Agent systems provide such memory for offline multi-shot generation, yet they read the whole script in advance, plan each shot before generating it, and regenerate failed shots, which a live stream does not allow. We present Agentic LongLive, an asynchronous agentic memory for long video generation. A memory agent on separate GPUs reads only the prompts revealed so far, never the generated video, and keeps a text state and evolving images for every person, object, and scene. From this entity memory it composes a global image and a per-shot memory image, which an auxiliary GPU prefills into the generator's attention sink before the next shot starts, and writes entity cards for the prompts that name them, so the video never waits for memory and video errors are not written back. A small language model, trained with reinforcement learning and an image reward calibrated using downstream video outcomes, decides what to remember before the next prompt is known. On LongStory-Online, a new benchmark of 25 stories (857 shots) revealed one shot at a time, Agentic LongLive improves persistent-state accuracy of LongLive-2.0, its base generator, by 26.3 points, protagonist identity by 44.9, and revisited-place consistency by 40.4, at about 1% extra generation time on H100. The full system streams faster than real time on one 8xB200 node with the generator in BF16 (34.3 FPS), keeps image quality stable over 14-minute stories, and improves identity on two MiniMax-H3 generators, one of which, LongLive-H3, also runs in real time with it. To our knowledge, it is the first real-time video generator with text and image memory kept by an LLM agent.","authors":["Shaoteng Liu","Shuai Yang","Wei Huang","Luozhou Wang","Song Han","Yukang Chen"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:54:03Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:54:03Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34907v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34907v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34907v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":144653,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, agent 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Real-time autoregressive video generators stream minutes of video and accept new prompts on the fly, but struggle to keep people, objects, and places consistent over a long interactive story. Agent systems provide such memory for offline multi-shot generation...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34903v1","title":"ProbFaS: Probabilistic Video Frame Selection for MLLMs via Multi-Fidelity Calibration","abstract":"Processing long videos with multimodal large language models (MLLMs) is prohibitively expensive, making informative frame selection essential for accurate video understanding. The default uniform sampling often misses task-relevant keyframes, while recent training-free selectors instead pick frames by how well each one matches the question under an image-text alignment (ITA) score. However, we show that ITA signals correspond poorly with the MLLM's true needs: their rankings correlate weakly with downstream accuracy and are even negatively correlated on over 40% of questions, whereas the MLLM's internal yes/no judgment yields a much stronger signal. We present ProbFaS, a training- and label-free probabilistic pipeline that fuses both signals for key frame selection. ProbFaS treats dense ITA scores as cheap, biased measurements and sparse MLLM judgments as accurate, costly ones, unifying them via co-kriging, a multi-fidelity Gaussian process, to calibrate relevance across all frames. A temporal determinantal point process then selects frames capturing relevance peaks while preserving temporal coverage. Across Video-MME, MLVU and LongVideoBench, ProbFaS is the most accurate among fourteen state-of-the-art training-free selectors and uniform sampling in 13 of 15 settings (8-128 frames), outperforming the strongest baseline by up to 3.1 percentage points. On MLVU and LongVideoBench, 32 ProbFaS frames match or surpass the top baseline at 128 frames and uniform sampling with up to 768 frames.","authors":["Tianru Zhang","Mayank Nautiyal","Hugo Eidmann","Prashant Singh","Ekta Vats"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:53:10Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:53:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34903v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34903v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34903v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":134771,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Processing long videos with multimodal large language models (MLLMs) is prohibitively expensive, making informative frame selection essential for accurate video understanding. The default uniform sampling often misses task-relevant keyframes, while recent tra...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34899v1","title":"Enhancing Maritime Domain Awareness using Latent Space Representations","abstract":"This work presents a novel application of Earth Observation (EO) embeddings for Maritime Domain Awareness (MDA) from the perspective of the geospatial foundation model TerraMind. By operating directly on compressed latent-space representations rather than on the original Sentinel-1 imagery, this approach directly addresses the data gravity bottleneck that increasingly constrains operational EO processing, in line with the objectives of the Embed2Scale project. In particular, linear and non-linear probing of compressed embeddings, latent space representations of synthetic aperture radar (SAR) imagery, is used to detect, localise, and characterise vessels from Sentinel-1 GRD images. Vessel detection achieves a ROC-AUC of 0.95, while vessel localisation has a median error of around 300 m for vessels longer than 100 m, and vessel AIS type-code classification achieves 62% accuracy for the majority classes. The latter, however, is highly correlated with vessel length. These results reveal both the potential and current limitations of Geospatial Foundation Models (GeoFMs) when applied to fine-grained MDA understanding in real-world scenarios. Additionally, a new maritime remote sensing dataset is released, pairing about 2,000 Sentinel-1 images with temporally aligned AIS signals from commercial vessels. Through manual inspection, each candidate sample was verified for bounding-box quality and for consistency between the AIS-reported vessel length and the observed vessel extent, cross-checking vessel identity and location against publicly available vessel records and, where available, photographs. The dataset was made ready for use with the NeuCo-Bench neural compression benchmark framework, publicly available on HuggingFace through https://huggingface.co/datasets/embed2scale/S1-single-ship-downstream .","authors":["Rikard Vinge","Miguel A. Belenguer-Plomer","Isabelle Wittmann","Octavian Dumitru","Michele Lazzarini","Sergio Albani","Conrad M Albrecht"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:51:36Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:51:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34899v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34899v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34899v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":47975,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：This work presents a novel application of Earth Observation (EO) embeddings for Maritime Domain Awareness (MDA) from the perspective of the geospatial foundation model TerraMind. By operating directly on compressed latent-space representations rather than on...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34894v1","title":"What a Drop Reveals: Sim-to-Real Material Estimation for Deformable Objects from Video","abstract":"How an object deforms when it hits the ground reveals what it is made of. From a single real video of a drop, we estimate a deformable object's density-normalized stiffness $E/ρ$, and its yield stress $σ_Y/ρ$ when the impact causes plastic deformation, with a feed-forward predictor trained only in simulation. Real videos carry no material labels, so such predictors are developed and validated on synthetic data alone. We show that synthetic validation ranks their geometric inputs in the wrong order. Material acts through 3D deformation, and tracklets lifted to 3D with estimated depth are indeed the most accurate input in simulation. However, on real drops they are the least accurate, and better depth does not repair them: the failure persists with simulator depth in training, LiDAR depth at test time, and exact depth on rendered replays of the real drops. Short 2D optical-flow tracklets do the opposite: they improve nothing in simulation, yet they transfer best. Material is therefore better read from what perception measures consistently in both domains than from what is most informative in simulation. Our predictor, PhysReader, reads the video and 2D tracklets and learns the lift to 3D in simulation by matching a teacher that sees exact 3D deformation. To measure transfer, we introduce PhysReader-Real: 16 everyday objects dropped five times each, with reference materials fit jointly to each object's drops and checked against tensile tests. On PhysReader-Real, PhysReader is closer to the reference materials than every other input and baseline. The one exception is a per-scene optimizer started from PhysReader's prediction, which ends slightly closer but takes 49 minutes per video instead of 12 seconds.","authors":["Ziyue Peng","Yanxi Hu","Junyoung Kim","Andrew Chen","Jin Yang","Chandrajit L. Bajaj","Etienne Vouga","William T. Freeman","Qixing Huang","Brandon Y. Feng"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:47:56Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:47:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34894v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34894v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34894v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":320884,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video, 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：How an object deforms when it hits the ground reveals what it is made of. From a single real video of a drop, we estimate a deformable object's density-normalized stiffness $E/ρ$, and its yield stress $σ_Y/ρ$ when the impact causes plastic deformation, with a...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34884v1","title":"FineDex: Bridging Fine-Grained Human-Object Interaction Generation to Dexterous Humanoid Manipulation","abstract":"Dexterity is, at its core, finger-level control, yet existing dexterous manipulation and human-object interaction (HOI) generation methods rarely let one specify how an interaction is performed: five-fingered hands are often reduced to grippers, or finger usage is copied from individual human demonstrations. We present FineDex, a framework that makes finger-level intent both expressible and executable. FineDex learns coordinated human body, hand, and object motions that follow fine-grained instructions on hand selection, individual fingers, and contacts with articulated objects. To bridge generation and execution, FineDex leverages generated interactions as actionable priors for an agent that discovers physically feasible contact strategies through practice in a physics-based digital twin. By adapting at test time without per-instance retraining, FineDex generalizes across fine-grained instructions and object configurations. Experiments demonstrate state-of-the-art HOI generation quality and fine-grained controllability, substantial improvements in dexterous manipulation success, and successful deployment on a real dexterous humanoid robot.","authors":["Yujia Zeng","Yuxin Chen","Yuzhen Song","Runhan Huang","Chensheng Peng","Yiyang Shao","Koushil Sreenath","Masayoshi Tomizuka","Yilun Du"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:40:47Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:40:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34884v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34884v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34884v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","instance"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":44454,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 agent, instance 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Dexterity is, at its core, finger-level control, yet existing dexterous manipulation and human-object interaction (HOI) generation methods rarely let one specify how an interaction is performed: five-fingered hands are often reduced to grippers, or finger usa...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34881v1","title":"Stranger Things: When Objects Appear Without Their Typical Neighbors","abstract":"Visual context is a powerful cue for object recognition: scenes inform what, where, and at what scale objects are likely to appear. Yet over-reliance on context becomes a failure mode when models predict objects based on their typical neighbors rather than the objects' own visual features, which we call typicality bias. While extensively studied in the pre-Transformer era, it has often been assumed that architectural and scale improvements would naturally solve it, and a systematic diagnostic across the modern vision stack remains absent. We provide such a diagnostic framework. We measure typicality bias via normalized pointwise mutual information (NPMI) over object pairs, and introduce STRANGE-Bench (Synthetic Typicality-RANked Grounded Evaluation Benchmark): a photorealistic, layout-controlled benchmark spanning 17 sub-tiers from typical to never-co-occurring object pairs. Across five model families (specialist detectors, open-vocabulary detectors, self-supervised, vision-language, and multimodal large language models) and three recognition tasks (supervised detection, open-vocabulary detection, and classification), the bias is prevalent: gaps of 5-11 p.p. persist when objects appear outside their typical company, and do not consistently shrink with scale. To mitigate it, we turn to data augmentation: existing augmentations are designed for visual diversity, not for breaking co-occurrence shortcuts, and sample from the very distribution that produces typicality bias. We propose Anchor-Atypicality Sampling (AAS), a simple sampling rule for paste-based augmentations that uses the NPMI signal to tilt the training-time co-occurrence distribution toward atypical partners. AAS reduces the typicality gap by up to 1.6 p.p. on natural-image COCO splits and 23.0 p.p. on synthetic STRANGE-Bench across three model families, without sacrificing overall performance.","authors":["Siddhartha Gairola","Jiahao Xie","Anna Kukleva","Francesco Locatello","Bernt Schiele"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:39:28Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:39:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34881v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34881v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34881v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":181196,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 multimodal 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Visual context is a powerful cue for object recognition: scenes inform what, where, and at what scale objects are likely to appear. Yet over-reliance on context becomes a failure mode when models predict objects based on their typical neighbors rather than th...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34877v1","title":"Towards Closing the Performance Gap in Cross-Modal Place Recognition","abstract":"Cross-modal place recognition (CMPR) matches natural language descriptions against databases of geo-tagged reference images, enabling localization without visual queries. Despite its potential for language-guided navigation, existing vision-language models lag far behind visual place recognition (VPR) baselines, even after task adaptation. Unlike general-purpose text-to-image retrieval, place recognition relies on subtle local cues to distinguish visually similar locations. We therefore hypothesize that a primary bottleneck in CMPR is capturing fine-grained correspondences between image regions and text details, which requires higher visual token granularity, strong text representation capacity, and local cross-modal matching. To address this challenge, we propose CrossPlace, a two-stage CMPR framework. Stage 1 performs global retrieval by pairing a jointly aligned DINOv2-B/14 visual backbone with a progressively fine-tuned BGE-Large text encoder, while Stage 2 refines top candidates using a lightweight token-level cross-attention reranker. Through systematic analysis, we isolate the drivers of retrieval accuracy and show that visual token density provides the largest performance improvement, while text encoder capacity and task fine-tuning offer complementary gains. To evaluate robustness under realistic conditions, we also extend CMPR evaluation to city-scale and severe weather and illumination settings. Across all existing and new benchmarks, CrossPlace achieves double-digit Recall@1 improvements over prior cross-modal methods, closes the gap to unimodal text retrieval, and narrows it to unimodal VPR. Code and benchmarks are available at https://github.com/oferidan1/CrossPlace","authors":["Ofer Idan","Dan Badur","Yoli Shavit"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:38:09Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:38:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34877v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34877v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34877v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":73268,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 retrieval, representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Cross-modal place recognition (CMPR) matches natural language descriptions against databases of geo-tagged reference images, enabling localization without visual queries. Despite its potential for language-guided navigation, existing vision-language models la...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34863v1","title":"Focus Only on What's Important: Training-Free Focus Maps for Frozen Vision Transformers in Long-Video Object Segmentation","abstract":"Vision transformers are strong but costly image encoders for video object segmentation. Training-free token merging cuts compute uniformly across the frame, including on tracked objects. We give a frozen DINOv3 ViT inside a trained Cutie head a training-free focus map: full resolution where the segmenter expects its objects and coarser merging elsewhere. Protecting only the previous frame's mask works on short clips but fails on long videos, where fast motion pushes targets outside the margin and into background merging, locking in loss. Our policy instead takes the union of velocity-shifted and block-matched masks, runs a camera-compensated surprise check, delegates uncertain regions to the compared merger, and falls back to it when all objects are lost. On long videos (LVOS V2 valid, pre-specified protocol, three ViT-S seeds), it beats ALGM 0.98 by +2.76 J&F (95% object CI [+1.22,+4.44]; video-cluster [+0.99,+4.52]) at 16% fewer counted encoder FLOPs (0.385 vs. 0.459), sitting 1.0 J&F point below the dense-encoder baseline; at ViT-B the gain over ALGM 0.95 is +3.89 [+2.03,+5.87]. On DAVIS 2017 val it stays within -0.19 J&F of the dense encoder at ViT-S (-0.12 at ViT-B). Controls qualify the result: protecting only the previous mask under ALGM matches or exceeds accuracy at higher FLOPs (0.486), so expectation and surprise mainly reduce counted encoder compute. Practically, lower counted encoder compute did not reduce startup-inclusive mean Cutie step time on a 12-video LVOS sample (3%-8% lower in steady state; an equally engineered ALGM is faster at ViT-S and ViT-B). Section 5.2 separately examines a Cutie permanent-memory schedule and its latency and memory trade-offs. Code: https://github.com/boletoms/focused-cv","authors":["Harsha Karipineni","Rajesh Reddy Chintha"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:30:28Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:30:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34863v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34863v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34863v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":93132,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 video 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Vision transformers are strong but costly image encoders for video object segmentation. Training-free token merging cuts compute uniformly across the frame, including on tracked objects. We give a frozen DINOv3 ViT inside a trained Cutie head a training-free...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34862v1","title":"BOP-Refer: Benchmark for Monocular 3D and 2D Object Grounding","abstract":"We introduce BOP-Refer, the first benchmark for monocular 3D and 2D grounding of everyday and industrial objects sourced from 9 diverse BOP datasets. We pair images from these datasets with VLM-generated and human-verified referring expressions, and derive accurate ground-truth 3D and 2D bounding boxes of the referred objects from their 3D models and 6DoF pose annotations. Our systematic evaluation of 17 frontier VLMs across the major proprietary and open-weight families (Gemini, GPT, Claude, Qwen, Gemma) shows that the best model, Gemini 4 Carbon, reaches 72.6% AP_IoU2D in 2D but only 12.4% AP_IoU3D in 3D, despite the more lenient IoU thresholds in 3D. The predicted 3D boxes are usually centered on the target objects in the image but often off in depth by more than the object's size, suggesting that metric scale estimation, which needs reliable object size priors, is currently by far the main source of error.","authors":["Tomas Hodan","Vineet Bhat","Martin Sundermeyer","Jonathan Tremblay","Yash Patel","Médéric Fourmy","Stephen Tyree","Junwen Huang","Varun Burde","Martin Cífka","Eric Brachmann","Vincent Lepetit","Stan Birchfield","Jiri Matas","Carsten Rother"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:29:36Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:29:36Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34862v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34862v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34862v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":168192,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 3d 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：We introduce BOP-Refer, the first benchmark for monocular 3D and 2D grounding of everyday and industrial objects sourced from 9 diverse BOP datasets. We pair images from these datasets with VLM-generated and human-verified referring expressions, and derive ac...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:50+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34860v1","title":"DocDUR-Net: Degradation-Aware Unified Document Image Restoration","abstract":"Captured document images often suffer from diverse degradations caused by uncontrolled imaging conditions, which reduce readability and obscure fine text structures. Single-degradation restoration methods are usually trained and deployed for individual degradation types, and therefore require model selection before restoration. Unified restoration models alleviate the need to maintain multiple restoration networks by sharing one network across tasks, but representative unified document image restoration methods still depend on external task selection. This dependency limits automatic document image restoration. We propose DocDUR-Net, a degradation-aware unified restoration network that derives restoration guidance directly from the input image. It introduces a Degradation Perception Module (DPM) to extract degradation representations and predict degradation weights, and Degradation Prior-guided Adaptive Feature Fusion (DP-AFF) to combine learnable degradation priors with degradation features extracted from the input. The resulting guidance is injected into a shared restoration backbone, enabling adaptive restoration without external task selection. DocDUR-Net also supports a manual mode with explicit task selection. Experiments on document image restoration benchmarks show that DocDUR-Net achieves the best average PSNR of 29.405 dB and SSIM of 0.960 among unified methods while remaining competitive with single-degradation methods.","authors":["Haowei Xu","Peirong Zhang","Xuhan Zheng","Lianwen Jin"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:29:14Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:29:14Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34860v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34860v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34860v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"html","source_text_chars":30843,"summary":{"model":"fallback-no-api","core_contribution":"该论文与 representation 相关；摘要显示其主要贡献为：Captured document images often suffer from diverse degradations caused by uncontrolled imaging conditions, which reduce readability and obscure fine text structures. Single-degradation restoration methods are usually trained and deployed for individual degrad...","method":"未配置 OPENAI_API_KEY，方法细节未调用模型总结。","source_used":"html","created_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:51+00:00","status":"fallback"},"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34854v1","title":"TIDE-Flow: Fine-Grained Temporal Evidence and Structured Displacement Matching for Spike Optical Flow","abstract":"Spike cameras capture high-speed motion through an integrate-and-fire mechanism, providing fine-grained temporal observations without exposure blur. However, their binary streams encode accumulated illumination rather than instantaneous intensity, making reliable motion estimation challenging. Existing spike-based methods exploit temporal aggregation to construct motion representations, but this process may weaken fine-grained firing patterns. Meanwhile, correlation-based estimators often rely on learned matching features without explicitly characterizing the spatial distribution and ambiguity of candidate displacements. To address these challenges, we propose TIDE-Flow, a spike-based optical flow estimation framework that jointly exploits Temporal Integration Evidence (TIE) and Displacement Evidence Encoding (DEE). TIE combines raw spike patterns, multi-scale firing statistics, temporal moments, and boundary-aware interval cues, preserving complementary temporal information before spatial feature compression. DEE further integrates structured candidate-grid features with explicit displacement statistics to characterize matching geometry and competing correspondences. Both components are incorporated into a difference-correlation recurrent refinement framework, enabling effective temporal motion modeling and correspondence estimation. Extensive experiments demonstrate the effectiveness of TIDE-Flow, with controlled ablations validating the contributions of temporal and displacement evidence modeling.","authors":["Hanwen Liang","Wenxuan Liu","Wenxin Huang","Jingling Yuan","Zhaofei Yu","Xian Zhong","Tiejun Huang"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:26:42Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:26:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34854v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34854v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34854v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34849v1","title":"Real2SimLab: A Unified Platform for Real-to-Simulation Transfer","abstract":"Real-to-simulation transfer, which builds simulations from real-world observations, has long been studied in research and industry. Recently, coding agents have become capable of generating simulation programs from video recordings, and several benchmarks have been proposed to evaluate them. However, each individual benchmark defines its own inputs, deliverables, and scoring pipeline. This leads to $N \\times M$ integrations when evaluating $N$ methods on $M$ benchmarks. To this end, we present Real2SimLab, a unified platform for real-to-simulation transfer. It evaluates a method by running its written program and scoring the output simulation with the original scorer of each benchmark. Moreover, Real2SimLab provides a pipeline to create training data and reward annotations for real-to-simulation methods. Our pipeline carefully avoids data contamination by filtering out the video recordings that exist in benchmarks. For each video recording, a program is first produced, and then our pipeline computes its reward with the same code that Real2SimLab uses for evaluation. So far, it supports 47 coding agents and 5 non-agent methods on 6 benchmarks. Both the platform and Awesome-Real2Sim, our research map of nearly 300 real-to-simulation papers, are open source, and we invite the Real2SimHub community to build them together.","authors":["Yongchang Zhang","Jie Huang","Yunhe Ni","Ningyu Zhou","Guangyu Zhao","Ruixun Liu","Rui Huang","Yidi Wu","TianZe Zhang","Zixiang Jiang","Xiang Li","Kevin Shen","Yiwu Zhong"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:23:41Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:23:41Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34849v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34849v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34849v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34839v1","title":"Diffusion Steering via Adaptive Reward Estimation and Resampling","abstract":"Text-to-image diffusion models can generate images that satisfy complex prompts, yet often produce images that omit requested objects, attributes, or relationships. We ask if models can more reliably generate text-aligned images by redistributing probability toward their more successful outcomes. We propose TrailMix, a training-free method that uses the base model's distribution of possible outcomes to adaptively guide generation toward better text alignment. During denoising, TrailMix generates inexpensive lookahead images from parallel trajectories (trails) and scores their alignment with the prompt using an external judge. The resulting distribution of reward scores allows us to estimate how strongly to steer generation toward a desired alignment level. We use these estimates to reweight and resample trajectories, favoring more promising outcomes. Across three text-to-image benchmarks, TrailMix outperforms existing baselines, with particularly strong gains on rare multi-object compositions. We further extend TrailMix to targeted image editing and introduce SEAM-Bench (Semantic Editing and Attribute Maintenance), a systematic benchmark evaluating whether semantic edits preserve other image content. Experiments on SEAM-Bench and existing benchmark PIE-Bench demonstrate the effectiveness of our approach for controlled image editing. Our results show how a model's own possible outcomes can inform reliable and controllable generation.","authors":["Abinitha Gourabathina","Sana Tonekaboni","Marzyeh Ghassemi","Akbar Rafiey"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:18:51Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:18:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34839v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34839v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34839v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34813v1","title":"Solve Vision with Code: Coding Agents Are Stronger Visual Reasoners Than Video Models","abstract":"Video generation models are increasingly evaluated as visual reasoners: given a first frame and a natural-language prompt, can the model produce the video in which the specified task is carried out? The paradigm is considered advantageous because the reasoning is native to the visual medium, unfolding directly in the frames rather than through language or code. Whether this native route is in fact the more reliable path to visual reasoning has not been tested against a symbolic alternative under the same conditions. We therefore pose the same tasks, with identical inputs, output specification and evaluator, to both media: rather than denoising pixels, a coding agent writes a program that renders the answer video, which an unmodified rule-based evaluator scores. On VBVR-Pro-Bench (Xu et al., 2026), a 500-instance suite spanning five cognitive categories with symbolic ground truth, the agent receives the same first frame and prompt as the video model, with no access to ground truth, metadata or generation models, and one attempt per instance. Three closed-model agents (Codex with gpt-6-astra, Claude Code with Fable 5.1 and Gemini CLI with Gemini 3.1 Pro) score 0.923, 0.894 and 0.893, each more than 0.22 above the strongest video model. Among 23 runnable open-weight models, the best, GLM-5, reaches 0.572, above all video models except the RL-trained one. Together, we show that for tasks whose solutions can be computed from a first frame and a prompt, program synthesis is currently a substantially more reliable route to visual reasoning than video generation. We further distinguish depictive programs, which navigate the image to recover a transferable scheme, from propositional programs, which operate on hard-coded values, revealing how visual reasoning unfolds in agent-written programs and what it implies for its nature of reasoning in general.","authors":["Dezhi Luo","Fengyuan Yu","Haotian Zhang","Zehong Zhao","Ran Ji","Siyuan An","Huayi Qin","Qingying Gao","Zhongang Cai","Lei Yang","Ziqi Huang","Ruisi Wang","Maijunxian Wang","Hokin Deng"],"published":"2026-11-25T18:01:22Z","updated":"2026-11-25T18:01:22Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34813v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34813v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34813v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent","instance"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34811v1","title":"HOG-WAM: Improving Visual Robustness through Future Gradient Prediction","abstract":"World-action models jointly predict future observations and actions, yet visual corruption can disrupt the resulting policies. We introduce HOG-WAM, which adds future Histograms of Oriented Gradients (HOG) as structural supervision derived directly from demonstration images. An auxiliary HOG diffusion transformer (DiT) module reads RGB features through asymmetric attention, allowing its objective to shape the visual representation used for control. The module is used only during training and removed at inference, preserving FastWAM's RGB-to-action architecture. We evaluate four controlled observation corruptions through a visual-corruption extension of RoboTwin, termed RoboTwin-Plus; HOG-WAM achieves the highest success among the compared policies under all four. Mixed-noise success increases from 78.86% to 82.86% relative to FastWAM, with comparable performance on clean scenes. On LIBERO Plus, overall success increases from 74.49% to 75.26%, with the largest gain under sensor noise, from 74.14% to 78.89%. Across four Franka tasks, average success increases from 40% to 59% under clean observations and from 17% to 24% under noisy observations. A representation ablation favors combining RGB observations with auxiliary HOG prediction over replacing RGB with HOG. These findings support future-gradient prediction as complementary supervision for visually robust control, with larger absolute gains on physical tasks than in simulation. Code is available at https://github.com/iPPLab/HOG-WAM.","authors":["Boxuan Dong","Yupeng Qian","Ying Wu","Heshuai Li","Junhao Tan","Zhenxiong Tan","Weijia Wu","Weihao Yu"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:59:24Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:59:24Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34811v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34811v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34811v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34810v1","title":"WorldExpand: Building Coherent 3D Worlds via Sparse Joint Panoramic Expansion","abstract":"Constructing complete 3D worlds from a single panorama is challenging because large portions of the scene remain unobserved. Existing panoramic-video methods expand spatial coverage through dense sequential generation, but neighboring frames contain substantial redundancy, long trajectories accumulate errors, and independently generated directions may be inconsistent. We present WorldExpand, which formulates single-panorama world expansion as the generation of sparse, complementary, and high-resolution panoramic observations. This representation concentrates the generation budget on informative scene regions and preserves the fine-grained details required for 3D construction. WorldExpand addresses two key challenges: where to observe and how to synthesize. Our Coverage-Guided View Selection identifies informative target locations using scene-adaptive quadrants and warp-based coverage scores, while our Joint Panoramic Diffusion uses Gaussian-splatting-based geometric warps to jointly synthesize four pose-grounded and cross-view-consistent panoramas. A diagnostic-guided global--local attention schedule retains global attention only in sensitive layers, reducing the training and inference costs of high-resolution joint generation. The resulting posed panoramas can be directly fed into an existing reconstruction pipeline to build an explicit 3D representation. Experiments show that WorldExpand recovers content behind occluders and around distant corners, improves both novel-view synthesis and downstream 3D reconstruction metrics across various datasets, and generalizes robustly to diverse in-the-wild scenes. WorldExpand runs significantly faster than the state-of-the-art panoramic-video method while producing higher-resolution observations with stronger cross-view consistency.","authors":["Yuyang Yin","Chenjie Cao","Xuhui Zuo","Yuning Gong","Pengfei Wang","Junta Wu","Zhenwei Wang","Yao Zhao","Tengfei Wang","Yunchao Wei","Chunchao Guo"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:58:06Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:58:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34810v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34810v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34810v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34801v1","title":"Guidance as Variance Matching","abstract":"Guidance repels the prediction of a diffusion model away from a weaker reference prediction. It substantially improves sample quality, but its mechanism remains poorly understood. We first find that, at the scales used in practice, guidance does not improve the prediction of the posterior mean (the model's training target); it makes the prediction worse, while samples improve. Second, posterior-mean predictions have less variance than the data, and we find that guidance restores it. We introduce variance-matched guidance: a closed-form rule that picks the guidance scale by matching the variance of the guided predictions to that of the data. Without tuning, it recovers the hand-picked scales of eleven widely used models across image, video, audio, motion, and world generation; to our knowledge, no previous explanation of guidance predicts them. Applied per noise level, it yields a schedule without tuned hyperparameters that users prefer to hand-crafted ones on SD 3.5 Medium. Third, we offer an interpretation of the mechanism behind guidance: each model makes a random error in predicting the posterior mean, and guidance rescales the difference of these errors into a sample with the posterior's covariance, which improves sampling. This view also suggests two new hypotheses that our experiments support: guidance can work with negative scales, and guiding a model with its own checkpoint needs a larger scale than guiding it with an independently trained model.","authors":["Ali Backour","Artem Lukoianov","Frank Permenter","Tommi Jaakkola","Justin Solomon","Vincent Sitzmann"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:53:37Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:53:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34801v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34801v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34801v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34795v1","title":"CIRKDV2: Cross-Image Relational Knowledge Distillation with Contextual Modeling for Efficient Semantic Segmentation","abstract":"Existing Knowledge Distillation (KD) methods for semantic segmentation often guide the student to mimic the teacher's structured information generated from an individual image. However, they ignore the global semantic relations across various images that are valuable for KD. This paper proposes Cross-Image Relational KD V2 (CIRKDV2), which focuses on transferring structured pixel-to-pixel, pixel-to-region, and channel-to-channel relations modeled from global images in the dataset. A large teacher network often constructs a well-structured feature space in terms of global contextual dependencies. CIRKDV2 makes the student mimic the better structured semantic relations of the teacher, thus improving the feature representational capability. Beyond relational distillation, we further propose contextual feature distillation by using context aggregation to augment student pixel embeddings to match the semantic level with the teacher. This avoids the semantic gap problem between the teacher and student occurred in traditional feature distillation studies. Experimental results on Cityscapes, ADE20K, Pascal VOC, and COCO-Stuff-164K demonstrate that CIRKDV2 outperforms the state-of-the-art semantic segmentation distillation methods by large margins across various network architectures. Our code and pre-trained models are publicly available at https://github.com/winycg/CIRKD.","authors":["Chuanguang Yang","Yu Wang","Chengqing Yu","Xinqiang Yu","Weilun Feng","Yuqi Li","Zhulin An","Libo Huang","Boyu Diao","Fei Wang","Fuzhen Zhuang","Yongjun Xu","Yingli Tian","Tingwen Huang","Yongduan Song"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:49:00Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:49:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34795v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34795v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34795v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34783v1","title":"SceneDesigner3D: Agentic Evolutionary Search for Principled Rearrangement of 3D Indoor Scenes","abstract":"Scene rearrangement is an essential task for 3D content creation applications, requiring generation of diverse, aesthetic and functional scene rearrangements. We present SceneDesigner3D, an agentic evolutionary search method for 3D indoor scene rearrangement that systematically explores the design space of 3D scenes and generates multiple solutions according to common interior design principles. Given an input scene and a text prompt, SceneDesigner3D represents the rearrangement as an editable program and evolves a population of candidate layouts. Starting from distinct design ideas, a VLM agent mutates the programs by refining promising candidates, exploring new rearrangement strategies or redesigning from scratch, while verifying the layouts for physical validity. Proposals are scored by a VLM judge for adherence to the input prompt and satisfaction of design principles such as balance, zoning and emphasis, as well as numerically computed principles such as circulation and clutter. An archive of top-performing, structurally varied candidates further increases diversity of rearrangements. Experiments on the SAGE-10k and SceneSmith datasets show that SceneDesigner3D improves prompt adherence and computed design principle scores over baselines by up to 5.1% and 15.2%, respectively. Code will be made publicly available.","authors":["Chandan Yeshwanth","Angela Dai"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:41:34Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:41:34Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34783v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34783v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34783v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34782v1","title":"Generation as Reverse Encoding","abstract":"Pretrained visual representations accelerate generative learning by abstracting away appearance and spatial detail. Yet the same abstraction makes the return to pixels far harder, shifting much of the burden onto a larger decoder. We ask why the representation-to-pixel path should be learned independently when the encoder already provides a learned pixel-to-semantics path (the semantic encoding path). We introduce RevFlow, based on the principle of generation as reverse encoding. RevFlow uses intermediate representations of a frozen encoder as successive generative targets, first generating deep semantic representations, then shallow representations that retain appearance and spatial detail, before decoding to pixels. To learn these transitions without training a separate generator for each stage, we develop HyC-DiT, a unified generative model with a standard DiT backbone shared across stages and stage-specific condition injection. We further show that native matching in semantic representation space enables few-step generation. RevFlow accelerates both learning and sampling. On ImageNet $256 \\times 256$, RevFlow reaches 1.96 FID at 690M and 1.74 FID at 1.55B after only 5 training epochs. It also achieves 30.25 dB reconstruction PSNR with an 89M decoder. After a short native-matching adaptation phase, RevFlow-XL achieves 2.85 FID using a single sampling step in each stage. Our code repository is at https://github.com/ChengShiest/RevFlow","authors":["Cheng Shi","Sibei Yang","Hanzhong Guo","Yipeng Qin","Pietro Lio","Yizhou Yu"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:40:56Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:40:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34782v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34782v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34782v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34750v1","title":"SOLVE: Sparse, Ordered Keyposes Make LLM Agents Versatile and Efficient Robot Manipulator","abstract":"LLM agents have made remarkable progress in robot manipulation, yet they suffer from expensive inference, dense step-by-step control, and tool bottlenecks. We introduce SOLVE, a training-free, versatile and efficient agent policy: a multimodal LLM agent writes a sparse, ordered sequence of 6-DoF end-effector keyposes per call, observes the result, and writes the next, while a motion planner interpolates between them. The agent decides the number and spacing of keyposes for both fast long-range motion and fine-grained manipulation. This granularity matches the sparsity of teleoperated manipulation: most demonstrations in DROID can be compressed into a few keyposes instead of dense actions. SOLVE grounds keyposes with sparse depth queries answered by noisy sources, such as multi-view triangulation, stereo, or monocular depth, and copes with that noise itself. It also improves from execution feedback, reflecting on its own rollouts and distilling a few rules per task. SOLVE reaches state-of-the-art performance of 74.5% on RoboDojo and 84.5% on RoboLab, with only $1.7 per episode on average, 12x less than previous dense-control agent policies. On a real robot, the same agent achieves an 88% success rate (75 of 85 episodes) over 16 challenging zero-shot tasks. Our project page is at https://serenos.github.io/solve-website.","authors":["Xiang Li","Hongyu Zhou","Ya-Li Li","Tao Li","Rui Zhang","Junjie Lai","Weihua Zhang","Shengjin Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:25:17Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:25:17Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34750v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34750v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34750v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34730v1","title":"WorldInteract: Controllable Streaming Video Generation for Multi-Material Interactions","abstract":"Video world models aim to create interactive physical worlds that capture how diverse materials move and interact. Yet material-dependent dynamics remain underexplored in controllable video generation, limiting reliable control in scenes with different materials. We present WorldInteract, a streaming video generation framework that decomposes cross-material interaction into material-dependent behavior generation and contact response computation. External pushes and contact impulses are expressed through a shared velocity-increment interface, allowing material behaviors learned from interaction-free videos to be reused in interacting scenes. A physics controller predicts contacts and computes velocity increments from estimated masses and restitution coefficients, while a causal generator executes these increments using learned material behaviors. This separation enables cross-material interaction without supervised contact videos spanning diverse material pairings and contact configurations. We train the generator on 30k simulated interaction-free videos spanning 22 material classes and introduce Material-Aware RL, using verifiable physical rewards to refine material behavior and control execution. WorldInteract substantially outperforms state-of-the-art methods on Physics-IQ and delivers more reliable push control on real-world images.","authors":["Ahmed Hatem","Caoliwen Wang","Robert Hoang","Siyuan Chen","Spencer Szabados","Zike Wu","Xiyang Tan","Shenyifan Lu","Rafael Bischof","Bernd Bickel","Renjie Liao","Peter Yichen Chen"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:14:27Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:14:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34730v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34730v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34730v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34724v1","title":"LiDAR Intensity Reconstruction with Limited Real Data: An Architecture Benchmark under Fixed-Epoch Training","abstract":"Physics-derived simulation can supplement scarce real-world LiDAR intensity measurements, but its effectiveness depends on the learning architecture and the amount of real training data available. We benchmark nine neural architectures for LiDAR intensity reconstruction from depth, semantic labels, incidence angle, and material reflectance, using real SemanticKITTI and synthetic VoxelScape range-image data. All models follow a fixed 20-epoch protocol; this controls the number of training passes, not the computational cost across architectures. We report mean absolute error (MAE), mean squared error (MSE), peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR), structural similarity (SSIM), inference timing, and spectral-energy agreement. We also evaluate four selected architectures with fixed-size training mixtures containing 0\\%, 1\\%, 5\\%, 10\\%, 20\\%, 50\\%, or 100\\% real frames. With real-only training, UNet-V2 achieves the best reported reconstruction metrics (MAE 0.234, MSE 0.140, PSNR 8.64 dB, and SSIM 0.647), followed by a denoising diffusion model (DDPM). In several mixtures containing 1--10\\% real data, DDPM achieves lower error and higher image-quality metrics than the evaluated deterministic alternatives, whereas UNet-V2 leads with 100\\% real data. Spectral analysis additionally reveals architecture-dependent discrepancies across frequency bands. These findings characterize reconstruction trade-offs under the stated data mixtures and fixed-epoch protocol, without establishing compute-matched superiority or isolating the diffusion objective from backbone differences.","authors":["Christopher M. Coovrey","Aniruddha Bora"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:10:43Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:10:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34724v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34724v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34724v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34719v1","title":"Reading, Not Adapting: Unlocking Frozen Visual Knowledge for AI-Generated Image Detection","abstract":"Generalization to unseen image generators has driven efforts to adapt pretrained visual representations for detection. Yet recent findings show that vision foundation models already encode transferable detection knowledge, while further adaptation can impair generalization. This motivates a shift from backbone adaptation to knowledge readout. Conventional linear probing offers a direct way to read this knowledge but is limited to final-layer RGB features. Multi-cue detectors broaden the evidence base, but their designs introduce other tradeoffs: adaptation may compromise pretrained transferability, heterogeneous encoders complicate feature integration, and late fusion precludes intermediate cross-view interaction. To overcome these limitations, we propose ReMoT, a Mixture-of-Transformers-inspired framework that reads complementary knowledge from spatially aligned RGB and wavelet views within a fully frozen visual foundation. ReMoT employs staged joint attention to enable controlled local and global cross-view interaction, while learnable fusion queries progressively read complementary evidence across Transformer depth without writeback. ReMoT achieves 91.2% average accuracy across eight benchmark groups with 3,074 trainable parameters, attaining the highest overall average among the evaluated methods. These results support structured knowledge readout as a promising alternative to backbone adaptation. Code is available at https://github.com/yunncheng/ReMoT.","authors":["Yuncheng Guo","Ying Dong","Xiaodong Gu"],"published":"2026-11-25T17:09:32Z","updated":"2026-11-25T17:09:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34719v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34719v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34719v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34700v1","title":"Seeing the Right Context: Grounded On-Policy Distillation for Remote Sensing","abstract":"Remote sensing scene understanding requires multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to interpret queried targets in relation to nearby objects and surrounding structures, which serve as spatial references or define the scope of an answer. However, standard answers and bounding boxes often leave these contextual relationships implicit. Thus, we construct GeoContext-5K to make them explicit in 5,394 training pairs for visual question answering (VQA), with spatial cues, visual facts, and supporting regions. GeOPD uses these annotations for on-policy distillation, guiding a frozen teacher with region marks on the full image and a visual fact while preserving surrounding spatial references. It prioritizes teacher corrections that agree with the shift in the student's next token distribution when evidence is restored. A complementary objective weights the contrast between the student's scores on the two views by how much masking lowers the teacher's log probability for the same sampled token. Experiments on five remote sensing VQA benchmarks show that GeOPD outperforms the evaluated open-source MLLMs and matched post-training baselines, improving over the 4B and 9B base models by 5.81 and 2.97 percentage points on average. The dataset and code will be publicly released at https://github.com/YeeCharm/GeOPD.","authors":["Yichang Luo","Hourun Zhu","Shixiong Liu","Kan Wei","Shi Liang","Yidan Zhang","Lei Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:58:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:58:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34700v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34700v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34700v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34693v1","title":"ShowNow: Fine-Grained Speculative Streaming for Responsive World Models","abstract":"Interactive world models must generate video continuously while responding promptly to user actions. Real-time throughput alone leaves input latency unresolved: coarse generation blocks delay action updates, and generation after input adds work to the response path. We introduce ShowNow, a fine-grained speculative streaming framework that uses computation available during playback to prepare action-conditioned futures before input arrives. Single-latent commitment enables frequent decisions, while configurable preparation covers one to eight actions. We construct a matched multi-future dataset to train alternative continuations from a common state; shared-history tree execution and fused commitment make their preparation efficient. On a single H100, our 5B model sustains 480p generation at 16 fps with eight prepared futures. In ViZDoom, the complete ShowNow deployment reduces median input-to-display latency from 620 to 208 ms relative to the native-block SolarWM-FT deployment. Matched-policy experiments reduce restart empty ticks from 1.56% to 0.09% (94%) and improve on-time responses to action changes. These results establish complementary roles for fine-grained commitment and advance preparation, with measured trade-offs in visual fidelity and long-horizon consistency. ShowNow turns playback-time computation into responsive visual interaction. Project: https://github.com/showlab/ShowNow.","authors":["Yiren Song","Guian Fang","Mike Zheng Shou"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:52:46Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:52:46Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34693v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34693v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34693v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34681v1","title":"Find the Clue, Not the Scene: Rethinking Reasoning for Entity Recognition in Vision-Language Models","abstract":"Answering a knowledge-based visual question requires first recognizing the entity in the image and then recalling knowledge about it. Thinking vision-language models (VLMs) reason for thousands of tokens before answering, yet it remains underexplored how well such thinking VLMs perform each of these two steps. We therefore analyze thinking VLMs on this task and make two key observations. (1) Recognition is the bottleneck, since 76.3% of errors across four models on ViQuAE stem from misrecognizing the entity, and 84.9% of these failures are resolved once the entity name is provided in the question. (2) Recognition is driven by entity-recall clues rather than by general descriptions of the image. An entity-recall clue is a distinctive visual detail that helps the model recall the entity, such as keys, a traditional attribute of Saint Peter. Based on these findings, we propose ClueRL, a reinforcement learning approach that extends Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with an advantage localized to the entity-recall clue in the thinking trace. Rather than injecting new entity knowledge, ClueRL changes how VLMs think: it leads them to attend more closely to visual details and spot these clues, helping them use the knowledge they already have. Consequently, it outperforms both GRPO and a perception-reward baseline on three entity recognition benchmarks. Moreover, this clue-seeking behavior generalizes beyond entity recognition, the only task used for training. Since recognition is the bottleneck, the gains carry over to knowledge-based visual question answering. They even extend to general multimodal reasoning, raising Qwen3.5-4B by 4.4 and 4.0 points on the ScienceQA and MMMU subsets of MMStar, respectively.","authors":["Kyungmin Min","Soyeon Kim","Junseok Kim","Kyomin Jung"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:44:26Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:44:26Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34681v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34681v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34681v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34680v1","title":"Neural Rendering Compiler: Generative Video Models as Programmable Renderers","abstract":"Generative video models are becoming powerful renderers, yet lack the predictable control interfaces of conventional systems such as HTML/CSS engines or Blender. Their capabilities emerge implicitly from training, making it difficult to determine what they can reliably execute. We present Neural Rendering Compiler (NeurCo), which automatically probes frozen video models to construct model-specific capability manuals describing which semantic requirements they can reliably satisfy. NeurCo represents user intent in a model-agnostic intermediate representation of objects, constraints, events, and cameras, then compiles it into model-specific execution strategies. Reliable requirements remain unchanged, while unreliable ones are selectively rewritten or compiled into alternative execution strategies. We introduce VComBench, a benchmark of 380 compositional, multi-requirement tasks spanning text-to-video, image-to-video, and reference-to-video generation. NeurCo improves requirement satisfaction from 46.8% to 52.3% on Wan and from 66.5% to 69.3% on Minimax H3, and achieves further gains when combined with prompt enhancers, including on Seedance 2.5. Our analysis reveals model-specific differences in control capabilities and trade-offs between controllability and photorealism, highlighting capability-guided compilation as a promising approach to programmable generative video rendering.","authors":["Yuxia Zhu","Xinglong Xu","Hao Liang","Linzhuang Sun","Caijun Jia","Qifeng Cai","Jincen Luo","Lei Li","Alden Cai","Asher Qin","Wentao Zhang","Cheng Tan"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:44:16Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:44:16Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34680v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34680v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34680v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34675v1","title":"RoboEvo$^2$: Recursive Robot Self-Improvement via Harness-Policy Co-Evolution","abstract":"Robots should adapt to unfamiliar environments and acquire new skills through interaction, then execute those skills efficiently with practice. Vision-language-action policies provide efficient control, but often need additional human demonstrations to handle unfamiliar settings; reasoning agents can discover solutions using broad prior knowledge and robot-control APIs, but repeated deliberation and repair are costly. Inspired by human learning through reasoning and practice, we propose RoboEvo$^2$, a framework for recursive harness-policy co-evolution. In Policy-to-Harness (P2H) evolution, policy failures guide a coding agent to search complementary Code, Code+ICL, and Code+Policy routes, consolidating verified solutions into reusable skills, routing strategies, and experience memory. In Harness-to-Policy (H2P) evolution, verified trajectories provide supervised training, while Counterfactual Preference Distillation (CPD) teaches the policy to favor successful corrections over failures from the same starting state. The improved policy provides stronger motor skills for subsequent search, while its remaining failures redirect harness evolution, closing the reciprocal loop. Through recursive co-evolution without additional human demonstrations, RoboEvo$^2$ improves standalone success from 11.0% to 84.1% and hybrid success from 30.4% to 90.5% on LIBERO-PRO, with similar gains and reduced agentic costs on RoboTwin C2R. Further experiments demonstrate autonomous task acquisition, cross-embodiment harness transfer, and 95.0% hybrid success on the Unitree G1-D. Our models and code will be publicly released.","authors":["Chenzhi Liu","Jiehong Lin","Wei Huang","Xuzhao Huang","Haoyuan Li","Yang Zhang","Junchao Huang","Chenyeyang Cai","Bo Wang","Kazuhiro Kosuge","Zhongrui Wang","Xiaojuan Qi"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:39:10Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:39:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34675v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34675v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34675v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34657v1","title":"Counting Beyond Frames: Codec Motion Tells Frozen Diffusion VLMs How Many","abstract":"Counting how many times something happens is among the simplest questions about a video and among the hardest for video-language models, autoregressive and diffusion alike. The difficulty lies between the frames: a model samples only a few of them, while each repetition unfolds as movement in between. Video codecs already record this movement densely as block displacements, and codec-based video models use it to shape their visual input. We are the first to show that this motion provides direct evidence for the count: a candidate count is a testable motion hypothesis, and unwinding the cycles it implies measures how strongly the observed directions support it. We present CoDiCount, a unified training-free framework that turns this test into a count potential and applies it while a frozen diffusion vision-language model (dVLM) denoises its answer. At every denoising step, structured count messages map each option letter, or each grammar-valid edit of an open integer, to the count it expresses and reweight the model's own probabilities within that set. Consistent gains over the official decoders on MotionBench, TOMATO, and SVCBench demonstrate the broad generalization of CoDiCount across diffusion models, datasets, and diverse counting tasks. With CoDiCount, a 7B dVLM outperforms GPT-5.1 and Gemini-2.5-Pro on MotionBench, surpasses GPT-4o and much larger open models on TOMATO, and matches GPT-5.4 on SVCBench.","authors":["Zhuoqin Li","Jiayu Ding","Ge Li"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:30:09Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:30:09Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34657v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34657v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34657v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34649v1","title":"Atunda: Building an Afrogenic Dance Movement Dataset for AI-Driven Classification","abstract":"This paper introduces Atunda, the first systematically collected video dataset of Afrogenic dance moves, designed for training and evaluating deep learning models for movement classification. The first phase of the dataset includes nearly 400 videos covering 13 distinct dance moves from African and African Diaspora performative culture, making it the first AI-ready resource of its kind. Using this dataset, we train and evaluate various configurations of temporal convolutional neural networks, achieving a classification accuracy exceeding 95%. The baseline results provide a benchmark for future studies in movement classification, especially in the understudied area of Afrogenic performative culture and tradition. This work lays the foundation for developing in the future digital metrics of similarity, automated attribution systems, and tools that preserve and promote the cultural heritage of Afrogenic dances.","authors":["Diego Carrascosa","Tristan Pank","Qudus Onikeku","Mas'ud Onikeku","Jeremy A Frusco","Osubi Craig","Angelos Barmpoutis"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:25:43Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:25:43Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34649v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34649v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34649v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34644v1","title":"Motion Beyond Pixels: Learning Future Visual Representations for Object-Centric Dynamics","abstract":"Many embodied world models predict future states in latent spaces optimized for RGB reconstruction, which do not explicitly prioritize object-level semantics and geometry relevant to motion generation. We present MotionRep, a visual representation world model that jointly generates future DINO representations and 3D object flow, coupling semantic and geometric information with object-centric dynamics. To make high-dimensional DINO features amenable to generative modeling, we introduce DINO-VAE, which combines temporal and channel compression with latent regularization to produce compact latents while preserving semantic and geometric information. Conditioned on an initial observation, object query points, and a language instruction, a unified diffusion transformer predicts future visual representations and 3D object flow through joint attention. MotionRep employs asymmetric modality-specific noise schedules to keep the visual stream ahead of 3D object flow during joint denoising, allowing progressively refined future visual representations to guide flow generation. Experiments on simulated and real-world manipulation benchmarks demonstrate that MotionRep outperforms pixel-based baselines in 3D object flow accuracy and generalization to unseen scenes, while requiring only three denoising steps for inference. These results support predictive visual representations as an effective future-state space for object-centric dynamics modeling.","authors":["Chengzhi Zhao","Juelin Li","Zhennan Wang","Sheng Wang","Zhuoman Liu","Yuhao Lu","Jiongxu Chen","Haitang Feng","Yanjie Li","Lin Shao"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:24:11Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:24:11Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34644v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34644v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34644v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34640v1","title":"PRAM: Activating Latent Multimodal Knowledge through Partwise Reasoning","abstract":"Emergent cross-modal classification and retrieval enable transfer between modalities without direct paired supervision. However, existing methods often prioritize sample-level alignment, overlooking local discriminative information and partwise statistical relations. To this end, we propose partwise reasoning across modalities (PRAM), which activates latent multimodal knowledge in frozen encoders by composing partwise statistical relations through a shared pivot modality. Specifically, we first present a multi-granularity covariance regularization module that estimates covariances from global and local vectors in two collections of modality-pivot pairs, stabilizing local estimates through adaptive blending with their cross-part means. Second, we devise a pivot-based spectral relational reasoning module that infers global and partwise statistical relations between target modalities through regularized covariance composition and extracts paired projection directions and their correlation strengths via whitening and spectral decomposition. Finally, we introduce a multi-granularity score fusion module that standardizes and fuses matching scores from global and local relations, incorporating task-specific information on pivot associations and class semantics for class prediction and candidate ranking. Extensive experiments across ten datasets demonstrate that PRAM achieves state-of-the-art performance in 16 classification settings and 19 retrieval settings, while exhibiting highly competitive performance in three sensing scenarios.","authors":["Haidong Chen","Ying Guo","Shangze Li","Zhongkuan Mao","Linrui Xu","Canran Xiao","Fei Shen","Tat-Seng Chua"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:21:29Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:21:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34640v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34640v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34640v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34622v1","title":"Beyond Pose Control: Anatomically Consistent Ultrasound Video Generation","abstract":"Camera-controlled video generation anticipates how a scene appears from new viewpoints by relying on a prior learned implicitly from large-scale data. Ultrasound poses the same problem, with the probe pose as the viewpoint, but under opposite conditions: anatomy offers an explicit prior that every sonographer relies on, whereas tracked scans are too scarce to learn it implicitly. As a result, pose control alone is not enough, and pose-conditioned ultrasound generators produce implausible anatomy in unscanned regions and inconsistent anatomy on revisits. We go beyond pose control and give the generator the two kinds of knowledge that make up a sonographer's mental model. An anatomical prior, built from a population of subjects, guides generation where nothing has been scanned, and is refined online by every generated chunk towards the scanned subject. A 3D latent memory, designed around the cross-sectional physics of ultrasound, compounds the generated slices into a volume of the patient and reslices it at new poses, keeping revisited anatomy consistent. The fine-grained memory enters the generator at the input, while the coarse anatomy prior guides the layout across transformer blocks. Experiments on thyroid, kidney, and lumbar spine show improved visual fidelity, pose following, and anatomical consistency over pose-conditioned and memory-augmented baselines.","authors":["Yue Zhou","Fangxu Han","Tony Danjun Wang","Nassir Navab","Yuan Bi"],"published":"2026-11-25T16:08:58Z","updated":"2026-11-25T16:08:58Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34622v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34622v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34622v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34608v1","title":"DuCo: Exploiting Dual Cross-Layer Consistency for Sparse Geometric Attention","abstract":"Dense global attention is crucial for capturing cross-view geometry in visual geometry foundation models, yet its quadratic complexity limits efficient inference on long image sequences. Existing sparse attention methods primarily select entries of the global attention matrix based on attention probabilities and construct masks independently across layers, overlooking value redundancy and cross-layer information reuse. By analyzing attention distributions and clustering value representations across layers, we reveal two forms of \\emph{cross-layer consistency}, characterized by highly similar attention probability distributions and localized changes in value redundancy structures. Building on these observations, we propose \\textbf{DuCo}, a training-free sparse attention framework that exploits historical attention information and value redundancy for efficient geometric inference. Specifically, DuCo leverages historical attention information to estimate current-layer dense attention probabilities and introduces value clustering to exploit redundancy, with clusters efficiently refined through local updates across layers. Both factors are integrated through probability-weighted value distortion to jointly optimize four cluster representation states under an attention budget for sparse mask construction. Experiments across three visual geometry foundation models and five benchmarks demonstrate favorable accuracy--efficiency trade-offs.","authors":["Liangyu Xiang","Junyu Gao","Xiaofeng Wang","Changsheng Xu"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:59:54Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:59:54Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34608v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34608v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34608v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34603v1","title":"VisualMontage: Manipulating the Beliefs of Vision-Language Agents with Authentic Image Sequences","abstract":"As agents based on vision-language models (VLMs) increasingly interpret social-media content, errors in their analysis may lend credibility to rumors and amplify their spread. In this paper, we study the problem of visual cognitive collusion, in which coordinated posts combine authentic images and locally faithful captions to induce VLM agents to infer and accept attacker-chosen target claims through their own reasoning. To enable systematic evaluation of this cognitive threat, we introduce a new benchmark comprising 544 target cases and 33.2K candidate images across eight application domains. We further propose VisualMontage, a multi-agent framework for simulating such attacks through narrative planning, image selection and ordering, and caption assignment under iterative review. We evaluate this threat through extensive experiments across 12 vision-language models, examining modality effects, production-team components, and downstream defenses. Using identical attack feeds across models without victim-specific optimization, we demonstrate cross-model transferability, achieving mean attack success rates of 61.93% for proprietary models and 68.71% for open-weight models. Moreover, 92.65% of generated feeds pass independent model-based validity auditing. Even after downstream review, residual attack success rates range from 35.3% to 76.7% across the evaluated configurations. Our additional controlled comparisons further reveal that captions can improve model-rated posting naturalness while reducing attack success. Together, these findings reveal a novel cognitive threat in social-media interpretation and motivate safeguards that assess how authentic posts jointly shape agents' reasoning and conclusions.","authors":["Jinwei Hu","Lai Wei","Zhuoyun Li","Youcheng Sun","Yi Dong","Xiaowei Huang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:57:55Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:57:55Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34603v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34603v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34603v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34601v1","title":"You Only Look Multiple Times: YoloMT, a Real-Time Streaming Model on a T4","abstract":"We introduce a new model for real-time streaming captioning on a single GPU. YoloMT is a 449.8M-parameter streaming captioner that provides Pareto-frontier performance among generative vision models in its class. Distilled from a 27B teacher by SFT and GRPO, it leads every model under 1B parameters on OVO-Bench, MVBench, StreamingBench and Video-MME, answered from its captions, and beats LFM2.5-VL-1.6B and SmolVLM2-2.2B. YoloMT is designed to read more frames cheaply and make them informative, encoding each frame once and caching it as 16 tokens. This allows the model to incorporate and infer from past frames without additional vision passes, making it efficient and effective. We also provide a detailed recipe for training YoloMT, with its stages and data, as well as ablations that demonstrate the contribution of each component.","authors":["Nessim Ben Abbes","Duc Han Le","Sabri Mtibaa","Van-Tam Nguyen"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:57:40Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:57:40Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34601v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34601v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34601v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34598v1","title":"SEAM: A Self-Evolving and Adaptive Probing Framework for Automatic MLLM Hallucination Evaluation","abstract":"The severe susceptibility of multimodal large language models (MLLMs) to hallucinations poses a critical threat to their reliability, making a rigorous evaluation framework indispensable. However, existing evaluation pipelines largely rely on predefined test items, suffering from high construction costs, rapid obsolescence as MLLMs evolve, and ineffectiveness in probing model-specific weaknesses. To address these limitations, we propose SEAM, a self-evolving and adaptive probing framework for automatic MLLM hallucination evaluation. Specifically, SEAM introduces a composable Hallucination Probe Construction Library comprising ten hallucination-probing skills, three elicitation wrappers, and five question formats. Building on this library, SEAM establishes a dual-loop framework. In the inner loop (adaptive probing), skill-guided visual analysis, question composition, and hierarchical quality verification are coordinated with verifier and target-response feedback to refine test items and uncover model-specific hallucinations. In the outer loop (skill evolution), a skill updater distills historical probing trajectories into localized, reusable rule updates, which undergo held-out validation and are frozen before testing. Driven by these innovative structural designs, SEAM establishes an automated and self-evolving evaluation paradigm. Experiments across four target MLLMs and ten hallucination types demonstrate that SEAM substantially outperforms representative static benchmarks, achieving an 89.2\\% relative improvement in mean valid hallucination discovery rate across 12 matched comparisons.","authors":["Shiyu Zhang","Zengyi Wo","Yuming Liao","Ran He","Caifeng Shan","Yanbo Fan"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:57:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:57:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34598v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34598v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34598v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34591v1","title":"ADClaw: A Programmable Visual Agent for Industrial Anomaly Detection and Understanding","abstract":"Industrial anomaly detection (IAD), the task of identifying and localizing deviations from normal product conditions, is essential to automated quality inspection in manufacturing. Many vision-language models for IAD rely on large domain-specific datasets for fine-tuning and make predictions in a single pass, leaving no opportunity to re-examine ambiguous regions. Although tool-using agents can revisit these regions, predefined tool interfaces limit how they can combine intermediate results in a follow-up check. When such checks are insufficient, models may miss subtle defects or misidentify the defect type. To address this limitation, we introduce ADClaw, an inspection agent that uses executable code to test anomaly hypotheses without task-specific fine-tuning. For each hypothesis, a Structured Normality Prior identifies the applicable rules and retrieves normal visual references for the inspection target. Guided by this prior, the agent writes and executes code in a persistent Inspection Workbench to combine masks, anomaly maps, and measurements from earlier steps into a new test of the suspected defect. After each check, Hypothesis-Guided Evidence Reasoning assesses whether the evidence supports the suspected defect and its location, using gaps or inconsistencies in that evidence to guide the next code action. ADClaw achieves competitive results in detection and localization across 2D and 3D industrial anomaly detection benchmarks and in anomaly understanding on MMAD.","authors":["Xinyi Wu","Rongxin Gao","YuHui Lin","Yonglin Hong","Chu Li","Wei Li","Dingzhao Li","Shijia Zhao","Chaopeng Li","Haixin Sun"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:51:47Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:51:47Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34591v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34591v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34591v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34581v1","title":"ChemoMAE: Self-Supervised Spectral Representation Learning Reveals Chemical Heterogeneity in Historical Wood","abstract":"In historical timber buildings, multiple environmental factors act over centuries on wood whose chemical composition varies across anatomical regions, producing complex patterns of degradation. Understanding how timber members degrade is important for the effective conservation and restoration of historical buildings, yet the mechanisms underlying these patterns remain unclear. To investigate these mechanisms, we used self-supervised learning to establish a common basis for comparing specimens from timber members that differ in wood species and service history. We examined transverse sections of 49 wood specimens from several historical buildings in Japan using near-infrared hyperspectral imaging. ChemoMAE, a self-supervised Transformer, learns pixelwise spectral representations through masked denoising that exploits multicollinearity among spectral variables. Clustering these representations yields spatial maps without predefined chemical-state categories. Because anatomy and degradation can produce spatially continuous regions, we formulated Local Label Agreement (LLA) to quantify agreement among labels assigned to neighboring pixels. Using spectra alone, ChemoMAE yielded higher mean LLA than baseline methods on validation specimens. We then trained ChemoMAE anew and fitted cluster centers across all specimens. Spatial structures consistent with wood anatomy and the expected effects of surface exposure emerged in the resulting maps. By cross-referencing regional spectra and spatial distributions across specimens in light of wood anatomy, service histories, and prior studies, we proposed models of internal and surface degradation as mechanistic hypotheses. This exploratory AI for Science framework gives researchers a basis for linking latent patterns learned through self-supervision with domain knowledge when investigating how material states form.","authors":["Ryuji Yamaguchi","Miyuki Matsuo"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:45:12Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:45:12Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34581v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34581v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34581v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34575v1","title":"Sage-Med: Scaling Medical Multimodal Intelligence","abstract":"Establishing effective scaling laws for medical multimodal large language models (MLLMs) remains a persistent and fundamental challenge. Our systematic evaluation shows that existing medical MLLMs tend to underperform frontier general-purpose MLLMs across a range of medical tasks, while scaling model size primarily improves text capabilities rather than multimodal perception, and enabling reasoning modes fails to close this visual gap. To investigate how medical multimodal intelligence scales in practice, we introduce Sage-Med-Preview, a 35B-parameter medical multimodal model with 3B activated parameters, built by combining stage-specific curation over 322.7 million candidate records with a progressive training curriculum spanning medical pretraining, staged supervised fine-tuning, and adaptive reinforcement learning. Across this curriculum, progressive supervised refinement and adaptive reinforcement learning yield consistent stage-wise gains, substantially advancing both specialized visual understanding and medical language capabilities. Evaluated against 77 competing models across 238 clinical tasks spanning 61 public and 13 private multi-center benchmarks in more than 40 modalities, Sage-Med-Preview achieves an average score of 58.26% on the 31 public multimodal benchmarks. It outperforms the state-of-the-art open-source medical model Hulu-Med-235B-A22B at 50.99% and the general scientific model Intern-S2-Preview at 52.24%, and approaches the advanced proprietary model GPT-6.2-Sol across multiple tasks such as comprehensive multimodal understanding, report generation, and visual grounding. Although substantial challenges remain in complex medical reasoning and multi-step clinical workflows, this report provides empirical evidence, actionable data and training recipes, and a rigorous benchmark toward scalable clinical multimodal intelligence.","authors":["Sage-Med Team",":","Ming Hu","Cheng Tang","Wei Li","Rongbin Li","Feilong Tang","Junru Hu","Hongze Zhu","Qiujuan Yan","Huanze Tang","Shaocong Wei","Siyuan Yan","Min Cen","Zhongxing Xu","Zhonghua Wang","Lie Ju","Zhen Yu","Wenjie Li","Shiyan Su","Jing Hao","Jingyong Liang","Diping Song","Fang Yan","Chenglong Ma","Junzhi Ning","Haochen Xue","Zanting Ye","Chenrun Wang","Jinjie Wei","Yi Xin","Jianghan Shen","Siqi Luo","Jiyao Liu","Huidong Xie","Jun Zhao","Rulin Zhou","Longquan Jiang","Ziyan Huang","Ying Chen","Hanxiao Zhang","Junyang Wu","Mouxiao Bian","Jiashi Lin","Chenyu Wang","Qi Qin","Jie Huang","Yiling Zhou","Jiawei Chen","Kefan Wang","Jingjing Hua","Yaoyu Liu","Huihui Xu","Tianbin Li","Jin Ye","Ziyang Chen","Shujian Gao","Zilin Lu","Jiangtao Yan","Changkai Ji","Wenhao Tang","Xinyi Zeng","Yuxing Chen","Hongwei Yang","Rongzhao Zhang","Jinru Ding","Yingcheng Wu","Zhenfei Yin","Jinzhe Li","Lu Lu","Yun Zhong","Zhishan Lin","Bingli Wang","Xiaohan Ren","Jianmin Qian","Penghao Zhao","Zhongbo Tian","Zhenbang Zhou","Peiling Dai","Mengjing Zhao","Jingyu Zheng","Pinan Liu","Shiwei Li","Victoria Mar","Cristina Vico-Alonso","Qiang Wu","Xianming Liu","Lingtong Huang","Dili Song","Minghui Zhang","Wenlong Zhang","Qipeng Guo","Yuqiang Li","Nanqing Dong","Yun Gu","Qi Duan","Hongliang Ren","Hongming Shan","Yin Xie","Xiang An","Benyou Wang","Yueming Su","Yefeng Zheng","Zongyuan Ge","Chun-Mei Feng","Jie Xu","Yu Qiao","Junjun He","Yirong Chen"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:39:45Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:39:45Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34575v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34575v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34575v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34568v1","title":"Physics-Guided Generative Modelling of Oxy-Combustion Flame Images: From Conditional LSGANs to Denoising Diffusion Models","abstract":"Validating industrial oxy-combustion burner designs traditionally requires costly experimental campaigns in which each operating condition is physically reproduced on an instrumented rig and imaged via OH* chemiluminescence, rendering iterative design exploration prohibitively slow. This work develops deep generative surrogate models that map a 15-dimensional vector of continuous operating parameters (thermal power, swirl numbers, momentum ratios, mixture fractions, etc.) directly to a $512\\times64$ heat-release flame image, trained on 1,609 experimental samples from the OXYTEC dual-swirl non-premixed oxy-combustion database. Two complementary generative paradigms are investigated, as they offer opposite strengths for surrogate modelling: a custom spatially-anchored conditional least-squares GAN (cLSGAN-L1-CBN), whose single-pass generation enables near-instantaneous inference, and a custom physics-guided denoising diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM), whose iterative sampling favours the smooth, continuous heat-release gradients of fluid flows. Both are validated through ablation on a stratified held-out test set. The optimized cLSGAN reaches RMSE 0.022 and FID 20.68 and generates an image in about 20 ms on a mobile workstation GPU, though extended training leaves its texture realism unchanged; the DDPM improves steadily over long training horizons, reaching RMSE 0.019 and FID 20.29 at 40,000 epochs, at the cost of roughly thirty seconds per image on the same hardware --- three orders of magnitude slower than the adversarial surrogate. A nearest-neighbour analysis traces the persistent worst-case errors to sparsely sampled regions of the parameter space, and a parameter-sensitivity study confirms smooth, physically consistent responses to perturbed inputs. These results demonstrate that carefully guided generative models can act as reliable visual surrogates for combustion experiments.","authors":["Imane Er-rabhi","Gorkem Oztarlik","Jean Caudal","Renaud Gaudron"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:35:33Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:35:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34568v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34568v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34568v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34564v1","title":"Score-based Black-box Attack via Adversarial Condition Guided Diffusion Model","abstract":"Generative models enable unrestricted adversarial attacks that aim to fool classifiers while preserving the natural appearance and semantics of the original image. However, existing diffusion-based black-box attacks largely rely on transferability from surrogates, making their effectiveness sensitive to differences between surrogate and victim. Improving transferability can also distort semantic content and degrade image quality. Moreover, retaining the original prompt to preserve fidelity leaves the diffusion model's conditional prior underused for adversarial guidance. We address these limitations by adapting score-based black-box attacks to reverse diffusion, using the victim's output probabilities to guide latent updates without accessing its gradients. We further introduce adaptive-target guidance, which uses the victim's current runner-up class as an adversarial condition to steer denoising towards the decision boundary. Because such guidance can introduce unwanted semantic changes, we also propose target-search guidance, which searches for a small perturbation of the original condition embedding using a score-based attack. This achieves comparable attack effectiveness with better image quality than runner-up guidance. Experiments on the ImageNet-Compatible dataset show that our framework outperforms transfer-based baselines in both attack effectiveness and image quality.","authors":["Yuhang Chen","Chih-Hong Cheng","Kurt Debattista","Siddartha Khastgir","Xingyu Zhao"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:33:29Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:33:29Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34564v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34564v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34564v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34561v1","title":"Compact Vision-Language Navigation in Continuous Environmentsvia On-Policy Training and Route-Bearing Output","abstract":"Instruction-following robots need navigation policies that reach high accuracy and remain inexpensive to train and to run on a single graphics processing unit (GPU). The most accurate vision-language navigation policies for continuous environments rely on language models with 4 to 8 billion parameters, long observation histories, and large auxiliary corpora. Two properties of the standard training setup limit smaller policies. Imitation labels cover the states that an expert visits, whereas the running policy reaches states that its own errors produce. In addition, an image-plane waypoint exists only while the route lies inside the camera view. We present \\textbf{\\paper}, a monocular red-green-blue (RGB) policy with 2 billion (2B) parameters that addresses both limitations. \\paper adapts Qwen3-VL-2B with low-rank adapters and processes an eight-frame memory and the current view in one forward pass. The policy predicts the route direction as a bearing in the body frame of the agent, which remains defined for every heading, and a deterministic controller converts this bearing into motion. This design allows training to relabel the states that the policy itself visits. On-policy correction improves success, while bearing decoding outperforms image-plane waypoint scanning across all evaluated start headings with identical weights. On the \\valunseen split, \\paper achieves \\rFinSR\\% success on Room-to-Room in Continuous Environments (\\rtor), averaged over three seeds, and \\rRxFinSR\\% on its Room-Across-Room counterpart (\\rxr). Inference requires \\rDecMs\\,ms and \\rInferGB\\,GiB of memory, while training takes \\rCostTrainTyp\\ GPU-hours on one 12\\,GB consumer GPU.","authors":["Reshad Ul Karim","Syeda Maliha Tabassum","Md. Khalilur Rhaman","Md Rashad Al Hasan Rony"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:32:39Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:32:39Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34561v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34561v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34561v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34560v1","title":"M$^{2}$AdaptVLA: Multimodal Memory-Augmented Vision-Language-Action Model with Predictive Test-Time Adaptation","abstract":"Effective robotic manipulation calls for multimodal memory and rapid adaptation to shifts in contact dynamics. However, many existing policies, which retain either visual or wrench history, may miss key cues from the other modality. Moreover, even with wrench memory, fixed policies can struggle when contact conditions shift at deployment. To this end, we introduce M$^{2}$AdaptVLA, a vision-language-action model that integrates visual and wrench memories with predictive test-time adaptation. For visual memory, the model uses test-time training (TTT) to encode observation history in fast weights. Each entry in the bounded TTT bank combines the corresponding frame's features with a readout from these weights, preserving cues that predate the oldest retained frame. For wrench memory, a context gate controls how much the action expert draws on wrench history as it jointly denoises future actions and wrenches. For predictive test-time adaptation, the auxiliary wrench prediction loss also provides online self-supervision. Using measured wrenches as targets, we update low-rank policy adapters by backpropagating only through the final denoising step. Real-world experiments show that the TTT bank outperforms visual memory baselines on progress tracking, scene recall, and demonstration following. On contact-sensitive tasks, the context gate yields higher success rates than direct injection or gated cross-attention. Predictive test-time adaptation improves robustness to shifts in contact dynamics. On eight tasks requiring visual and wrench memory, M$^{2}$AdaptVLA achieves a mean success rate of 82.71%, a 31.46% absolute gain over the strongest baseline. The project page is available at https://tlf-tlf.github.io/M2AdaptVLAPage/.","authors":["Liufan Tan","Tong Wang","Yuehao Yin","Jian Chen","Yihang Yin","Ying Qu","Wenyu Li","Ruyi Gan","Hao Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:32:21Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:32:21Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34560v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34560v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34560v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34549v1","title":"CADENCE: Sensor-GroundedWorld-Consistent Gaussian SLAM for Collaborative Mobile Reconstruction","abstract":"Collaborative reconstruction with consumer phones promises faster and more complete indoor maps. However, heterogeneous devices introduce degraded observations and unreliable cross-device associations, so a single poor contributor or incorrect match can corrupt the shared Gaussian map. To address this, we propose \\textbf{CADENCE}, a collaborative Gaussian simultaneous localization and mapping (SLAM) system that separates finding cross-device matches from deciding whether and how peer observations update the map. To find matches, a sensor-grounded regional memory summarizes committed observations and ranks candidate peer regions using appearance, relative pose, gravity, barometric altitude, and observation quality. Deterministic geometric verification and optional physical checks then decide which of the proposed matches are accepted. Once a match is accepted, separate cue-based confidences control how its observations contribute to pose constraints, geometry, appearance, and memory. As a result, useful depth can still contribute even when color is unreliable. Throughout this process, local tracking remains isolated from mapping, verification, and networking. We evaluate CADENCE on eight Matterport3D houses that were not used to train the memory. On these houses, regional memory achieves an area under the receiver operating characteristic curve (AUROC) of $0.95$ to $0.97$ and thus outperforms appearance retrieval, while verification keeps false-link rates below $0.5\\,\\%$. Furthermore, soft confidence weighting reduces the mean receiver peak signal-to-noise ratio (PSNR) loss from $2.66$ to $1.05$\\,dB across eight degradations while retaining useful peer depth. Finally, using inertial and platform motion, CADENCE tracks all twelve held-out iPhone scans with a mean absolute trajectory error of $6.98$\\,cm, and its refined maps achieve better average rendering quality than the baselines.","authors":["Reshad Ul Karim","Abrar Samin","Md Rashad Al Hasan Rony"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:26:02Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:26:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34549v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34549v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34549v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34545v1","title":"Recalling the Past to Answer the Present: Context-Aware Multi-Evidence Reasoning for Streaming Video Question Answering","abstract":"Vision-language models (VLMs) have achieved remarkable progress in video understanding, yet existing video question answering (VQA) benchmarks mainly evaluate models in offline settings. Real-world streaming scenarios instead require models to answer context-dependent queries by retrieving and reasoning over evidence distributed across the video history. We introduce StreamRecall, a benchmark for context-aware Multi evidence reasoning in streaming video QA. StreamRecall requires models to retrieve, associate, and reason over temporally scattered evidence to answer queries at the appropriate moment. It contains 4,983 questions over 462 videos, with context-aligned query times and fine-grained temporal evidence annotations. We further propose the Evidence Utilization Cascade to diagnose model reasoning through evidence localization, grounding, chain completion, and answer derivation. To enable efficient long-term streaming reasoning, we present EviStream, a training-free causal visual memory mechanism that preserves reasoning-critical evidence under a fixed KV budget. Experiments reveal substantial gaps between current VLMs and realistic streaming requirements, establishing StreamRecall as a foundation for developing evidence-aware streaming video intelligence.","authors":["Cui Yakun","Tiantian Geng","Huixiang Kong","Xingqun Qi","Sirui Han","Yike Guo"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:24:02Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:24:02Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34545v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34545v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34545v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34544v1","title":"HAS: Hierarchical Adaptive Frame Sampling for Video Understanding","abstract":"Due to context-length constraints, most MLLMs cannot process full-length videos and therefore rely on sampling a subset of frames as input. However, existing sampling methods, ranging from uniform sampling to relevance-based selection, are often driven by a single sampling principle and thus struggle to accommodate the heterogeneous evidential requirements of different queries: some demand a holistic understanding of how the video unfolds over time, while others hinge on fine-grained events within a short temporal window. To address this limitation, we propose \\textbf{Hierarchical Adaptive Frame Sampling (HAS)}, a two-stage frame sampling framework. In the first stage, \\emph{Backbone Frame Construction}, we apply a Determinantal Point Process (DPP) to sample frames that are both query-relevant and non-redundant. These selected frames capture key moments across the video and form the backbone of the sampling set, providing a foundation for subsequent enrichment. In the second stage, \\emph{Adaptive Contextual Enrichment}, we analyze the temporal distribution of the backbone frames to infer the query's requirements and adaptively allocate the remaining frame budget between Local and Global Context. The Local Context enriches the backbone with fine-grained temporal dynamics and short-range causal relations, whereas the Global Context connects temporally isolated evidence and provides a holistic view of the entire video to support global understanding. Through such a hierarchical two-stage process, HAS effectively addresses diverse query requirements. Incorporated into three leading MLLMs, it demonstrates consistently superior performance across the Video-MME, LongVideoBench, and MLVU benchmarks.","authors":["Yuyao Sun","Daiqi Shi","Shuang Li","Liao Zhang","Yang Cai","Deqing Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:23:58Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:23:58Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34544v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34544v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34544v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34521v1","title":"PURA: Video Token Pruning via Unified Spatiotemporal Redundancy Assessment","abstract":"Video large language models incur substantial inference costs from long visual token sequences. Existing video token compression methods mitigate these costs by exploiting spatial redundancy within frames and temporal redundancy across frames. However, many approaches impose predefined processing orders or fixed allocation ratios for spatial and temporal compression, limiting their ability to adapt to the varying balance of spatiotemporal redundancy across videos. To address this limitation, we propose PURA, a training-free method that prunes visual tokens through unified spatiotemporal redundancy assessment. For each token, PURA constructs spatial and temporal reference candidates and selects the reference with the lowest representation error across the two dimensions. The representation error is combined with token importance to determine each token's removal cost. To avoid the time overhead of removing the lowest-cost token one by one, we introduce Coordinated Pruning, which iteratively groups mutually compatible low-cost removals into batches for simultaneous execution and updates the pruning state after each batch until the target budget is reached. Extensive experiments on three video large language models and five video understanding benchmarks demonstrate that PURA achieves a favorable trade-off between task performance and inference efficiency.","authors":["Yuyao Sun","Hongli Yuan","Shuang Li","Tao Deng","Deqing Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:08:53Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:08:53Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34521v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34521v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34521v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34515v1","title":"Decoder-as-a-Probe: Verbalizing and Steering Visual Representations in LVLMs","abstract":"It is commonly accepted that high-level concepts are represented linearly in the representation space of large neural networks. For Large Vision Language Models (LVLMs), finding those interpretable directions in visual representations is important for interpretability. To find them, the classical approach is to train a linear classifier on the representations, but it needs a large amount of labels and only recovers what can be read from the representations, not what the model actually uses. In this paper we start from the following insight: The LLM Decoder of an LVLM is also a classifier whose classes are the possible next tokens. So we use its first-order Taylor expansion as a linear classifier. Hence, we introduce Decoder-as-a-Probe (DaP), which significantly outperforms classical probing and steering baselines especially when training data is sparse. (1) It verbalizes what an LVLM perceives in a given image. (2) It can surgically change the perception of the LVLM about specific attributes (e.g. change only the color, the clothes worn or the action done). Due to its simplicity and data efficiency, we believe DaP can serve as a plug-and-play tool for LVLM research, such as interpretability, hallucination mitigation or instruction tuning.","authors":["Adhemar de Senneville","Xavier Bou","Jérémy Anger","Rafael Grompone","Gabriele Facciolo"],"published":"2026-11-25T15:04:57Z","updated":"2026-11-25T15:04:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34515v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34515v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34515v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34509v1","title":"Long-Horizon Planning with Latent World Models via Graph Anchors","abstract":"Latent world models plan in a compact latent space by predicting the outcomes of actions. Their planners judge a plan by the latent distance between its predicted end state and the goal image. We show that this distance fails in two distinct ways. First, it loses directional signal a few steps from the goal, causing distant goals to fail. Second, it weighs the robot arm over the manipulated object, causing manipulation to fail at contact. We measure both failures directly on recorded data by asking whether a lower latent distance corresponds to true progress toward the goal. Across five environments, six visual representations (including raw pixels and V-JEPA 2), and nine Meta-World tasks, this ranking test cleanly separates the two failures, and the graph distance to a goal predicts where closed-loop planning breaks down. Both failures share a single remedy: replacing the distant goal with a nearby target that lies on a recorded trajectory. We realize this principle with Graph Anchors, a training-free planner for frozen latent world models. It builds a sparse graph once over the model's own training frames and aims each planning step at a node a few moves ahead along the shortest path to the goal, keeping paths on recorded transitions wherever contact matters. On goals up to 400 steps away, Graph Anchors raise LeWM's success from 13% to 85% on PointMaze-Medium, from 8% to 87% on PointMaze-Large, and from 5% to 71% on Push-T, where stronger search toward the goal fails and where prior training-free planners break down under missing step horizons, subsampling shortcuts, or fragmented contact.","authors":["Syed Nazmus Sakib","Abdul Monaf Chowdhury","Peizhen Li","Nafiul Haque","Chandak Chakma","Md Mehedi Hasan","Zehong Cao","Shifat E. Arman"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:58:31Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:58:31Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34509v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34509v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34509v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34489v1","title":"Who Is Ego? Scaling Cross-View Camera Wearer Identification and Segmentation beyond Humans","abstract":"A first-person (ego) image reveals what a camera sees, but not who or what carries it. Given a synchronized third-person (exo) image, camera wearer identification asks who is ego: which candidate carries the ego camera? We extend this task beyond humans to robots, vehicles, animals, and drones, and broaden its spatial scope (where is ego) from selecting a provided candidate mask to wearer segmentation with absence prediction and mutual camera localization across ego-exo views. We introduce FindEgo-1M, comprising 1.16M real and simulated ego-exo pairs across 12 wearer types, 17 domains, and 111 test settings. Our approach exploits a geometric relation: the ego camera's projection into the exo image, i.e., the epipole, lies on or near its wearer. We introduce EgoRay-ZS, the first method to turn a frozen feed-forward 3D reconstruction model, VGGT-$Ω$, into a training-free solution for wearer identification and segmentation. Its geometric readout ranks candidate masks or segmentation proposals by angular offset and distance to the ego camera, while the relative pose provides mutual camera directions. We then develop the full EgoRay model through pose-supervised training on FindEgo-1M and label-free test-time adaptation constrained by candidate masks. Without task-specific training, EgoRay-ZS achieves 78.0% identification accuracy on TF2023's original labels, versus 52.9% reported for the dataset-trained PEN. On FindEgo-1M, EgoRay achieves state-of-the-art chance-corrected accuracy of 87.1 and 77.7 on seen and unseen settings. On matched queries, EgoRay also outperforms GPT-6 Astra by 31.6 and 21.3 points on seen and unseen settings, respectively, while running $18\\times$ faster. Data, code, and models will be released.","authors":["Yifan Jin","Zheng Zhang","Tianwen Qian","Dong Wang","Bo Zhao","Yanwei Fu","Yuqian Fu"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:48:48Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:48:48Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34489v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34489v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34489v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34476v1","title":"TACC: Target-Aware Carrier Compression for Dense-Context Novel View Synthesis","abstract":"Recent feed-forward approaches mark a paradigm shift in novel view synthesis by eliminating the reliance on per-scene optimization, directly inferring 3D representations from input views. However, their reliance on global scene-token context incurs substantial memory and latency overhead, constraining their ability to handle increasingly dense source-view contexts. Compressing source representations offers an intuitive way to limit this token budget. Prior general-purpose compression methods are not tailored for novel view synthesis, as their fixed, target-agnostic nature fails to adapt evidence allocation to individual target views. To overcome this, we introduce TACC, a target-aware compression module tailored for feed-forward latent NVS. Specifically, TACC organizes each source view into a progressive carrier hierarchy. Driven by source-target geometric relevance, it adaptively retrieves tokens to dynamically allocate exact computational quotas. This selective retrieval directly yields a compact, target-conditioned representation. Extensive experiments on the RE10K demonstrate that under dense input settings, TACC achieves $9.23\\times$ and $64\\times$ higher Effective FPS than Full Context on LagerNVS and LVSM, respectively. Effective FPS excludes one-time source encoding and Carrier construction.","authors":["Yiming Fan","Yijia Guo","Zewen Guo","Liwen Hu","Yuheng Yuan","Yang Li","Zhiwei Li","Xitong Ling","Caihong Yuan","Shengbo Chen","Tiejun Huang","Lei Ma"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:41:33Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:41:33Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34476v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34476v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34476v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34465v1","title":"Evidence-Aligned Benchmarking and Learning for Medical Video Language Models","abstract":"A medical video language model should answer from the visual evidence its question asks about. We find three places where this breaks down and address each with an approach we call MedEvidU. First, benchmarks reward answers that ignore the video: on one dataset, the position of a clip within its recording predicts the surgical step in 37% of questions, and a constant answer beats every trained model on a standard safety-assessment metric. We build MedVidBench-v2, 34 tasks from 18 surgical and clinical video datasets, in which prompts and metrics are audited with answers given without video, and which tests unseen hospitals and windows cut blindly from whole recordings. Second, common video encoders merge consecutive frames into shared tokens, so a question about one frame is answered from a blend of two. Query-slot inputs give each queried frame its own tokens at almost no extra cost. Third, a fine-tuned model has memorized its training questions, which leaves little to learn from. Label-matched question resampling (LQR) adds unseen questions about the same recordings while keeping the answer distribution fixed, in a final supervised epoch and in reinforcement learning. Together, these raise the grounding IoU of a 4B model from 30.4 to 44.4, above a supervised 27B model, and instrument grounding at an unseen hospital from 33.9 to 72.5. On blindly cut windows, event-presence accuracy improves by 5.5 points and localization F1 by 8.1 points. Project page: https://gaozhongpai.github.io/MedEvidU/","authors":["Zhongpai Gao","Anwesa Choudhuri","Meng Zheng","Benjamin Planche","Terrence Chen","Ziyan Wu"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:35:42Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:35:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34465v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34465v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34465v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34461v1","title":"SceneDesigner++: A Unified 9-DoF Pose Control Framework for T2I and T2V Generation","abstract":"Precise 9-DoF pose control is essential for visual content generation, enabling users to specify object location, size, and orientation in 3D space. However, existing methods typically focus on either text-to-image (T2I) or text-to-video (T2V) generation, with limitations in pose controllability and temporal coherence. We present SceneDesigner++, a unified framework for 9-DoF pose control across both T2I and T2V generation. We construct ObjectPose9D++, a dataset containing high-quality images and videos with accurate pose annotations. Trained on ObjectPose9D++, SceneDesigner++ combines absolute and relative pose encoding strategies to decouple frame-wise spatial grounding from inter-frame motion modeling. Specifically, absolute pose encoding leverages CNOCS map sequences to provide explicit, frame-wise 9-DoF pose guidance. Meanwhile, our relative pose encoding, termed VPRoPE, incorporates relative pose information into rotary position embeddings to explicitly model inter-frame pose transitions, thereby promoting temporally coherent object motion. A learnable orthogonal transformation is further introduced for each attention head, allowing different heads to capture complementary geometric relationships while preserving input feature norms to promote training stability. We further adopt a two-stage training strategy to learn the absolute and relative pose encoding mechanisms separately. Benefiting from this lightweight and decoupled design, SceneDesigner++ also supports integration with an independently trained camera-control module, enabling separate or joint control over object and camera motion. Finally, we introduce T2IVPoseEval, a benchmark for evaluating diverse object poses and motion patterns in both T2I and T2V tasks. Extensive experiments demonstrate that SceneDesigner++ consistently outperforms same-backbone baselines in pose fidelity and motion quality.","authors":["Xincheng Shuai","Zhenyuan Qin","Henghui Ding","Dacheng Tao"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:33:51Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:33:51Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34461v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34461v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34461v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34457v1","title":"ProxyGenBench: Benchmarking Proxy-Guided Video Generation","abstract":"Recent video generators produce realistic videos, yet controlling how scenes unfold remains challenging. Structural proxies, such as animated 3D blockouts, guide generation by specifying scene layout, motion, camera behavior, and event order. However, these coarse inputs leave details unspecified and may contain artifacts that should not be reproduced. Successful proxy-guided generation must therefore Preserve intended structure, Complete missing content and correct proxy artifacts, and Bind text, image, and audio conditions to the right entities and events. We introduce ProxyGenBench to study these interacting requirements, with 312 evaluation cases from 60 scenes across three scene tasks and controlled variations in proxy fidelity, text detail, and multimodal inputs. We evaluate five video generators using verified, case-specific checklists. Experiments show that proxies improve spatiotemporal control, but greater proxy detail can coincide with poorer completion, while strong structural adherence does not ensure correct binding.","authors":["Zhenchen Tang","Haolin Lu","Songlin Yang","Xiaotong Zhao","Ran Zhang","Bo Peng","Shuai Li","Haotian Fan","Haobin Zhong","Yanni Zhang","Songlian Li","Ning Zhang","Xun Gao","Alan Zhao","Jing Dong"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:31:23Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:31:23Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34457v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34457v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34457v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34435v1","title":"Most of the Way There: Turning a Strong Video Prior into an Action-Conditioned World Model","abstract":"Modern video generators excel at producing visually plausible futures guided by text and image prompts. Yet their emphasis on semantic plausibility does not guarantee fidelity to the physical consequences of specific actions. Action-conditioned world models (ACWMs), in contrast, aim to simulate how the world responds to robot actions, enabling scalable policy evaluation and improvement without costly real-world rollouts. The challenge is not merely to generate a plausible future, but to predict what follows from a specific physical action. As video priors grow stronger, we ask how little adaptation is truly needed to bridge this gap. We introduce Dianjing, a minimal recipe that preserves and unlocks the capabilities of strong video priors through unified physical action conditioning, training concentrated at the high noise levels where motion and layout, and thus the consequences of actions, are decided, and native history conditioning. Built upon MiniMax H3, a 33B video foundation model, Dianjing freezes the pretrained backbone and trains only 0.34% of its parameters using approximately 65 hours of robot video across two embodiments, without additional robot-specific pretraining or camera calibration. Controlled ablations show that where training is allocated across noise levels matters more than how actions enter the network. On WorldOracle, which evaluates predictions against paired real-world executions, Dianjing achieves the best scores on four of the five evaluation tracks and ranks second on AgiBot-Long. Our findings suggest that strong video priors already bring us most of the way to world modeling; lightweight physical action grounding can unlock their potential for real-world prediction.","authors":["Zanyi Wang","Xia Chen","Liu Liu","Xianglong Guo","Dengyang Jiang","Haodong Li","Tingyang Xiao","Wenhao Hu","Zhizhong Su"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:19:27Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:19:27Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34435v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34435v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34435v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34430v1","title":"One Representation to Rule Them All -- Open-Vocabulary Change Detection from Text or Visual Examples","abstract":"Open-vocabulary change detection (OVCD) aims to localise user-specified changes without retraining for predefined categories. Existing approaches typically rely on textual descriptions and combine separate models for region proposal, semantic recognition, and temporal comparison. This limits how target concepts can be expressed and makes detection dependent on several intermediate predictions. We introduce OVTSID3, a training-free framework that unifies region grouping, concept identification, and temporal comparison in the representation space of DINOv3. OVTSID3 supports two complementary modes of concept specification: zero-shot textual queries or one-shot visual prompting through a masked reference pair. OVTSID3 outperforms all prior training-free OVCD methods on two building and two natural-disaster benchmarks, raising the average F1 by 8.9 points. OVTSID3 also supports semantic change detection and achieves state-of-the-art on SECOND. These results demonstrate the flexibility of a unified representation for text- or example-guided change detection without task-specific model training.","authors":["Blaž Rolih","Matic Fučka","Filip Wolf","Luka Čehovin Zajc"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:16:57Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:16:57Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34430v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34430v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34430v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34422v1","title":"Can Visual World Models See Force?","abstract":"World models and vision-language-action policy models are increasingly proposed as foundations for robot manipulation. They predict how scenes evolve and which actions to take, yet successful manipulation depends not solely on following observable trajectories but also on the hidden variable of interaction force. Neurophysiology has shown that humans possess a visual prior for this hidden variable, motivating us to ask the question: Do the representations of visual foundation models also capture the forces involved in an interaction? We define force awareness as the ability of representations to (1) indicate when contact force is applied, (2) track how it changes during an interaction, and (3) reflect how large it is. We probe frozen image, video, world- and policy model representations with a lightweight readout trained on synchronized video and force measurements from two manipulation datasets. Our experiments show that most models capture when contact occurs but not how much force is being applied. Surprisingly, robot post-training does not improve this either, highlighting an important gap in current models capabilities.","authors":["Felix Tristram","Benjamin D. Killeen","Nassir Navab"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:12:10Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:12:10Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34422v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34422v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34422v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34420v1","title":"DocSEAM: Bidirectional Cross-Page Encoding for Multi-Page Document Understanding","abstract":"Multi-page document vision-language models encode each page independently, creating a feature-level discontinuity at page boundaries: visual tokens are formed without access to content on adjacent pages, and the language model must reason over these page representations formed in isolation. We introduce DocSEAM (Document Selective Enrichment via Anchored Masking), a lightweight module inserted between a pretrained vision encoder and the language model that injects the inductive bias of local cross-page context into visual representations. For each page, DocSEAM forms a local triplet with its two neighbors, applies center-anchored masked self-attention with directional salience gates to fuse information from neighbor pages, and returns only the enriched center-page tokens, leaving the visual token count unchanged. After a supervised cross-page warm-up and multi-page fine-tuning with DocSEAM frozen, our 9B-parameter model achieves state-of-the-art results among the evaluated open-weight models of up to 10B parameters on five multi-page document VQA benchmarks. We further show that under controlled re-pagination, DocSEAM reduces sensitivity to boundary placement and improves cross-page span localization","authors":["Hoang-Nhat Nguyen","Chen Change Loy"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:11:06Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:11:06Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34420v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34420v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34420v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34414v1","title":"SemProxy: Semantic 3D Proxies for Controllable Agentic 3D Modeling","abstract":"Coding agents are increasingly capable of generating 3D models, yet enabling users to effectively control the modeling process remains challenging. Existing workflows rely primarily on text prompts and reference images, which often struggle to convey precise spatial intentions and require iterative corrections. We introduce SemProxy, a lightweight intermediate representation that bridges intuitive human interaction and agentic 3D modeling. Instantiated as labeled 3D bounding boxes, semantic 3D proxies allow users to directly specify object parts and spatial arrangements while leaving geometric details to coding agents. These proxies can be serialized into structured text and rendered as images, making them directly accessible to existing coding agents without additional training or modifications. We further develop ProxyStudio, a user interface that integrates text, image references, and interactive 3D proxy editing into agentic modeling workflows. We demonstrate the versatility of SemProxy across object- and scene-level modeling tasks with different coding agents, and evaluate its effectiveness in supporting controllable human-agent collaborative 3D modeling through a user study.","authors":["Shuyuan Zhang","Changjian Li"],"published":"2026-11-25T14:08:05Z","updated":"2026-11-25T14:08:05Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34414v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34414v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34414v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","agent","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34397v1","title":"Strong Starts, Simple Recipes: Unified Multi-Teacher On-Policy Distillation from Multimodal Specialists","abstract":"Multi-teacher on-policy distillation (MOPD) integrates separately trained domain specialists into a single unified model. Existing methods focus on refining the distillation procedure while initializing the student from a base model or a multi-domain SFT checkpoint, which leaves capability integration largely to distillation itself. Instead, we treat the starting point as a design variable and propose Unified MOPD (Uni-MOPD), which pairs a strong start with a simple recipe: a training-free uniform merge of specialists from the same base model initializes the student, and short MOPD with uniform domain sampling and full-vocabulary reverse KL refines it. Across five multimodal domains and 19 benchmarks, our study establishes three findings. (1) The starting point matters more than subsequent training choices: before any training, the uniform merge already matches the score that the base model reaches after 200 MOPD updates, and after short distillation it matches higher-scoring, search-based starts. (2) Short MOPD recovers most of the capabilities the merge leaves unintegrated; it improves every domain, with the largest gains where the domain teacher still leads. (3) From a strong start, uniform domain sampling and full-vocabulary reverse KL match or exceed every evaluated sampling strategy and divergence, without schedules, per-token weighting, or truncation hyperparameters. Uni-MOPD outperforms Open-MOPD at the same training budget while training 3.3 times faster than its released code. We will release the training framework, specialist checkpoints, and unified student.","authors":["Wenzheng Chang","Taiqiang Wu","Yuqi Xu","Yongfu Zhu","Zelin Peng","Ji Zhao","Shuai Dong","Wenpu Liu","Caoyuan Ma","Xuehui Wang","Ziyue Wang","Weichu Xie","Haiyuan Wan","Ngai Wong","Tong He","Wenqi Shao","Jiaqi Wang"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:59:32Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:59:32Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34397v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34397v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34397v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34370v1","title":"MoCA4D: Monocular 3D Bounding Box Lifting and Tracking with Camera and Object Motion","abstract":"Monocular 3D bounding box tracking must account for both object motion and changes in camera viewpoint. Framewise box lifting with temporal filtering leaves these motion components implicit, while static-scene fusion assumes stationary objects. We introduce MoCA4D, a framework for 3D box lifting and tracking from monocular video, associated 2D box prompts, and camera intrinsics. An observation branch estimates per-frame geometry, while a dynamic branch predicts a camera transformation shared across objects and a separate rigid transformation for each object. We propose Star Optimization to refine current depth and orientation using the observation and historical boxes propagated through these transformations. The refined box then guides subsequent propagation. Across all evaluated objects in Argoverse 2 and ADT, Star Optimization reduces center motion error by 38.1% and 38.7%, respectively, relative to Kalman filtering of the same observations. On MOVi-F, the dynamic branch achieves the lowest corner and rotation errors and the highest IoU among the evaluated methods over eight-transition motion windows. Real-data camera-only controls further link predicted object motion to improved localization on dynamic-object tracks.","authors":["Changwoo Jeon","Jongyoo Kim"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:34:03Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:34:03Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34370v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34370v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34370v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34360v1","title":"One Patch, Many Policies: Object-Associated Physical Patch Attacks on Robotic Manipulation","abstract":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) and World Action Models (WAMs) are vulnerable to adversarial visual interference, motivating the study of universal and transferable physical patch attacks. We propose Object-Associated Patch Attack (OAPA), a universal and transferable physical patch framework that enables an attacker to select which task object's manipulation to disrupt through patch placement, without re-optimizing the patch. Our framework combines object-associated placement, differentiable physical compositing, and final action-expert representation divergence. Physical compositing accounts for perspective and occlusion across camera views, while representation divergence guides patch optimization using either relative RMS or cosine distance. Experimental results demonstrate strong attack transferability across task suites and policies, together with the ability to selectively disrupt manipulation of an attacker-chosen task object through patch placement.","authors":["Yiqian Liu","Yuqi Lin","Hui Lu","Jiaming He","Qi Zhu","Deyi Ji","Bing Li","Kaizheng Wang","Zhiyuan Zha","Yi Yu"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:28:04Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:28:04Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34360v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34360v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34360v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34347v1","title":"AIRIS: Benchmarking Social Embodied Intelligence in Egocentric Streaming Videos","abstract":"Embodied agents operating around people must not only recognize social cues, but also decide when the available evidence is sufficient, whether to intervene, and how to respond without violating social norms. Existing benchmarks typically evaluate these abilities in isolation or with access to completed videos, obscuring where an online social decision fails. We introduce AIRIS, a causal streaming benchmark that formulates social embodied intelligence as a time-anchored chain of Attention, Intention, Reaction, and Interaction (A-I-R-I). AIRIS contains 8,000 expert-curated questions grounded in 357 egocentric clips across 11 context categories and 36 capability dimensions. At each query, models receive only the observation prefix available at that time; decision-aware metrics reward calibrated waiting and complete reactions while penalizing premature commitment. Evaluating 45 proprietary and open-source general-purpose, embodied, streaming-video, and omni-modal models reveals fragmented and temporally miscalibrated competence. General-purpose VLMs excel at passive cue grounding and intent recognition, whereas embodied specialists lead on isolated reaction sub-decisions; yet across all families, models frequently guess intents before sufficient evidence emerges and fail to jointly align intervention timing, action domain, and concrete execution within the same episode. AIRIS thus provides a diagnostic foundation for bridging the gap between passive social perception and reliable embodied interaction.","authors":["Yuchen Zhou","Hongsheng Wu","Jiayu Tang","Yunxuan Mao","Hanjing Ye","Shenghao Zhang","Jing Tang","Chun-Kai Fan","Yufei Huang","Yonggen Ling","He Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:18:30Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:18:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34347v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34347v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34347v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34344v1","title":"SPARC: Semantic Calibration with Latent Multimodal Knowledge for Robust Image-Text Retrieval","abstract":"Robust image-text retrieval aims to maintain accurate cross-modal matching when image or text queries are incomplete, supporting reliable information access in real-world applications. However, existing methods typically focus on adapting to distribution shifts, without adequately addressing candidate discrimination for incomplete queries and ranking instability. To this end, we propose semantic projection and alignment with relational constraints (SPARC), which elicits latent multimodal knowledge from frozen encoders to improve candidate discrimination and ranking stability. Specifically, we first devise a semantic calibration module on a product of spheres that refines incomplete visual and textual features through tangent-space updates, maintaining the unit-sphere structure used for cosine similarity. Second, we introduce a robust semantic guidance module that combines latent multimodal knowledge encoded in complete image-text features with cross-modal semantic anchors to promote semantic alignment of the calibrated features. Finally, we introduce a matching relation constraint module that fuses original query features with their calibrated counterparts and applies a soft ranking constraint to penalize reductions in initially positive matching margins, thereby limiting ranking perturbations. Extensive experiments on Flickr30K and COCO demonstrate that SPARC outperforms competing methods under various image occlusion and word deletion settings, while achieving consistent gains across different pretrained backbones.","authors":["Zhongkuan Mao","Wenzhuo Zhao","Haidong Chen","Shangze Li","Yidong Wang","Jinhe Bi","Yunpu Ma","Junfeng Fang","Fei Shen","Tat-Seng Chua"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:16:30Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:16:30Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34344v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34344v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34344v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["retrieval","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34319v1","title":"PostMesh: Self-Rewarded Preference Post-Training for Flow-Based Artist Mesh Generation","abstract":"Artistic mesh generators turn a dense 3D object into an artist mesh: a compact, quad-dominant mesh that is easy to edit. Most of current models are auto-regressive methods which decode a mesh token by token, take tens to hundreds of seconds per mesh. Diffusion- and flow-based generators are much faster, but their meshes still fall short of artist quality. To our knowledge, PostMesh is the first post-training method for a diffusion- or flow-based artist mesh generator, and it needs no expensive artist-made ground truth: the generator is trained on its own samples, scored by a reward computed from the dense input and the output. We propose an artistic mesh reward that keeps the output consistent with the dense input while making the mesh structure into artistic. We also propose a multi-negative flow preference optimisation that transfers the reward to the generator. After a short LoRA post-training, PostMesh is comparable to or better than slow autoregressive generators on most artist-mesh quality metrics and in a blind VLM preference test, at a fraction of their time.","authors":["Gihyun Kwon"],"published":"2026-11-25T13:05:42Z","updated":"2026-11-25T13:05:42Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34319v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34319v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34319v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34292v1","title":"WebVibe: Multimodal Interactive Website Generation via Reverse Synthesis and Visual Self-Distillation","abstract":"Although multimodal large language models translate single screenshots into static HTML, generating interactive functional websites from design specifications still struggles with multi-page routing and stateful widget transitions. Examining existing training pipelines reveals two bottlenecks: (1) in forward data synthesis (X -> Y), writing the specification X before code execution leaves unimplemented or altered requirements in X as supervision noise; and (2) in reinforcement learning, scalar rewards discard the rich state transitions observed during replay. Based on these findings, we introduce WebVibe and Interactive-SDPO to train InteractiveCoder. WebVibe reverses synthesis into Y -> X by verifying cross-page and in-page interactions in the browser before deriving multimodal inputs from replay-confirmed states to build the 31,542-site WebVibe-32K corpus with reusable execution contracts. Interactive-SDPO then conditions a weight-sharing self-teacher on the replay trace and a 2x2 state-transition montage to provide token-level supervision at zero inference-time overhead. On the public Vision2Web L2, one-shot InteractiveCoder-9B nearly 3x its base model's functional score (20.2 to 55.2 FS) and matches a 1T-parameter coding agent in a single pass with 111x fewer total parameters and 78x fewer tokens. On our WebVibeBench, InteractiveCoder-4B and 9B further outperform all evaluated baselines by over 23 points.","authors":["Tianxiang Jiang","Sheng Xia","Yilin Long","Kanghui Tian","Linquan Wu","Yongheng Zhang","Daixian Liu","Di Yin","Yi Wang","Yu Qiao","Qinglong Zhang"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:46:28Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:46:28Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34292v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34292v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34292v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["agent","multimodal"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34290v1","title":"StateWeaver: Multi-Agent Coordination of Story-State Transitions for Long-Horizon Video Generation","abstract":"Modern video foundation models generate high-quality short clips, but preserving long-video consistency and correctly depicting event-driven state changes remain challenging. Existing agentic workflows face these challenges because continual planning and repair accumulate context, while reference updates remain limited. We introduce StateWeaver, which combines Active States and Hierarchical Collaboration to connect review-driven visual reference updates with cross-shot state inheritance. Active States translate state discrepancies identified during review into concrete visual asset requirements that guide reference construction, validation, and regeneration, preserving identity and already-correct content while repairing target attributes. Confirmed generation results are linked to the corresponding asset states, story time, and reference versions for verification and reuse by subsequent shots. Hierarchical Collaboration isolates execution contexts by task, clip, and repair round, and uses hierarchical memory to organize this process across multiple sets. Across multiple benchmarks, StateWeaver outperforms existing methods and produces long videos with sustained cross-shot consistency under predefined and autonomously planned storyboards.","authors":["Modi Jin","Xuying Zhang","Yijing Guo","Haoran Li","Yanwen Ma","Jing Wang","Chenyu Ge","Ming-Ming Cheng","Haoyang Huang"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:43:37Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:43:37Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34290v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34290v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34290v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","agent"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34288v1","title":"Rehearse in 3D, Render in 2D: Physically Consistent and Editable Robot World Models","abstract":"Embodied world models aim to predict future interactions that are both visually realistic and physically consistent. Yet explicit 3D motion prediction alone does not guarantee that object structure and contact relationships are preserved. Beyond motion prediction, 3D representations provide an explicit state space for imposing structural and physical constraints. Building on this insight, we introduce RehearsalWorld, an editable robot world model driven by 3D Interaction Rehearsal (3DIR). Given an initial RGB-D observation and robot actions, Interaction-Consistent Rehearsal (ICR) uses a pretrained 3D point-flow model as a motion prior. It refines the predicted trajectories through response-aware object grouping, persistent grasp memory, and contact structure refiner, preserving scene stability, object structure, and grasp attachments. Rehearsal-Aligned Video Generation (RAVG) then uses geometric projections of the rehearsal as temporal conditioning. We fine-tune a video generator on projection-video pairs to synthesize realistic interaction videos. Thanks to the decoupling of physical response from visual synthesis, RehearsalWorld also supports independent editing of material properties and camera viewpoints. Compared with PointWorld on real DROID grasping sequences, RehearsalWorld reduces gripper-relative offset by approximately 79% to 84% across deformable, articulated, and rigid objects and lowers reference motion error. For video prediction, it achieves a PSNR of 23.92 dB and an FVD of 149.","authors":["Guangyu Liu","Hanzhang Wang","Huijia Zhang","Hanbei Zhan","Linjie Hu","Bin Xiao","Xuan Zhou","Dong Zhang","Hanwei Liu"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:41:56Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:41:56Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34288v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34288v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34288v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["video","3d","representation"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34284v1","title":"ReMeDi: Single-Image PBR Material Estimation via Reparameterization between Metal and Dielectric Hypotheses","abstract":"Physically based rendering (PBR) material estimation facilitates relighting, material editing, and 3D content creation. As the reflectance of a surface heavily depends on whether it is a metal or a dielectric, producing any map that determines albedo involves a decision on metalness, which we call the \"intended metalness\" of the map. Rendering maps with mismatched intended metalness yields physically implausible or misinterpreted materials. The metallic--roughness workflow makes metalness explicit and thus encourages base color to match it, but does not enforce this match, especially for automated methods directly predicting each map. Therefore, we propose ReMeDi, which enforces the match through two components. First, we reparameterize base color as a metalness-weighted blend of its two variants, estimated under the all-metal and all-dielectric hypothesis, respectively. Second, as available supervision cannot teach a single network both hypotheses at every pixel, we restrict each of two separate networks to one hypothesis, so that each variant is ensured to follow its hypothesis. Besides enforcing the match, this also improves each estimated material map. Experiments show that ReMeDi outperforms state-of-the-art methods in both material accuracy and relighting fidelity.","authors":["Fan Fei","Xin Huang","Yixin Zhu","Jiawei Lu","Xin Yang","Jinglin Liang","Ruining Tang","Yifei Feng","Boxin Shi","Chunchao Guo"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:37:52Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:37:52Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34284v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34284v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34284v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["3d"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"},{"id":"2611.34279v1","title":"Semantic State Reading for Joint Image-Semantic Diffusion Transformers","abstract":"Pretrained visual representations improve diffusion-transformer training, yet exploiting both global context and local spatial structure requires deciding which semantic states to model and how to access them. We introduce Semantic State Reading (SSR), which jointly denoises image latents, a global CLS token encoding image-level context, and a compact patch state preserving spatial correspondence. SSR couples this global-local modeling with distinct reading paths: local states are fused with corresponding image tokens at the input, while independent projections inject the current global state into every transformer block. Both semantic scopes thus remain accessible throughout denoising. Ablations show complementary benefits from local-state modeling and repeated global reading, and semantic-trajectory interventions alter image layout under a fixed class label. On ImageNet $256 \\times 256$, SiT-XL/2 with SSR reaches or surpasses the reference FIDs of SiT, REPA, and Representation Entanglement for Generation (REG) with $100\\times$, $40\\times$, and $2\\times$ fewer training iterations, respectively. SSR achieves unguided FID 1.78 at 2M iterations and FID 1.34 with classifier-free guidance alone. Combining classifier-free guidance with autoguidance yields FID 1.31 at 1M iterations, achieving the best FID in our main comparison table.","authors":["Guanxi Lai","Zixuan Li","Zhiwei Li","Qi Li"],"published":"2026-11-25T12:36:00Z","updated":"2026-11-25T12:36:00Z","abstract_url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.34279v1","html_url":"https://arxiv.org/html/2611.34279v1","pdf_url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2611.34279v1.pdf","matched_keywords":["representation","diffusion"],"source_used":"abstract","source_text_chars":0,"summary":null,"first_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00","last_seen_at":"2026-11-26T09:05:49+00:00"}],"tracked_paper_count":82,"storage_mode":"latest_overwrite"}};
